1:# Hivemind architecture: the hedge fund research brain
5:## 1. What the system does
7:Hivemind is building a hedge fund brain: a system that follows the decision process of an investment analyst. It receives source material, identifies information and ideas that may matter to assets or events in the world, connects that information to the research being followed, and maintains a dossier of the evidence and events behind each thesis.
15:## 2. Architecture at a glance
45:| Hummingbird stream | Incoming material from its event pipeline. | An item and its available source information. |
50:| Step 2: Jev | Incoming evidence and the returned evidence candidates. | An evidence relationship: repeat, revision or new. |
51:| Step 3 | The Step 1 proposals and Step 2 results. | Saved theses, evidence, sources and relationships. |
54:## 3. The information the system works with
58:| Incoming item | The article or other material Hummingbird supplies for processing, with its available source information. |
61:| Evidence source | The material and relevant passage or location behind the evidence, with speaker and date when known. |
62:| Evidence relationship | A connection showing that evidence repeats or revises an earlier statement. |
64:| Run trace | The source item, the thesis context shown to the model, its output and the resulting thesis state, available for review. |
68:## 4. Step 1: extract theses and evidence
70:### Input
76:### What the model decides
87:### Output
92:- The evidence contains the relevant statement and enough source information to find and interpret it.
95:Section 12 shows a proposed JSON response shape: one list of new theses and one list of evidence, with links from each evidence item to the existing or new theses it informs. Local references connect records in one response; application code assigns persistent identities. The runtime schema has not been finalized or enforced. Preserve the meaning of the source and distinguish what the source says from what is inferred. Do not invent a speaker, date or figure absent from the item.
99:| Evidence for an existing thesis | Existing thesis reference plus one or more evidence statements and their sources. |
104:The item itself is the source of the new evidence. The open thesis descriptions provide context for deciding where it belongs. The historical evidence search happens later.
106:### Step 1 contract and the existing ledger
108:Step 1's research result is a collection of thesis–evidence pairs. The structured-output example in section 12 is a **proposed model response**, not the existing production response or a final database write contract. A pair can refer to an existing thesis or propose a new one; each pair has one or more source-backed evidence items. One statement can inform more than one thesis.
118:### Example
129:## 5. Step 2: find and compare related evidence
131:### Why this is separate
135:### Find candidate evidence
143:### Compare with Jev
149:- Jev is asked the clean path's own piece question, aligned with line 2's test: a repeat reports the stored piece's main statement, and
150:  an official's denial of a report is a revision.
153:- On c6's records: search 97.9%, wrong merges 1.9% (2 of 104), both lines met; line 3 not re-measured.
161:| New evidence | It is not identified as a repeat or revision of the evidence compared. | A different analyst offers a separate forecast. |
163:The words, amount, speaker, source and time can matter to the comparison. A later statement about the same subject is not necessarily a revision. A matching amount alone does not prove that two statements are the same.
165:The result “new evidence” is the outcome of the comparison performed; it is not proof that no matching evidence exists elsewhere in the library. When the incoming evidence is judged a repeat of several candidates, it joins only the group Jev judges it most like: the highest same-statement probability, with ties going to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08). A comparison that fails is not saved and is reported.
167:**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.
171:2. At most 2% of merges are clearly wrong.
178:## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links
186:| Repeat | Place the incoming record in the existing evidence record's group: add its source and any missing link to the relevant thesis, and keep the incoming record's own text and fields inside the group. Do not rewrite the existing record, and do not create a separate evidence record for the same statement. |
187:| Revision | The new evidence and its source, linked to the earlier statement as a revision. |
189:A revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original ("A 继续；跑council loop 开始做"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). How a judgement reads a group is being tested. Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the chosen thesis, evidence and relationships are saved. How they are physically stored and written is a separate implementation decision.
191:## 7. Step 4: judge the thesis
199:## 8. Evaluate Step 1
201:Step 1 evaluation is an offline forward run followed by a source-by-source review of the trace and the changing thesis state. Alex's direction is to inspect what the system actually does instead of first constructing a hypothetical golden library of thesis–evidence pairs. This does not change the existing frozen references used by historical measurements.
207:- Did it preserve what the source says, including the speaker and date when available?
212:### Forward run and state review
214:Run selected articles in a fixed order against a known initial library. Capture, for each article, the source text, the exact open-thesis context, the raw Step 1 output, and the resulting library state. The next article sees the state actually produced by the preceding article. Review the trace and state against the source to identify misses, incorrect evidence or links, and duplicate theses. Document mistakes and their downstream effects rather than silently correcting the run's history.
218:### Current Step 1 forward run
220:The [current article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-2026-07-08-09-continuing/review.html) completed all 49 selected source items from an empty isolated library. It contains 22 thesis records and 160 evidence items; two proposed source passages could not be matched and remain visible as unverified. These are counts from one exploratory run, not a quality score. Unmatched passages and unresolved links are kept for review; affected and subsequent state is provisional. After a stop on article 21, the run continued in a new trace from article 20's recorded state. The [first attempt at article 21](../poc/data/clean-step1-2026-07-08-09-continuing/first-attempt.html#article-21) remains available; the current replay shows the completed prefix and new attempt in order. The source collection has known related-article contamination, and its fixed processing order is not necessarily publication-time order. [Run decisions and learning](STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) record blockers, investigation, changes and their checks.
222:The [49-article trace review](step1-trace-review.html) explains the source-backed findings and includes an article-by-article assessment. Its [Markdown source](STEP1-TRACE-REVIEW.md) is rendered with `node clean-arch/render_trace_review.mjs`; check synchronization with the same command plus `--check`. This review is not a frozen-ruler acceptance result.
224:### Prepared-source Step 1 replay
226:The [seven-article prepared-source replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-prepared-2026-11-06/review.html) uses a separate experimental source database assembled from recorded source-role answers and the original page lines. It ran the existing clean Step 1 prompt in the earlier selected order, starting from an empty isolated library. The viewer shows the selected article, earlier cleaned text, full archived page, source-role decisions, provider response, and before/after thesis state. The recorded source selection is not an integrated live source-preparation service, and neither the selected order nor the underlying captured page is asserted to be complete or chronologically authoritative. This historical replay's `cleaning` metadata describes the earlier cleaned text, not the model-selected text; that field was not included in the model request. New materializations retain the earlier cleaning metadata separately and identify the selected-source preparation.
228:The seven calls completed, producing four new thesis proposals and 21 evidence items with 33 source passages. Every passage was located contiguously in both the selected text and the archived page, but that **does not prove it supports the whole summary**. Independent readers found that Firmus evidence-1 omits the preceding passage supporting the US$2 billion fundraising amount in its summary; FEV ev-2 omits the passage supporting the limited/no-internet condition. Several other quotations need preceding context to identify the named party. These are visible in the replay. The model's later review identified the Firmus omission and missing FEV party context but did not identify the missing internet condition; those leading follow-ups do not prove why the original answers missed the passages. A controlled comparison completed on the same seven source inputs and before-states, testing the location of the existing final check and a general citation-support clarification. Independent readers assessed anonymous answers before the settings were revealed. In three draws per arrangement, moving the check or adding the clarification reduced this particular Firmus citation miss, but some changed arrangements omitted a named SambaNova customer/deployment or weakened the FEV connectivity condition. Seven Firmus answers also added a currency to a $15.5bn valuation whose currency was not explicit in the source. Neither prompt change is adopted; the [blind review](../poc/data/clean-step1-prepared-2026-11-06/citation-placement-comparison/BLIND-REVIEW.md) records all four arrangements and limitations. The cases were already known and cannot establish production accuracy.
230:The prepared input restored the article 1 CFO outlook line, which Luna included in E3; it excluded the unrelated October recommendations from the Firmus input while retaining the reported investment. It also restored the article 4 SambaNova funding paragraph, which the model did not extract; whether it matters to NVIDIA's competitive research is a separate judgment. The FEV source is a hosted page with an FEV-issued release; the output does not fully preserve that issuer-versus-host distinction. The FEV and Firmus displayed local dates and stored New York dates are consistent with their timezone-aware timestamps; listing timestamps are separate values. The SambaNova page, however, gives 20:19 UTC in machine-readable metadata and 21:19 UTC in its visible byline. Both are the same New York day, but its exact publication time is unresolved.
232:This replay uses recorded source-role answers rather than calling the source selector live. The selected source and citations remain provisional; exact source location cannot guarantee complete citation support, correct attribution, or relevance. No production database or frozen prompt was changed.
234:### Citation-completion diagnostic
236:A separate model-assisted review was tested on nine saved, known source-and-evidence packets, with three calls per packet and two independent readers. All three Firmus reviews found the missing US$2 billion fundraising passage, but all three FEV reviews missed the source passage for limited or unavailable internet. All three also missed a different FEV record’s reversed local-versus-cloud backup relationship; only two of three identified an unsupported US-dollar currency on the Firmus valuation. Six of 69 expected evidence reviews were left unaddressed because returned identifiers did not match the evidence identifiers. These are selected examples, not a quality rate. [The detailed review](../poc/data/clean-step1-prepared-2026-11-06/citation-completion-probe/BLIND-REVIEW.md) keeps the source-backed examples and limitations.
238:**Status:** This extra review is an experiment and is not part of the runtime. Additional passages can support a missing citation, but cannot repair a wrong summary or link. A located quotation does not establish that every claim in its evidence is correct. The recorded source-preparation and contiguous-page quotation checks are separate improvements; they do not decide whether a claim matters or is semantically supported.
240:### Page-time-order Step 1 replay
242:The [page-time-order replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-page-time-2026-11-06/review.html) runs the same seven prepared source texts in the order of the archived pages' timezone-aware publication metadata, starting from an empty isolated library. It produced three new theses, 17 evidence items and 30 located source passages. The earlier selected-order replay produced four new theses and 21 evidence items. These are two single exploratory draws with different preceding states, not a controlled model-quality comparison. Page publication time is not proof of event time or pipeline arrival order; the SambaNova page's visible and machine-readable times disagree by an hour, although both give it the same position in this selected subset. [Recorded ordering evidence](../poc/data/clean-step1-page-time-2026-11-06/page-time-order.json) keeps the original timestamps.
244:The Parasail/d-Matrix release appeared first and opened a heterogeneous-inference question; the later d-Matrix report reused that question rather than opening a duplicate. The same run still has material errors: the SambaNova benchmark is described as a deployment although its cited text establishes a test; a named planned customer/deployment present in the source was omitted; and the FEV link explanation reverses which system is the backup, even though its summary states the direction correctly. The FEV summary also omits the source's explicit no-internet case and treats an example model as if it were a definite selection. Firmus cites both investment paragraphs in this draw but assigns a currency to the $15.5bn valuation that the source does not explicitly specify. These findings are annotated in the replay. In a later leading self-review, Luna identified the omitted planned customer but did not identify the reversed FEV backup direction; this does not prove why the original answer failed. Exact source location does not establish a correct link, complete summary or correct attribution.
246:### Step 1 source-faithfulness council
248:Codex and Claude Code reviewed the current source, prompt, code and saved answers, then challenged each other over four rounds. Their [detailed council record](STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md) links to each exchange and distinguishes observed errors from unproven causes. Claude could see earlier experiment reviews during its first pass, so this was not a blind independent assessment.
250:The shared conclusion is that no tested semantic fix is ready. One evidence item can simultaneously have an incomplete citation, an altered claim, a questionable attribution or an incorrect thesis explanation. Exact source location alone cannot verify these. The tested follow-up review found Firmus's missing financing paragraph in three draws, but missed FEV's missing connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship in all three; it is not adopted. A quote-first output or stronger reasoning setting may be worth testing later, but neither is proven and either could change or omit useful evidence.
252:The agreed next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical forward run and its 160 returned evidence records, with separate, overlapping flags and a separate article-level review for omitted facts. Preserve source contamination and incompleteness as distinct findings, and adjudicate difficult cases with another reader. Then compare the two seven-item replays separately: all seven source IDs are already in the 49-item manifest, and their inputs and order differ. Before the full review, check the review method against known problems and independently checked ordinary records. This is an investigation of recorded runs, not a production-quality rate or a change to the prompt.
254:### Proposed Step 1 link implementation
266:This is a proposed clarification, **not a verified fix**: the current request already implies much of the same behavior. Relevant context, counterevidence, multiple valid links and meaningful new topics must remain. The first comparison reuses six stored article requests and their actual open-thesis states, with the original request as a same-day control; it will compare all outputs and useful omissions. The prepared request files pass the existing structural checks, but have not been sent to Luna. [The implementation council plan](STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md) records the exact approach, source examples, independent audit, controls and limitations. The earlier 49-item fidelity review remains incomplete.
268:### Step 1 link candidate: completed comparison
270:The [article-by-article comparison](../poc/data/clean-step1-link-none-2026-11-06/review.html) ran the original prompt and the proposed clarification on six saved articles, three times each. All 36 calls completed. Independent readers examined the source, open theses and answers before the prompt labels were revealed. This is a known-case diagnostic with frozen historical states, not a representative accuracy score or a stateful forward replay.
272:The candidate did not reliably eliminate the problem. On the GeForce NOW article, the answer attached the server expansion to the Shield TV thesis while saying it did not inform it in **1 of 3 candidate draws**, versus **2 of 3 original draws**. In the DeepSeek article, two original answers attached the reported custom chip to an NVIDIA inference-chip partnership thesis; none of the three candidate answers did. These small counts are observations, not proof of a durable improvement. In another article, named domestic-competitor evaluations were absent in two candidate answers versus one original answer; the source and full answer require an analyst's assessment.
274:Both arms also omitted the DeepSeek article's reported Kyber delay **and** NVIDIA's denial in all six answers, even though the article contains both and the Kyber thesis was open. Its revenue and margin guidance appeared in one of three answers in each arm. These omissions show that avoiding a particular wrong link does not ensure the system captures other important evidence. The active prompt is unchanged. [The detailed blind review](../poc/data/clean-step1-link-none-2026-11-06/BLIND-REVIEW.md) records all six articles, attribution concerns, known source limitations and the blind arm mapping. The next investigation is to trace the persistent missed-evidence case and its full request before proposing another change.
276:### Trace: Kyber evidence omitted during extraction
278:Article 34's supplied text reported a possible Kyber delay to 2028 and NVIDIA's denial. An open Kyber thesis explicitly asked whether the roadmap or planned 2027 timeline would be affected. The model's original response and all six later comparison responses omitted the two statements; the runner did not remove them. Current revenue and margin guidance appeared in only two of the six later responses. The [source and request trace](../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md) records the inputs, answers and limitations.
280:Earlier article 10 had reported manufacturing setbacks and a company roadmap statement, but not the specific possible 2028 delay. Step 1 sees open theses, not prior evidence; the later evidence comparison is responsible for deciding repeats. The model can recover the missing statements in a leading follow-up, but a separate neutral self-account said it could not know its earlier reasoning and did not identify Kyber. Neither account proves why the original extraction missed it. In article 10, Kyber appeared later in the text and was returned in all six comparison answers; the different articles and thesis states do not establish that position or an existing thesis caused article 34's omission. No prompt change is adopted from this trace.
282:### Kyber trace: thesis-visibility diagnostic
286:The independent reader first assessed anonymous source and evidence without seeing thesis identities or links. The second pass checked links against the actual input; at that point the condition could be inferred. No thesis state advanced. This result does not support claiming that the mere presence of the existing Kyber thesis caused the omission in this example, and it does not establish the underlying cause. Removing a thesis is a diagnostic only and is not a proposed production change. [The complete trace](../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md) preserves the source, self-account, requests and limitations.
288:### Kyber trace: full article versus its source paragraph
290:With the same model, prompt, title, source metadata and all 20 open theses, an isolated comparison sent either the full saved article or just its exact 408-character Kyber paragraph, three times each. The full-article answers omitted the reported possible 2028 delay and company denial in **3/3 draws**; the paragraph-only answers returned both and linked them to the existing Kyber thesis in **3/3 draws**. The [six-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/review.html) shows the text actually sent, thesis links, source quotations and attribution fields.
294:### Whole-article coverage council: prior attempts and next candidate
296:Codex and Claude Code reviewed the saved extraction traces, older paragraph-by-paragraph experiments, source-first extraction and later split-prompt results. They agree that article 34's Kyber omission is in Luna's raw answer, but that its cause is not established and no complete coverage fix has been validated. The historical experiments found useful content as well as noise, incorrect links and lost context; the paragraph-only Kyber test also changed several input characteristics at once. The [council record](STEP1-COVERAGE-COUNCIL.md) maps each attempt to its results, limitations and original sources.
300:### Whole-article coverage: completed order comparison
302:The proposed evidence-block reorder was tested without changing the clean prompt in the PRD. On nine previously inspected articles, three fresh answers per arm completed: **54/54 responses**. Each used the saved article and pre-item thesis state; no production state advanced. Independent readers assessed all answers against their source and thesis list before the arm mapping was revealed. The [interactive comparison](../poc/data/clean-step1-coverage-order-2026-11-06/review.html) shows each article, six answers, source passages and review observations.
306:This selected-case comparison does **not** validate the reorder as a fix, and it is not adopted. It also found an attribution error even when the citation locates: an article 41 control summary attributes an Ayar Labs executive's 2028 forecast to a Lightmatter executive, while its own quotation identifies the Ayar speaker. The [detailed results](STEP1-COVERAGE-ORDER-RESULTS.md) distinguish facts, uncertain judgments, blind review, source limitations and the next source-fidelity investigation. The original active prompt and frozen production configuration remain unchanged.
308:### Completed review: 49 articles and 160 evidence items
312:Four records were judged to change source meaning or qualification, including article 29's stock-market investment becoming infrastructure spending and article 28's example model becoming a definite selection. Eight records were flagged for incomplete citation support or an unverified quote; ten records across three articles were flagged for unsupported thesis links, seven of them from one robotics article under a different agent-stack question. Four date-field cases were identified; other date interpretations remain concerns. These categories overlap, include human judgment and describe one selected run, not a population accuracy rate. The source itself was contaminated in article 3, while article 2 and article 16 lost relevant captured-page text before the model saw it. The [full review](STEP1-FIDELITY-REVIEW.md) records controls, uncertainty and the distinction between source losses, model errors and the unimplemented clean writer.
314:A completed isolated comparison moved only `source_passages` before `summary` in the output schema. Across 11 previously inspected articles, three answers per condition were reviewed with condition labels hidden. The provider followed the requested field order, but both conditions omitted article 34’s Kyber delay and denial in all three answers, and all six article 11 answers stated a $1bn financing without citing the sentence that establishes the amount. Article 28 still turned an example model into an actual selection in one candidate answer, and one candidate turned investment in stocks into infrastructure investment in article 29. The small, selected comparison does not establish a general rate or a fix; the change is not adopted. See the [full results](STEP1-CITATION-ORDER-RESULTS.md) and [66-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-citation-first-2026-11-07/review.html). The original prompt, production configuration and databases remain unchanged.
316:### Source handoff council: ongoing investigation
318:Codex and Claude Code are examining the source-to-extraction handoff and prior attempts. The original 49 saved requests and seven prepared-source requests had empty author fields; the prepared records retained proposed source roles, but those roles were not sent in the extraction request. Roles can mix bylines, outlets, dates and uncertain page material, so they cannot safely be promoted to an author field. A 20-article overlap check found that previously cited passages excluded by the recorded selections were an unrelated recommendation or explicitly labeled AI summaries; it does not establish full body coverage. Sixteen repeated article-34 controls omitted the same Kyber report and denial; the cause remains unproven. The [council record](STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) links the reproduced comparisons and previous failures. An isolated ten-article comparison completed 60 calls with the same prompt, schema and pre-item thesis states. Prepared text removed a known unrelated-story error in article 3 (2/3 original, 0/3 prepared) and generated-summary citations in article 8 (2/3, 0/3), but did not reliably improve extraction: article 16’s restored financing paragraph was in all three prepared inputs and in none of their answers; article 28’s example model was stated as a selection in all six answers; and article 34’s delay report and denial appeared in 2/3 controls and 0/3 prepared answers despite being present in both inputs. These are selected-case observations, not general rates or established causes. The [full results](STEP1-SOURCE-TEXT-RESULTS.md) and [60-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-text-comparison-2026-11-07/review.html) show source and output. In two retrospective follow-ups, the model recognized that the article 28 wording did not establish a fixed model selection, but could not recover its original reasoning; the article 16 follow-up did not identify the financing paragraph. These accounts are diagnostic, not a fix. No production change is adopted. An isolated low-versus-medium reasoning comparison on six known prepared articles completed and was reviewed with setting labels hidden. Both settings stated an example model as actually used in all three article-28 answers. In article 29, stock-market flows were blurred into infrastructure spending in 3/3 low and 0/3 medium answers, but the medium answers did not retain that stock-flow statement. Other attribution and context concerns remained. These are selected-case observations, not a production acceptance or proof of cause. See the [full results](STEP1-REASONING-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) and [36-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-reasoning-fidelity-2026-11-07/review.html). The [source-fidelity prompt audit](STEP1-FIDELITY-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) maps observed failures to the complete request and signed standards. The council and an independent reader reviewed a field-local wording hypothesis that asks summaries to preserve source qualifications. In a six-article isolated comparison, all three answers in each arm still described an example model as part of the proposed system without preserving the “such as” qualification; other context and linking concerns remained. The field-description candidate is not adopted. [Full results](STEP1-SUMMARY-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) and [36-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-summary-fidelity-2026-11-07/review.html) preserve the cases and limits; the council is investigating the next step.
320:The continuing council has reviewed a possible structured representation of source statements and link explanations within the same extraction call. It is an untested diagnostic, not an adopted design or demonstrated fix. A prior reviewer missed a reversed relationship even with the full article and one evidence record; the proposed representation must still preserve legitimate inference, multiple sources and existing pairs. An independent audit found that the first draft could exclude the very link explanation being investigated, and the council chose not to run this enlarged diagnostic as the next test. A further low-versus-medium comparison completed on six manually selected real articles outside the original 49-item replay, with one fixed 22-thesis exploratory state for both settings. These use the corpus cleaned text, not the earlier prepared-source path; their prior exposure outside the clean replay is unknown. Earlier mixed results and regressions remain unresolved. The [council record](STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) and [draft experiment](../poc/data/clean-step1-structured-claims-2026-11-07/design.md) preserve the discussion, offline checks and limits.
322:The additional-source comparison produced mixed results: both settings retained several central claims, while the higher setting also produced unsupported CPU-related links and date-field errors in individual answers. An independent review also found 13 fields across eight repeated answers that appear to use an event or data period as the original statement date; ambiguous cases were kept separate. In three later, specifically prompted continuations, the model correctly distinguished those time references; this does not establish the original cause or a fix. A quotation found in the cleaned input was not contiguous in the captured original page. These are selected-case observations, not population rates or a reason to change the production setting. [Results](STEP1-ADDITIONAL-SOURCES-RESULTS.md) and [article-by-article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-reasoning-new-sources-2026-11-07/review.html) show the source, context, answers and limitations.
324:An additional [date and link support review](STEP1-DATE-AND-LINK-CENSUS.md) examined the original replay's non-null date-text fields and 76 links from three later test articles. It separates event periods, uncertain original dates, source facts found elsewhere in an article, and clearly labeled inference. The results identify recurring issues and an open link-citation contract question; they do not establish a fix or a population-wide error rate.
326:An isolated date diagnostic compared one revised `statement_date_text` schema description with the saved request on eight known source/context pairs. It leaves all schema types, evidence pairing, prompt body and production configuration unchanged. Controls include a third-party statement independently dated to the article day, explicit announcements and a dated party release. Independent review identified risks of losing valid metadata-only or announcement dates; these were inspected alongside omissions and linkage. The results were mixed, including persistent date mistakes, unsupported-link concerns and literal `"null"` text in two fields; the candidate is not adopted. [Results](STEP1-DATE-DESCRIPTION-RESULTS.md) and [48-answer replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-date-description-2026-11-07/review.html) retain the details. The [design](../poc/data/clean-step1-date-description-2026-11-07/design.md) records the exact scope and prior attempts.
330:A further isolated comparison completed on eight new source IDs selected before reading article contents using a recorded seed and a structured inventory of available clean-Step-1 artifacts. It reuses the same unmodified date-text candidate and the same fixed exploratory thesis context for both arms. The selection cannot prove absence from all past work or exclude syndicated stories under different IDs. An independent source review found related-story contamination and unlabeled generated summaries in some cleaned inputs; these were assessed separately. One company article lost its supported full statement date in all three candidate answers, while another preserved its date in both arms; the candidate is not adopted. [Results and replay](STEP1-DATE-NEW-SOURCES-RESULTS.md) and the [selection design](../poc/data/clean-step1-date-unseen-2026-11-07/design.md) retain the evidence.
332:An independent [thesis-link review](STEP1-ROUTING-CENSUS.md) examined all 214 links across the eight preregistered articles. It records unsupported topic bridges and explanations that negate their own link, alongside valid indirect context and source-supported prospective topics. These are selected-case findings, not population error rates or a hardcoded relevance filter.
334:A [source-provenance census](STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md) found 163 records across three explicitly recognizable page components where generated-summary content was retained but its label was removed. This is a bounded source-structure count, not a count of model errors. On two NVIDIA pages, the cleaner's template removal and long-line restoration reproduce the mismatch; a general provenance-preserving handoff is under review. An offline probe reproduced the current cleaned text for 163 affected pages and 200 seed-selected additional pages. It retains all candidate original positions, marks transformed bylines, and shows one missing-HTML fallback; positions do not by themselves establish source role. [Lineage replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/review.html). Source content remains available and no production data or prompt has been changed.
336:An [offline source-context observation](STEP1-SOURCE-CONTEXT-OBSERVATIONS.md) compared immediate original-page neighbors with complete adjacent unmatched runs, without modifying inputs. They exposed 144 and 149 respectively of 163 known labels but also many unrelated page lines, and did not cover the TradingKey cases. Thirty preselected pages received a line-by-line human review. These are partial diagnostics, not an adopted cleaning rule. A replay of 153 quotations from 18 saved answers across three selected articles found no changes in quote-verification status under either hypothetical text, but the Ising quotation falsely accepted after cleaning remained falsely accepted because both approaches omitted the intervening lines. A further full-page quote check flagged that Ising passage as unverified; this does not test extraction on the full page. This limited replay does not establish general safety or source meaning. In the earlier 49 completed forward-play articles, a separate count found 248 of 250 saved passage instances located in the captured page and two already unverified; the [observations](STEP1-SOURCE-CONTEXT-OBSERVATIONS.md) distinguish this from semantic accuracy. [Source-context viewer](../poc/data/clean-step1-context-restoration-2026-11-07/review.html).
338:A [saved quote-integrity census](STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) independently matched 360 answers in eight overlapping cohorts to their original requests and results, and reconstructed a further 60-answer source-text comparison separately. It distinguishes exact page location, unverified quotations and cleaned-text-only joins. Five unverified passage instances are full matches under the existing normalizer, including spaces before punctuation. The isolated Step 1 runner now locates a complete normalized match when every matching source slice has the same original bytes. It retains the model quotation, displays the located source text, and records lookup errors without stopping the exploratory stream. The [installed-path review](STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) covers saved-answer replays, Unicode and duplicate controls, resource checks and limitations. This fixes a narrow location issue in the isolated runner; semantic accuracy, original-page provenance and production compatibility remain unproven.
340:A [source-first 49-article coverage review](STEP1-COVERAGE-CENSUS.md) separates text removed before extraction from material present in the input but absent from Luna’s raw answer. It records concrete cases and uncertain matter boundaries, without creating a new golden set or asserting a population recall rate. Previously tested source selection, prompt order and reasoning changes remain unproven. Seven older production-era raw answers returned the same article’s Kyber report and denial, but differed in request context, schema and run date, so the [continuing council](STEP1-COVERAGE-COUNCIL.md) treats the difference as a lead rather than a proven cause.
342:In the continuing council, Codex and Claude Code agreed that repeating one article alone would measure variation rather than identify a remedy. The complete clean prompt already includes examples and asks for a full article reading; no single prompt sentence has been established as the cause of the omissions. One proposed general clarification to the standard's named-party claims and estimates is an untested audit hypothesis, not an adopted prompt. A useful future comparison would hold each article and its pre-article thesis state fixed, interleave control and one candidate, and review complete outputs against the source for missed information, noise, attribution, conditions and links. Its chosen articles and review procedure must be recorded before the sends; selected cases cannot establish production quality. Source text lost before the model call is a separate problem, to diagnose against the existing cleaning code and saved pages. Step 1 remains one call with thesis–evidence pairs. [Council exchange and limits](STEP1-COVERAGE-COUNCIL.md).
344:The [source-loss investigation](STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traced two different ways the historical cleaner can discard article content before Step 1: a financial statement grouped with related-story headlines, and a financing paragraph omitted by the HTML extractor whose final marker prevented the long-line fallback from keeping it. A corpus-wide count identifies thousands of lines meeting the underlying structural conditions, including many unrelated titles, code and advertisements; these are not all lost investment facts. No general source-cleaning repair has been established. A new read-only council is examining the trace and previous source-selection attempts; the original corpus remains unchanged.
346:**Test-data correction:** Alex confirmed on 2026-11-07 that the source-cleaning script and source test data may be changed when their defects are confirmed to affect testing. Preserve the original inputs for comparison and verify the effect of a candidate on real sources. This permission concerns the test source data; it does not change the frozen evaluation standard, gold, production configuration or production database.
350:An existing HTML-tag-based candidate was also replayed across all 6,463 originally fed articles. It reproduced its earlier 49-article comparison, but would change 1,054 pages. For example, it adds unrelated news recommendations to an AI investment report and removes a sourced timeline from an AI training article. This does not establish a general error rate; it shows why HTML tags alone cannot decide whether material belongs to the article. No candidate has been integrated.
352:A [full-page diagnostic](STEP1-FULL-PAGE-DIAGNOSTIC.md) compares the cleaned text with the saved captured page under the same thesis state and instructions. It checks whether Luna can find the target article while preserving its source and ignoring unrelated page material. The captured page can itself be wrong or incomplete, and using it is not an adopted implementation.
354:Two independent readers have completed [source-first notes for the 17 proposed articles](STEP1-FULL-PAGE-SOURCE-NOTES.md), before any new comparison outputs. These notes identify source roles, incomplete captures, uncertain statements and later page material; they are not a new golden set or mandatory extraction checklist.
356:The isolated full-page comparison completed all **102 calls** across 17 articles, with three answers for each input per article. Three independent readers examined the answers against the source, with imperfect condition hiding. In one selected article the page exposed an outlook qualification absent from the cleaned input, and all three page answers included it; in another, all three cleaned-input answers misattributed an unrelated later-story teaser, while the page answers did not. In a third, five of six answers turned an example model into a definite selection despite both inputs preserving the qualification. The [source-backed results](STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) distinguish source loss, attribution and model meaning errors, and the [article-by-article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-full-page-diagnostic-2026-11-07/review.html) shows the inputs and answers. These selected cases do not establish a general accuracy rate or production readiness.
358:The cleaner also switched off 615 of 6,463 articles originally marked for processing. A replay of the current cleaner reproduces all 615 switches, and one confirmed example contains a 9,874-character article compressed to a single line: it fails the cleaner's requirement for three long lines. Other excluded captures are a paywall, a weather page or challenge code. An offline candidate that replaces long-line counts with long-sentence counts would recover 89 records but disable 49 currently processed records, including readable reports; a sample of the 89 includes unrelated pages. These are concrete evidence of a source-test problem and of why simply adjusting a length or count threshold would not resolve it. How many articles were wrongly excluded remains unknown; the [results](STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) link the cases and reproducible comparisons.
360:A [test-source preservation candidate](STEP1-SOURCE-PRESERVATION-DESIGN.md) describes a separately versioned comparison that retains the saved page, the previous cleaned input and both historical processing flags. It is a proposal for making source losses inspectable and exposing previously excluded inputs; it does not assert that a saved page is a complete or correctly identified article. Its database mapping, runner behavior and offline checks are specified before implementation. The existing production source and frozen configuration are not changed by this candidate.
362:The isolated candidate has now been built and its 34,084 records compared with the two source snapshots. A fake-answer run through the actual Step 1 runner confirms that the previously excluded CNBC article reaches the runner with its saved page. Replays also preserve the recorded validation results for 102 saved comparison answers and 49 completed forward-run answers. This verifies the handoff and historical checks, not model extraction or article identity. The [council regroup](STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) records why source cleanup comes first for a stable test and which model-processing errors remain separate.
364:The [source-only controls](STEP1-SOURCE-CONTROLS.md) document a real article, a wrong-page capture and two partial or paywalled captures before any new model run. A partial headline may itself carry a useful attributed statement; a wrong page does not establish that the missing target article was irrelevant. Two additional non-NVDA-tagged bad captures are source controls only and are not placed into an NVIDIA thesis context.
366:The [control-run design](STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-DESIGN.md) sends four eligible captured pages independently with one fixed hypothetical thesis state, plus one comparison using the CNBC article's previous cleaned text. It preserves the existing extraction request and treats missing article content, source attribution and model interpretation as separate questions. All five calls completed; the [replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-capture-controls-2026-11-07/review.html) shows the actual inputs and proposals. In these selected draws, the wrong page yielded no pairs, while the two partial pages yielded qualified proposals. Source review also found summary details whose supporting headline or article passage was not included in the attached quotations. The [results](STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) record the evidence and remaining uncertainty; these draws are not a general quality measurement. A [request audit](STEP1-CITATION-SUPPORT-AUDIT.md) confirms that the current prompt already asks for all passages needed to support each summary; the cause of these omissions is not established.
370:An independent [citation review of all 160 returned records](STEP1-CITATION-COMPLETENESS-49.md) in the historical 49-article run found nine preliminary cases where details appear in the supplied article but not in their own evidence passages. A second reader checked those cases, distinguishing substantive missing conditions from source-identity or contextual antecedents. Meaning changes and whole-article omissions are separate problems, measured in different units; this selected run does not establish which problem is largest in production.
372:The council is also examining whether selecting source-unit IDs could reduce copying errors. An [offline boundary and size check](STEP1-SOURCE-ID-OBSERVATIONS.md) found that the existing quote-repair boundary heuristic splits real abbreviations and that many saved quotations cross its units; a simple numbered JSON representation also increased source-only input size on the checked pages. IDs would not establish semantic support or article identity. No such representation is adopted.
374:### Source-fidelity council: what must be preserved
376:Codex and Claude Code completed five rounds of review of the source-fidelity failures, existing trace and downstream code, and previous experiments. They agree that no tested fix has yet been established. An article 41 answer quotes an Ayar Labs executive accurately but names a Lightmatter executive in its standalone summary; a previous FEV link explanation reverses the relationship described by its source. Missing citation context, incorrect source meaning and contaminated inputs are different failures. Exact quotation matching cannot settle them.
378:The agreed record-by-record review of the original 49-article, 160-evidence run is now complete, with the later 54-answer comparison kept separate. For each evidence item, compare its claims, citations, actor, reporting source, time, qualifications and thesis-link explanation with the complete source. Check real downstream readers and include correct records as controls. The subsequently completed field-order comparison is described above; it did not establish a fix. No automatic split, semantic gate or arbitrary count threshold is adopted. [Full council analysis and prior attempts](STEP1-FIDELITY-COUNCIL.md).
380:The existing clean trace keeps multiple passages, but its Step 3 mapping to the older single-quote evidence store has not been implemented. The existing writer stores an unknown original day as empty; the older Jev comparison and revision-order path can use publication day as a fallback. These are different paths and do not establish that a clean production write has already lost information.
382:### Step 1 joint review: causes and priorities
384:**Execution order approved by Alex:** (1) source text and event ordering, (2) thesis links, (3) faithful summaries and omissions, (4) thesis granularity and noise. Investigate and validate each in that order; retain the original traces and distinguish proven causes from proposals. The detailed findings below are the recorded joint-review consensus.
386:Codex and Claude Code (`claude-fable-5-1`) reviewed the 49 source records, original requests and answers, state, and the clean runner over three rounds. They challenged each other's claims against the saved records and code. Claude read the earlier Codex review before completing its first pass through all 49 articles, so this was **not a fully blind independent review**. The [first](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/claude-round1.md), [second](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/claude-round2.md) and [third](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/claude-round3.md) responses, [Codex's checks](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/CODEX-OBSERVATIONS.md), and local packets are retained for inspection. These are findings and proposed investigations, not approved changes to the architecture or production behavior.
390:| 1. Wrong thesis links | In article 4, valuation and revenue are linked to Nemotron performance; in article 27, a cloud-gaming server is linked to the Shield TV thesis. Three of the 199 returned links explicitly say the item does not inform the selected thesis. Article 18 puts robotics evidence under the Nemotron/NemoClaw thesis; article 41 links general networking details to a specific Kyber setback. One 2027 roadmap statement in article 41 can also be defensible context for that thesis. | Luna returned these links; the clean runner checks reference identity and source location, not whether the link answers the thesis. That separation is deliberate: code does not decide importance. The cause of the model's decision is **not proven**. A forced pairing or insufficient deliberation may contribute; neither is established by one run. | Compare matching decisions against the complete question and outcome, including valid indirect links. Preserve the user's thesis–evidence pairing rule. Do not add a hard-coded list of keywords or make code decide investment relevance. |
391:| 2. Source contamination and order | Article 3's July Firmus text includes two October related-story summaries; the model uses one as Firmus evidence. Article 28 also includes later recommendations, though its output did not cite them. Of 46 records with parseable timezone-aware page timestamps, 26 of 45 adjacent comparisons in that known-timestamp subsequence are inverted relative to the run order; one crosses a New York day. The other three lack comparable timestamps, and page metadata may itself be wrong. An earlier count of 45 and 25 missed a valid `+0000` timestamp. | In article 3, the clean pipeline re-adds long lines the structural extractor omitted; both unrelated snippets meet that fallback. The same fallback retains real guidance and other article text in article 34. In article 28, the structural extractor itself includes the recommendations. The manifest follows fixed processing order, not guaranteed page-publication order; a Google listing time is not automatically the page's original time. | Test source extraction and time provenance separately from model changes. Any cleaning proposal must remove unrelated content without losing real article tails. For a stateful chronological test, define and inspect the timestamp source and missing-time handling; do not rewrite the recorded run. |
394:| 5. Handoff and provenance | The clean trace stores per-link `relevance`; the inspected existing `evidence_links` table stores evidence ID, thesis ID and sequence, not that explanation. Step 2 and the clean Step 3 writer were not run. | This is a future integration risk, not a claim that this experiment wrote or lost data in production. Unmatched source passages and unresolved links remain visible and provisional in the exploratory path. | Verify the actual writer and reader mapping before claiming the explanation or a multi-passage source survives into a dossier. Compare repeated evidence in Step 2, rather than counting Step 1 repeats as a dedup failure. |
398:**Link-setting diagnostic (completed; results below):** use the same stored source and before-state for a few wrong-link examples and valid direct/indirect-link controls; compare same-day `low` and `medium` settings with repeated sends. Have readers inspect source, output, thesis and attribution without seeing the setting or reported token count. Record both wrong attachments and useful evidence lost. This can indicate whether a setting is worth studying; it cannot prove why the model erred or establish production improvement. The frozen historical ruler and gold remain unchanged, and no new golden dataset is required for this exploratory trace review.
400:**First no-cost source test, completed as an investigation, not a replacement pipeline:** compare saved HTML, the structural extractor and the current cleaned text for article 3 and article 28, with article 34 as a positive control for real text that the fallback preserves. Count both unrelated content retained and relevant article content lost before proposing a change. The [candidate census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source_fallback_census.py) found 112 long retained lines across 23 of these 49 records that are absent from the extractor output; these are candidates, **not 112 proven errors**. The [timestamp census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/timestamp_census.py) and raw outputs record its scope and limitations.
402:### Execution 1: source text and event ordering
404:The source investigation used the 49 archived pages and the exact text sent to Luna. It identified both added and missing content:
407:- Article 2 loses a genuine statement that NVIDIA excluded China revenue from its outlook because the headline-removal step removes it. Article 16 loses the reported $1 billion funding and $11 billion valuation sentence because the fallback does not recognize its final symbol as punctuation. These are defects in the supplied source, before Luna reads it.
408:- The same long-line fallback preserves real guidance and other body text in article 34. The default extraction mode removes the unrelated article 3 and 28 examples and restores article 34, but returns unrelated headlines instead of the actual article 16 body. Another existing extractor loses substantive article 28 content. Neither is a safe wholesale replacement. A separate structural candidate recovered the omitted article 2 and 16 passages and removed article 3’s recommendations, but added unrelated headlines and promotional text and missed article 28’s recommendations. Its narrower variant proposed 14 additions across six articles, not all valid. Extractor agreement also either retained promotion or lost real content. These are observed controls, not a complete measurement of all source errors.
411:The author gap is wider than one article: all 34,084 records in both the saved page and cleaned snapshots have an empty `authors` field; the clean runner forwards that value. The assembly script initializes it empty and the cleaner does not populate it. In the 5,848 cleaned, feed-eligible article rows it is also empty. On 16 selected pages with visible named bylines, the existing HTML metadata extractor captured all visible names on 11, some on two and none on three; some wrong names appeared in page metadata even when the metadata identified the correct article URL. It is a source hint, not a safe automatic author replacement. Article 6's cleaned text also omitted the visible author. The [source-only diagnostic design](STEP1-DESIGN.md) tests byline handling without changing a production writer or selecting an unverified name.
413:A separate model-assisted source-preparation experiment asked Luna to identify article lines in the archived whole-page text without rewriting them. One draw on six known pages recovered the real body paragraphs checked in articles 2, 3, 16, 28, 34 and 42, including the previously missing CFO and funding statements; it excluded the identified unrelated recommendations in articles 3, 28 and 34. An independent reader checked the selected text against the page and HTML. But article 2's selected text presents the page's AI-generated summary without its label, and also includes a photo caption and credit. Articles 3 and 16 lose visible author names while the stored author field is empty. All six returned no uncertain lines. Each selected line was copied unchanged from the stored normalized page, with no invalid or repeated IDs; exact copying does not solve source attribution. These six selected pages and one draw do not establish production safety, quality across the source pool, or completeness of a paywalled article.
415:A second isolated source-boundary experiment separated article text, bylines, captions, page-generated summaries and uncertain material, allowing a line to have several roles. An independent reviewer compared one draw on ten selected pages with the saved page and HTML. They found no wholly missing authored body paragraph or visible byline name among those pages, and the explicitly labeled AI summaries were separate from the article role. The CFO and funding statements remained, and the known unrelated recommendations were excluded. Figure captions on two pages were available only in the caption role; article 7's AI-widget disclosure was omitted; and the source of some widget “Next Steps” text is unclear even though the model marked no lines uncertain. A downstream consumer would need the separate source roles and their provenance, not just the article text. These are selected-case observations, not a completeness or accuracy guarantee. A further check used three sends each on ten other articles, randomly selected from the same 49-article corpus. Independent readers found the substantive paragraphs visible in the archived article regions in all 30 outputs, but one output omitted a visible author whose stored `authors` field was empty. Some outlet labels, contributor roles and article/caption roles also require care. The model later identified the missed author when prompted, which does not prove why it missed her initially. These articles appeared in the earlier extraction review, so this is not a fully unseen corpus; the [independent check](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source-prep-check/INDEPENDENT-REVIEW.md) is not a population accuracy estimate. The [second probe](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source-prep-probe-v2/) is not integrated into the pipeline.
417:A subsequent blinded source-role comparison fixed a caption field-name mismatch in both arms, then tested a byline clarification against the corrected baseline: ten sends per arm on article 6 and three per arm on six provenance controls. The author was retained in the source role in 8/10 baseline and 10/10 candidate answers. However, two of three candidate outputs for article 28 incorrectly labeled the host's introduction as generated; all three also selected a site clock as source information. The introduction itself remained selected as article text. The candidate therefore fails the source-attribution controls and is not adopted. This is a selected-case diagnostic, not a population score; [the blind review](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source-byline-comparison/BLIND-REVIEW.md) records the cases and their limits.
419:For the 49 selected records, the archived whole-page text contains about 107,119 local tokenizer tokens versus 49,656 for the current cleaned text; a separate source-preparation call would add those input tokens and another model call before extraction. This is a local token estimate, not a provider bill or a projection over all records. The [six source-preparation traces](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source-prep-probe/) are isolated; no model-selected text has been substituted into the replay or production pipeline.
421:**Status:** the causes above have been traced, but no general source-cleaning or ordering fix is established. Do not silently remove the fallback, switch extractors or invent times for missing records. Compare a candidate against both contamination and genuine-body controls before using it in a new stateful run. The [source fallback census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/source_fallback_census.json), [headline-filter census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/headline-filter-census.json), [extractor comparison](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/default-extractor-comparison.json), and [corrected timestamp census](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/timestamp_census_v2.json) preserve the local investigation. The censuses identify candidates, not independently adjudicated error counts.
425:### Execution 2–4: link, fidelity and topic review
429:For articles 4 and 27, Luna was shown its own stored answer and the original context and asked to review the links. It acknowledged both contradictions. In article 4 it suggested a new, evidence-backed revenue thesis; in article 27 it suggested no pair. It attributed the errors to an overly broad interpretation of indirect relevance. This is a **retrospective account**, not proof of the original cause. The full [requests and responses](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/link-account-probes/) are retained. A controlled exploratory comparison resent eight unchanged saved requests and their original before-states three times at low and three at medium reasoning. Independent readers reviewed anonymous outputs before the settings were revealed. In five selected wrong-link scenarios, 6 of 15 low-setting answers contained at least one clear wrong-product or unrelated-question link, compared with 0 of 15 medium-setting answers in that specific class. Other links and source-fidelity issues remained, and the examples were selected after the original run, so this is not a general accuracy estimate or proof of cause. The 24 low responses used 45,091 completion tokens versus 106,839 for the 24 medium responses; these are usage counts, not a cost or latency comparison. [Blind review and limitations](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/link-setting-probe/BLIND-REVIEW.md) records the cases. The experiment did not advance state or change the production setting. A second isolated test compared the saved prompt with a candidate that makes the existing final check actionable and makes the technical-comparison exclusion explicit. Three independent readers inspected six unlabeled answers for each of three known articles before the prompt labels were revealed. Every candidate and baseline answer still retained excluded technical comparisons, while also retaining the IBM integration, relevant AMD competitor comparison and Perplexity usage in the applicable controls. A candidate answer additionally misstated which company’s hardware was in a 100 kW rack. The candidate is not adopted. This small, selected test does not establish a general rate; the [blind review](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/benchmark-prompt-probe/BLIND-REVIEW.md) records the outputs and limits. Neither test changes the current prompt or database.
431:For source fidelity, article 29 changes flows into AI infrastructure **stocks** into infrastructure **spending**, which the source does not say. Article 34's input contains revenue guidance, margins, its China assumption, and a reported delay and denial, none of which appear in the answer. Article 36 omits reported management revenue **visibility**, not realized revenue. In articles 17 and 37, a factual result and an analyst or author's interpretation appear under one fact tier. The summaries identify the commentary as such; article 37's `original_party` field names only the company, so whether that field alone gives a downstream reader enough context needs review. The standard allows a mixed single matter to use its main claim's tier; separate matters should be evaluated separately. Article 34 also attributes a report to unnamed sources although the supplied article only says reports. The exact source and raw answer establish these examples, but not why the model made each choice. The request instructs the model to preserve meaning, attribution and guidance; it asks for separation when attribution would blur, while allowing a mixed statement to use its main claim's tier. Those instructions can coexist when applied to the correct unit; whether they were applied correctly to these items remains an analyst judgment. Article 34's supplied text also contains unrelated recommendations.
435:**Status:** these reviews identify source-backed errors and prompt tensions; they do not yet establish a general fix or justify editing the prompt. The original 49-item answers and states remain unchanged. In a separate follow-up, the model recognized article 29’s substitution and several omissions in article 34; on article 8 it described the benchmark items but read the rival-comparison exception more broadly than the original prompt states. These are retrospective answers to leading questions, not proof of the original decision. The [full continuation requests and answers](../poc/data/clean-step1-review-consensus/fidelity-account-probes/) and [request audit](STEP1-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) are preserved. The controlled comparison is a diagnostic on selected known cases, not a fresh stream, acceptance test or general accuracy estimate.
437:### July 8–9 Step 1 replay
439:The [July 8–9 article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-2026-07-08-09/review.html) records an exploratory run of 49 selected NVDA-tagged articles in source order, starting with an empty isolated thesis library. Five July 8 articles completed; article 6 failed source-passage validation and stopped the run. The remaining 43 articles were not processed. Four theses were in the isolated library after the completed articles. This is not a production write or a golden evaluation.
441:The failed article's model proposals are visible as unaccepted output; they were not added to the library. The first proposed quotation replaced the source words “The GPU giant” with “Nvidia,” so it was not an exact source passage. On a copy of the answer, restoring the full original phrase makes all three proposed evidence items pass source-location checks; this is a manual diagnostic, not an automatic recovery or a semantic correctness check. Article 5 reused the revenue-growth thesis opened by article 4.
445:The stored article text also includes two recommendations from October that were appended to the July article; the underlying stored page identifies them as related articles. All six comparison outputs quoted one of those later snippets as evidence. The article's stored day is July 8 in New York while its page displays July 9 in Sydney; the page timestamp represents the same instant in both time zones. The listing timestamp is a separate value. This is not itself a date error. The replay's fixed source order is not proof of publication-time order, and even a verified quotation can be wrong evidence if the supplied source text contains later material. These issues must be addressed before treating this replay as a reliable chronological assessment; the stored run is preserved unchanged.
447:The trace and reading page are local run artifacts, not included in Git. If available, regenerate the page with `python3 clean-arch/step1/render_replay.py --source poc/data/clean-step1-2026-07-08-09/trace.sqlite --output poc/data/clean-step1-2026-07-08-09/review.html --annotations poc/data/clean-step1-2026-07-08-09/replay-notes.json`.
449:The [subsequent rerun](../poc/data/clean-step1-2026-07-08-09-rerun/review.html) used the revised prompt, the same 49 selected items and an empty isolated library. It stopped on article 1: one proposed source passage added “NVIDIA” before the source's “NemoClaw,” so the quotation was not contiguous. No thesis state advanced and the other 48 articles were not processed. The failed answer and its proposed pairs are preserved in the rerun replay. The known source contamination and publication-time ordering limitations remain unresolved.
451:### Step 1 run replay
453:The [article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-h200-2026-11-06/review.html) shows the actual three-article Luna run: the thesis proposals or existing thesis references, associated evidence and recorded source passages for each article. The reader shows review notes for the known incomplete citation and source limitations. It is an exploratory run using the earlier single-passage schema, not a golden answer or a test of the revised multi-passage prompt.
455:The replay is generated from a local run database, which is not included in Git. If that local run is available, regenerate the reading page with `python3 clean-arch/step1/render_replay.py --source poc/data/clean-step1-h200-2026-11-06/trace.sqlite --output poc/data/clean-step1-h200-2026-11-06/review.html --annotations poc/data/clean-step1-h200-2026-11-06/replay-notes.json`. The architecture page and prompt drafts remain readable without the local trace.
457:### Exploratory playforward: H200 articles
459:This is a manual simulation of Step 1, not a Luna model run, a frozen golden reference or a measurement. It uses three cleaned records from the local source database in their stored replay-day order, with an initially empty hypothetical NVIDIA thesis library. This is a selected example for inspecting the design, not evidence of broad performance. The old production Luna prompt was consulted as a reference, but it proposes candidates and leaves folding into an existing question to later code; the clean design asks Step 1 itself to identify the existing thesis.
474:**2. July 9 — [Why China is finally letting AI firms buy the Nvidia H200](https://www.scmp.com/tech/policy/article/3360027/game-changer-why-china-finally-letting-its-ai-firms-buy-nvidia-h200).** A source with knowledge of the matter tells the South China Morning Post that China plans to allow selected companies, including Alibaba, to buy limited numbers.
480:**Library after article 2:** the same two theses. Step 1 proposes the article's evidence with the existing thesis; deciding whether it repeats an earlier statement belongs to Step 2, not this simulation.
491:The precise boundaries between these two theses, and which additional material an analyst would retain from each complete source, need independent review. The exercise illustrates how to save before-state, article, proposed pairs and after-state. It does not establish a finalized golden answer or acceptance rule.
497:## 9. Walk one item through the system
502:2. **Extract:** Step 1 returns the existing thesis linked to the 25% statement and its source. It does not need to read all of the thesis's old evidence to extract this pair.
504:4. **Compare:** Jev identifies the 25% statement as a revision of the earlier forecast, if it is given enough information and judges the relationship correctly.
505:5. **Save:** Step 3 saves the 25% statement, its source and its link to the thesis, and records its revision relationship to the 12% statement. The earlier evidence remains available.
510:## 10. Open design questions
516:5. **Saving:** What are the exact record, link and write contracts for theses, evidence, sources and relationships?
521:## 11. Design boundary
525:## 12. Step 1 prompt: c8, adopted (D387)
550:| 50 fresh, unused articles (2026-09-05 to 09-07, a holiday weekend with no NVIDIA posts), one draw, two readers and a third on disputes | helpful 132/139; wrongly opened theses 1/10; search 141/143 available repeat pairs (141/145 for the whole system; one 11-article story supplies 55 pairs; revisions barely exercised); clear wrong merges 1/57 (2 overlaps); detail retention 22 = 22 (a storage comparison, not a usability test) |
555:### What the model receives
557:- The whole source item, with available source, speaker and publication information.
561:### System prompt
566:You are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one source item for the company in scope (current_entity); "the company" below is that
581:- source_item: the complete text and its source metadata.
628:  source, forecast or direction alone does not make a new thesis. Related questions can be separate when their outcomes matter
644:  source supports it.
682:  product already on sale; another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks; an annual quantity after next year (an
692:- If an open thesis tracks sales growth, a revised sales forecast is evidence for it; do not open a second thesis to repeat it.
705:  available. The item's publisher and publication date come from its source metadata.
715:- rumour: the item attributes the claim to unnamed sources or calls it unconfirmed.
722:Apply the first matching category in that order. An unnamed-source or unconfirmed claim remains a rumour even if it describes an
739:### Closing check
744:Before you return the answer, check it once. Read each evidence item back as the analyst following the company and keep it when you would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, about the company or its customers, suppliers, competitors, regulators or the economy. Ask first whether it is new: repeated common knowledge, a long-standing situation retold and an unargued view are noise. Do not drop a reported step, figure, guidance, forecast or decision because the item retells it or another outlet reported it before: a later step compares each statement with the evidence already stored and records a repeat there. Guidance, a forecast, a plan or a decision still in force keeps its place even when retold, including a change to a plan, design or schedule that another outlet reported days or weeks before. Judge the action or information itself, not its verb tense, and do not take a statement as old because its date is missing. Fitting one of the four kinds does not keep an item. A missing amount, date or scale never drops an item on its own: a forecast, a rumour, a counter-example or a distant or conditional statement is judged like any other.
753:- the same fact said again in other words within this item: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording ("a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years"; "cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total"); a deal one party signs under a wider programme, with its own amount or capacity, is its own evidence item, and a fact another outlet reported before is not a repeat within this item;
762:### Proposed per-item input
764:The following is an illustrative user-message payload. It is data for this article, not the system prompt or the output schema. The item-level publisher and publication date stay with the source item; an evidence statement's original speaker and date can be different. Whether the runtime sends this as serialized JSON or equivalent labelled text remains to be decided.
779:  "source_item": {
783:    "source_uri": "https://example.com/article",
789:### Structured-output schema
884:     "source_passages",
903:     "source_passages": {
906:      "description": "Contiguous verbatim passages from this source item that jointly support the summary; return each passage separately.",
920:      "description": "Party whose action, figure or claim is reported; null if unknown. Use the reporting outlet or author as the original party only for its own estimate, analysis or unnamed-source report."
1028:         "description": "How this evidence informs the referenced thesis, distinguishing source claims from inference."
1041:      "description": "Source tier of the statement; an unconfirmed or unnamed-source claim takes precedence as rumour."
1076:Application checks are needed for cross-record and source rules the schema alone cannot enforce: each existing target must resolve to the supplied open list; each new target must resolve to one new thesis in the same answer; each new thesis must be referenced by evidence; local IDs must be unique within their list; each evidence item must have a link; and every evidence item must contain at least one nonempty source passage, and every passage must come from this item. Code retrieves an existing thesis description from the supplied list rather than asking Luna to echo it. These are the intended source and identity relationships, not proof that the source supports every summary claim or that an analyst agrees with the thesis. In the isolated exploratory runner, unmatched passages and unresolved links are retained and explicitly marked as provisional rather than blocking the stream; an unlinked new thesis is not added to the state. Production integration remains open.
1078:Alex approved returning the evidence tier, original statement date and described period in Step 1. The draft also identifies the original party and reporting speaker separately. Mapping these fields to the existing writer and readers remains to be verified, including how source-specific attribution and tier are retained when a repeat adds another source. The signed historical standard uses more specific opening categories and check-date rules; Alex has clarified that the clean design may open a meaningful topic without a known decision date when later observable developments can help assess it. Other differences remain subject to audit. This draft must not be described as fully compatible with that standard or the production schema without a separate review.
1080:### Illustrative output for the sample input (earlier Luna draft, kept as history)
1113:      "source_passages": [
1138:      "source_passages": [
1147:The second evidence item is written once, contains two separately quoted passages from the same article, and links to both theses; the sales connection is explicitly an inference rather than a source fact. If there are no pairs, the output is `{"new_theses": [], "evidence": []}`. The example explains the proposed contract; it is not a golden case or an actual Luna response.
1149:### Mapping to the Step 1 diagram
1161:### Request review and remaining limits
1163:The review covered the visible system prompt, sample input, proposed schema and sample response together, with an independent reader. It found three places needing more than formatting: the thesis bar did not explain which business outcome the question could change; the within-item repeat instruction could hide distinct useful facts; and the output did not distinguish the original party from the reporting speaker. The draft above addresses those points. Its expanded examples teach these distinctions across industries; their added length is intentional for that purpose, not a measured quality improvement.
1165:As historical context, two saved responses and their input articles were read in the local [older-run archive](../poc/data/e2e-v83-seed-d2/archive.sqlite): call 4 records two descriptions of an investment in the same article, while call 7 reports a company's plan alongside a separate party's capacity figures. These are examples from an older request, not outputs of this draft. They illustrate why the new request must distinguish repeated descriptions from distinct facts and the original party from the reporter; they do not establish that the revised prompt fixes either case.
1167:The assembled clean request and partial forward-run answers now exist in the local trace. This review is not the measured failure-class audit required for an existing measured prompt. The exact thesis-opening boundary, storage mapping for evidence tier and source provenance, handling of ambiguous thesis identity, and compatibility with the existing ledger are still open. The partial run and source contamination do not establish that the draft works end to end.
1169:## 13. Step 2 prompt: Jev working draft
1175:**Repeat decision:** `same_statement` is the comparison needed for Alex's chosen repeat behavior. The application must retain which existing evidence candidate produced that result so Step 3 can reuse it, add the incoming source and create any missing thesis link. A similar topic or number alone is not enough to establish a repeat. This is a clean-design requirement, not a claim that the current writer already does it.
1177:### What Jev receives
1180:- The incoming evidence, including its available source, speaker and stated time.
1184:### Proposed comparison instruction
1188:Use the supplied statements and their available source, speaker and time.
1193:retelling or another source reporting that claim.
1197:source. Merely being later or about the same subject is not enough.
1200:than a repeat or revision of the same claim. Distinct original claimants can
1201:make separate claims; a different reporting source may simply retell one.
1203:Consider the wording, figures, speaker, original source, what each statement
1210:### Input to be inserted at runtime
1217:<statement and available source, speaker and time>
1220:<one search result and its available source, speaker and time>
1225:## 14. Existing components to reference during the rebuild
1229:### Thesis and evidence database
1231:The documented local serving default is the existing [thesis ledger](../poc/data/served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite), together with its [source item database](../poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite). The ledger stores both theses and evidence; the diagram's two libraries do not require two physical databases. The source item database holds the original material used to resolve evidence sources. These data files are local and are not included in a fresh Git checkout. [Local data and service defaults](../README.md#local-requirements-and-data).
1233:Read-only inspection of this local serving snapshot found 68 theses, 4,298 evidence records, 6,578 thesis-evidence links, 2,023 recorded repeat relationships and 121 revision relationships. Its 746 distinct source item IDs all resolve in the documented local item database. These counts describe this snapshot, not the state of any live deployment.
1235:The existing [storage implementation](../src/hivemind/views/store.py) defines `views`, `evidence`, `evidence_sources`, `evidence_links`, `same_claims`, `revisions` and `judgements`. Evidence records retain the source quotation and attribution; links connect evidence to theses; repeat and revision relationships preserve earlier records. The tables are append-only. This is a strong basis for reuse, but compatibility with the new Step 1 output, Step 2 outcomes, writer, readers and any actually deployed database must be verified before adopting it as the new system's write contract.
1237:### Source pool and golden validation data
1243:| **34,084 stored source records** | The full local collection of source records in the stand-in Hummingbird database. | The pool from which a replay selects source material for processing. The current replay does not send every record to extraction. |
1244:| **148 golden articles** | Selected articles with expected extraction answers: 118 development and 30 test. | Comparing extraction output with the existing golden reference. These articles are a selection from the source database, not an additional 148 documents. |
1246:The 148 golden articles are not the total amount of source material available. Conversely, the 34,084 figure does not mean all records are eligible for processing or have golden answers. The 30 test articles were used in previous experiments and are not a fresh unseen test for the rebuild.
1248:### Source data standing in for Hummingbird
1250:The existing replay uses the local [cleaned source item database](../poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite) as a stand-in for the incoming Hummingbird stream. Its records come from collected documents, including news, filings and official material; they are not invented articles. The [corpus pipeline](../poc/corpus/README.md) assembled and cleaned them for experiments. Each record can contain a title, publisher, publication date, source URL, company identifiers and cleaned text; `text_page` retains the fetched page text. This source database is separate from the thesis and evidence ledger.
1254:The existing [replay entry](../scripts/replay.py) defaults to this database, though a run can select another. The [source reader](../src/hivemind/testdb.py) reads the chosen date window in publication order, including feed items and identified copies; [dispatch](../src/hivemind/dispatch.py) then decides which items enter extraction. Thus the number actually processed depends on the window, filters and dispatch. This is a historical replay path; a live Hummingbird connector and its contract have not been established for the clean architecture.
1256:### Existing extraction reference split
1258:The existing frozen extraction reference is a small labelled selection, not a split of every record in the source database. Its files name different source items, with no shared `input_id` between the two sets:
1265:All 148 IDs resolve in the local source database. Of the 118 development records, 117 are currently marked `feed = 1`; all 30 test records are. The source categories in each set are 20/73/25 and 5/19/6 for T6/T7/T8 respectively. The two sets have different time windows, but the test set has been used in prior experiments, including full-set runs in the [historical experiment log](../docs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/loop-log.md); it must not be described as a fresh unseen test for the rebuild. NVDA appears in the stored company identifiers of all 118 development records and 26 of the 30 test records.
1267:These files are the existing frozen reference, not an approved evaluation set for the clean architecture. Their applicability to the new thesis-and-evidence output and linking needs to be assessed; no new split or acceptance criteria is decided here. The source item database and the golden references serve different purposes: source items are input material, while golden references are expected answers for selected items.
1269:### Diagram-to-code map
1273:| Hummingbird stream | [Item dispatch](../src/hivemind/dispatch.py), [replay entry](../scripts/replay.py), [source item database](../poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite) | The existing path accepts stored items and records processing. A live Hummingbird event connector and its contract have not been verified in this map. |
1278:| Evidence database | [Ledger store](../src/hivemind/views/store.py), [evidence writer](../src/hivemind/views/writing.py) | Evidence, sources and thesis links already exist in the same ledger. Verify the new writes and existing reader behavior on a copy before choosing how to reuse the writer. |
1282:| Repeat, revision and new outcomes | [Evidence comparison](../src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py), [evidence writer](../src/hivemind/views/writing.py) | Existing code records these relationships. The clean design must specify the outcome and uncertainty contract before reuse is accepted. |
1283:| Step 3: save and link | [Writer](../src/hivemind/views/writing.py), [store](../src/hivemind/views/store.py), [item orchestration](../src/hivemind/views/round.py) | Existing code writes theses, evidence, source links and relationships. Its current input contract includes old matching decisions and must not be assumed to accept the new handoff directly. |
1285:| Dossier and source reading | [Bundle reader](../src/hivemind/bundle.py), [service](../src/hivemind/service.py), [HTTP entry](../scripts/hivemind_api.py) | Existing readers assemble thesis, evidence and source information. Their coverage of repeat, revision and provenance relationships must be checked against the new dossier requirements. |
1287:One concrete reader boundary matters for this reuse: the local serving snapshot records a revision from `e191` to `e280` under `v8`, and a repeat from `e6` to `e16` under `v1`. A read-only call to the existing bundle reader returns the newer evidence records without those revision or same-claim relationships. The ledger retains them, and the [store's evidence reader](../src/hivemind/views/store.py) reads them; the [bundle reader](../src/hivemind/bundle.py) currently reads the older `replacements` relationship instead. These are the existing matcher's recorded decisions; their semantic correctness was not reviewed here. This is a reader compatibility finding for these inspected cases, not a claim that the underlying evidence is missing or that every reader has the same behavior.
1289:Do not write to the documented serving snapshot as part of this mapping. Any future compatibility exercise must identify the actual target database and source store, use an appropriate copy, and verify both new writes and existing reads. Reusing data and storage does not imply adopting every old prompt, routing rule or judgement behavior.
1291:## 15. Architecture challenge: questions before building
1297:3. **Evidence without a thesis: resolved as an output design.** Alex's direction is that Step 1 outputs thesis and evidence together: either evidence for an existing thesis or a proposed new thesis with evidence. The working prompt states this pairing. There is no separate standalone-evidence output in this design; this is a product choice, not proof that every source fact will be extracted.
1298:4. **Can the evidence history be reconstructed? An implementation and validation question for the existing flow.** Jev's role in Step 2 is to decide whether evidence repeats or revises a prior statement, or is new. Search must first return the relevant prior evidence, and the saved relationship must reach the dossier reader. In the inspected local database, repeat and revision relationships exist, but the existing bundle reader does not expose the two inspected relationships described in section 14. This does not require a new architecture stage; it identifies retrieval and reader behavior to verify when implementing the existing stages.
1299:5. **What should judgement see?** Two different sources can independently support or contradict one another without being a repeat or revision. Step 2 can save them as different evidence, but whether those relationships must be recorded explicitly or can be understood by Step 4 remains open. Step 4's input and output are not yet designed.
1301:The next proposed work is the Step 1 forward run and source-by-source trace review in section 17. The stored repeat and revision relationships and the dossier reader remain relevant to the later saving-and-reading milestone. Their historical model-generated labels are observations, not automatically verified reference answers.
1303:## 16. Future design: entity relationships and cross-entity linkage
1307:### Why this is needed
1311:### Separate, asynchronous relationship maintenance
1315:- **Cold start:** preprocess an initial map of entities and their explicit relationships from available sources. The sources, coverage and way to resolve entity identity are open.
1319:An entity relationship identifies a possible path of relevance; it does not by itself prove that an event affected another entity, or establish the direction or size of an impact. The system needs to retain the reason and supporting source for a link, including whether a connection is reported in a source or inferred. The representation of uncertainty and time remains open.
1321:### Illustration: a supplier disruption
1323:This is a hypothetical example, not a statement about actual companies. Suppose a source reports a disruption at Foxconn, and a previously established relationship indicates that Foxconn supplies Apple. The system could maintain one disruption thesis and its evidence, then identify Apple as potentially relevant through the supplier relationship. If later evidence changes the assessment, or the supplier relationship is found to have ended before the disruption, the affected connection would need to be reconsidered. The original source and the history of how the connection was reached should remain understandable. The exact reassessment and update mechanism is not yet designed.
1325:### Questions to resolve before cross-entity production use
1328:2. How are the initial map and its sources built, checked and refreshed, and how is incomplete or conflicting information handled?
1329:3. How are a relationship's source, reason, uncertainty and valid dates recorded and updated?
1337:## 17. Implementation plan for review
1341:**Implementation progress:** the isolated [Step 1 runner](step1/README.md) and its [design](STEP1-DESIGN.md) exercise the handoff with recorded responses. The bounded live path reuses the existing model client and archive and records source, request, raw response and changing test state. The [prompt audit](STEP1-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) records the evidence-boundary review and Alex’s opening rule. On 2026-11-06, a selected three-article Luna forward run completed on an isolated test library. Its local trace and source-by-source review are exploratory, not a production-compatible extractor or a general quality result. A source-passage repair in the first article lost part of the cited support; investigate it before relying on that path.
1343:### Working boundaries
1345:- Work in the clean-architecture area on the `clean-arch` branch. The existing source database and thesis/evidence ledger are inputs and references; inspect and reuse their components where they fit. Establish compatibility before treating any existing code as the new contract.
1349:- The PRD is the architecture source; regenerate the HTML after each document change. Keep run traces and findings identifiable by run, separate from the agreed architecture and from historical artifacts.
1351:### Milestone 0 — Prepare the first Step 1 run
1355:1. Use the field map in section 18 to identify the source data and fields needed for the first run. Keep the meaning of unknown values explicit. A full census of production writers and readers belongs before milestone 3, not before the isolated Step 1 test.
1359:5. Review the complete assembled request and response checks as a unit: instructions, source and open-thesis payload, structured-output schema and field descriptions, followed by the code that reads them. The prompt must not ask for a value the schema or reader cannot represent. Apply the repository's prompt-audit and independent-review rules; the draft has not had a measured clean-design run to audit.
1363:### Milestone 1 — Make Step 1 runnable and inspectable
1365:**Goal:** send an ordered selection of real source articles to Luna, one at a time, while the test thesis library grows.
1367:1. **Choose the input and starting state.** Read the local source database through an explicit path. Select one entity, a bounded date range and an ordered list of eligible articles. Record their IDs, dates, source information and the order used. Define the initial open-thesis library explicitly: an empty library can exercise new theses, while a copied existing library can exercise reuse. The exact first selection and initial state are still to be chosen.
1368:2. **Implement the reviewed handoff.** Supply the article and source fields, relevant company tags, and complete open-thesis identities and descriptions. Consume the reviewed response for existing-thesis evidence, new thesis with evidence, and no result. Keep source passages and attribution. The field mapping and any unresolved thesis-identity boundary must be reviewed in milestone 0.
1371:5. **Capture a readable trace.** For every item preserve the source article, the exact company tags and thesis context supplied, request configuration and schema, raw model answer, interpreted thesis–evidence pairs, and library state before and after. Record failures with their cause and the state reached. Preserve the run's sequence so a reviewer can follow a mistake into later items.
1372:6. **Keep the exploratory replay moving.** If a source passage cannot be matched, retain the article, model's original passage and evidence, mark that passage unverified, and advance the isolated test-library state so the remaining articles can be reviewed. If an evidence link references a thesis the model did not supply, retain the unresolved link for review without inventing or adding a thesis; a proposed new thesis without a resolved evidence link is not added to the open state. The affected and subsequent states are provisional and are not production records. Do not present a partial or inferred passage as a verified quotation. Malformed responses and schema failures retain the raw response and stop the run. Record any source correction and its original wording; do not silently drop a pair or retry. Alex chose this exploratory behavior on 2026-11-06 to avoid mechanical checks stopping the whole review.
1373:7. **Review the forward run.** Read the complete articles beside the traces and states. Inspect both what Luna returned and information it missed; check source meaning, attribution, new-versus-existing thesis decisions, links and duplicate accumulation. Report the articles inspected and concrete findings. The intended review is not a prebuilt golden set; a limited run does not prove overall quality.
1375:**Milestone output:** a reproducible ordered run, its before/after state and raw traces, and a source-backed review of what worked and failed. Before any model send, check the exact assembled request and its reader using stored or fake responses, and run the applicable no-cost preflight. The existing production preflight is not assumed to exercise the new clean-design path; establish what it covers and what no-cost checks the new path needs. The first-run size, model cost budget and article selection remain open and should be explicit before a paid run. Review the resulting traces and state before deciding what quality is sufficient to proceed. This exploratory review does not replace the frozen ruler for historical measurements or product acceptance; no such run has been performed for this plan.
1377:**Review before moving on:** Does Step 1 produce useful pairs, retain the relevant source meaning, and reuse the existing thesis when the library contains it? Which observed failures are prompt, input, output-shape or test-library issues? A finding is traced to the article and raw output before changing the request or code.
1379:### Milestone 2 — Retrieve and compare prior evidence
1385:3. Connect Jev to compare incoming evidence with retrieved candidates for repeat, revision or new evidence. Define the response and what happens when several candidates conflict or the comparison is uncertain before choosing write behavior. Keep attribution, original date, amount and source available to the comparison.
1386:4. Review search and Jev traces against real source passages. A correct Jev decision with a missed candidate does not prove retrieval works; a retrieved candidate does not prove the comparison is correct. Choose the review set and criteria as part of this milestone.
1390:### Milestone 3 — Save and read evidence and links
1394:1. Specify the write contract for a new thesis, new evidence for an existing thesis, a repeat and a revision. For a repeat, reuse the existing evidence and add the incoming source and any missing thesis link, per Alex's decision. Earlier statements remain available for revisions. Decide the storage mapping for one evidence item linked to multiple theses before implementing that case.
1395:2. On a copy of the intended ledger, check the existing store, writer and reader against the new contract. In particular, trace saved repeat and revision relationships through the actual dossier reader; section 14 records two local examples that the existing bundle reader does not expose. Check source passages before treating an old relationship as correct.
1396:3. Implement or adapt the smallest compatible write-and-read path and verify that the resulting thesis, evidence, sources and relationships can be read back. Define how repeated processing and an interrupted write should behave before connecting an ongoing stream.
1400:### Milestone 4 — Connect and review the four-step flow
1402:**Goal:** take a bounded source sequence through the completed extraction, comparison and saving path; design judgement before implementing it.
1405:2. Run the completed Steps 1–3 together on an isolated library and inspect the source-to-dossier trace, including search misses, comparison decisions and saved links. Verify that a later item sees the state produced by the earlier item.
1406:3. Only after the Step 4 contract is decided, integrate and review judgement using saved evidence. Define the actual Hummingbird event contract and live integration separately; the local source database remains the stand-in until then.
1410:### Decisions to resolve as the work reaches them
1415:| First paid forward run | Entity, initial state, ordered source items, run size, budget and what the review will inspect. |
1417:| Production-compatible saving | Existing ledger compatibility, write and read contracts, repeated processing and interrupted writes. |
1418:| Step 4 and live integration | Judgement contract, source event contract and timing. |
1422:## 18. Luna draft and existing production compatibility review
1426:### The concrete compatibility check
1442:### What differs and why it matters
1447:| Evidence and attribution | Evidence includes `id`, `quote`, `one_line`, `speaker`, `original_who`, `original_day`, `tier`, `event_time` and `links`. | Evidence has a response-local ID, source passages, summary, attribution, tier, original statement date and date text, described period and per-link relevance. | Analogous fields need a tested mapping; the described period is not automatically equivalent to `event_time`, and local identity differs from a stored ID. The current writer does not write per-link relevance. In the 49-article exploratory forward trace, 63 of 160 evidence items have multiple passages; one survey item needs its second passage to identify the 60-person population. This confirms that dropping additional passages loses source context in an actual output, while the correct storage and reader mapping is still open. [Handoff evidence and counts](STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md). Unknown-date representation also differs. |
1448:| New thesis | A candidate includes the object, question, kind, year, due date, outcome ranges, typed identity fields, opening reason and evidence IDs. | A new proposal has a question, tracked outcome, period when known and response-local ID, referenced by evidence; code supplies the entity. | The existing thesis creation and downstream judgement contracts need additional information that the draft does not supply. Missing dates, outcome ranges, source tiers or research meaning cannot safely be manufactured just to satisfy the old writer. Whether Step 4 needs outcome ranges remains open. |
1453:The existing [writer](../src/hivemind/views/writing.py) consumes the production-shaped evidence and candidate records. It stores source, speaker, original party, original day, tier and event time; tier and original timing also participate in its revision checks. The [ledger tables](../src/hivemind/views/store.py) already represent thesis–evidence links, repeat relationships and revisions. The finding is about the contracts and behavior of the current readers and writer; it is not evidence that the database or its existing records must be replaced.
1455:### Field ownership and proposed mapping
1459:| Information | Proposed source for the clean path | Existing field and reader | What still needs to be decided or verified |
1461:| Source item, publisher, URL and publication date | Supplied with the article by the source database or later Hummingbird input. | The writer records `input_id`, category, publication day and location in `evidence_sources`; the source item database supplies document details. | Confirm the selected target has the source records and the dossier can resolve them. Publication day is not automatically the original statement day. |
1462:| Source passages and standalone statement | Luna returns `source_passages` and `summary`. Each evidence item has one or more separately checked passages from the same article. | Existing evidence uses one `quote` and `one_line`; the extraction checker and writer read them. | Verify each nonempty passage against the item; the production storage mapping for multiple passages is not yet designed. |
1464:| Evidence tier | Luna returns `tier`: `fact`, `party_statement`, `third_party_view` or `rumour`, following the signed standard's precedence. | The existing writer stores `tier`; Jev and judgement receive it, and revision checks use it. | Verify the mapping and how source-specific tier and attribution are retained when several reports link to one evidence record. Do not assume the current source table already retains all such distinctions. |
1465:| Described period and event time | Luna returns `evidence_period` as supported by the source, or null when unknown. | The existing writer expects `event_time`. | Verify whether and how these meanings map and which readers require them. A described period is not automatically publication day, original statement day or the old `event_time`. |
1472:### Acceptance of the Step 1 handoff
1474:Before any paid forward run, a no-cost check should exercise the **actual new path**, not just parse the example JSON. Use one example for each outcome: existing thesis with evidence, new thesis with evidence, both in one item, and no pairs. Verify that source passages and supplied IDs resolve, that each pair has evidence, that unknown values remain unknown, and that the test library's next item sees the preceding result. Check the captured request and output as the runner actually constructs and consumes them. A separate check on a copy of the intended ledger is required before claiming compatibility with its writer and dossier reader. The prior single-passage path has been exercised locally and in one selected three-article run. The revised multi-passage schema was sent to Luna in the exploratory run; its output has not been validated against the production writer.
1476:### Prompt review and next design task
1478:The draft prompt describes the new Step 1 decision, evidence pairing, source attribution and examples, but the **full request and its downstream path are not ready to run**. In particular, the precise thesis identity boundary and what the existing writer and judgement require for a new thesis need further work. The production prompt's opening rules and its later thesis matcher are not automatically part of the clean design. The old production configuration is frozen; the new draft is not a supported production prompt version.
1482:## 19. Claude review: verified findings and open choices
1484:This section records the verification of [Claude's review](CLAUDE-REVIEW-FINDINGS.md). The snapshot counts below are local observations, not a semantic audit or a claim about live production. Alex has decided the repeat behavior, minimum new-thesis fields, Step 1 evidence tier/date/period fields, entity-wide evidence search and review of the first run before deciding whether quality is sufficient; other product choices remain open.
1488:| 1. Repeat handling | The local snapshot has 2,023 `same_claims` rows for 4,298 evidence records, and the current writer saves a separate linked record. These are model-generated relation labels, not 2,023 independently confirmed duplicates. On 2026-11-06 Alex selected option B: reuse the evidence record and add the new source and missing thesis link. Sections 5, 6 and 13 record the decision and Jev handoff. |
1494:| 7. Evidence tier, date and period | Alex approved returning tier, original statement date and the period described. The draft separates a full ISO statement day from date text and evidence period; unknown values remain null. The current production `statement_day` converts a month or other non-ISO value to an empty day before writing, so Claude's example that `"May"` passes through this normal path as a literal comparison is not established. Writer fallbacks for revision ordering do not prove the original statement date. Mapping and source-specific provenance still need verification. |
1496:| 9. Pass line | Alex agreed to review the first forward run's traces and state before deciding what quality is sufficient to proceed. A review sheet should record missed material, noise, incorrect links, duplicate or unhelpful theses and attribution/date errors, with source passages; a sample of reviewed traces does not establish general accuracy. Formal acceptance remains governed by the frozen ruler where applicable. |
1497:| 10. Failed checks | The first-run plan originally stopped on schema, reference or source failures. Alex subsequently chose to keep unmatched source passages and unresolved references visible and provisional while continuing the isolated run; schema and other blocking failures retain their recorded behavior. The existing production path can drop individual evidence items; this isolated-run behavior is not a claim about production. Existing source-preserving quote normalization and repair are candidates for reuse only after their behavior is checked on the new path; an accepted repair and its source passage must be visible. |
1501:In the Claude-review revisions, the system-prompt draft grew from 7,579 to 10,469 characters. The added text supplied the signed standard's previously missing not-kept boundaries, their exceptions, the related-period distinction, the list-and-link handoff and the supplied-entity boundary. The subsequent clarification of Alex's new-thesis minimum increased that draft to 10,753 characters. The approved evidence tier and time fields brought the prior draft to 12,397 characters. The topic, evidence audit and multi-passage changes increased the system prompt from 12,397 to 13,419 characters and from 2,498 to 2,696 tokens using the local o200k_base tokenizer. The added wording states the agreed topic rule, the signed evidence boundaries and how to return multiple source passages; no model quality result is claimed. The earlier prompt bytes were not archived for a token-count comparison; no token-cost or model-quality improvement is claimed. The assembled-request review is recorded in the Step 1 audit. The provider accepted the exploratory multi-passage requests; output quality remains under review and has not been formally accepted.
1503:Additional checked boundaries: the current `service.evidence_record` and bundle evidence responses read legacy `replacements`, while `store.evidence_in_force` reads `same_claims` and `revisions`; this is a reader-contract gap, not proof about every UI. The snapshot has zero `replacements` rows. Jev's current evidence question uses the three criteria in section 13; its `cannot_tell` criterion belongs to thesis comparison. The current repository's `docs/check.py` invokes the clean-architecture renderer check; whether that coupling should remain is a maintenance choice, not an extraction-quality finding.

