reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/round-1.jsonl:29:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016A6erdjjU2Hp82pD2NiPXo","type":"tool_result","content":"129\t## 5. Step 2: find and compare related evidence\n130\t\n131\t### Why this is separate\n132\t\n133\tAn open thesis may accumulate many evidence records. Step 1 needs to understand the article and the open research; it does not need to read every prior statement to do that. Step 2 uses each proposed `{thesis, evidence}` association to find the history worth comparing.\n134\t\n135\t### Find candidate evidence\n136\t\n137\tThe proposed design places semantic search over the evidence library. It uses the thesis and incoming evidence to find top-N related evidence across the current entity, including evidence linked to other theses. The thesis is search context, not a restriction to evidence already linked to that thesis. This also applies when the proposed thesis is new. For example, “raised its revenue outlook” and “expects higher sales” can be related despite different wording. Alex chose this entity-wide scope on 2026-11-06.\n138\t\n139\tSearch returns **candidates**, not a final decision. Alex discussed a starting point of top 10; the implementation and quality requirements need to be measured.\n140\t\n141\tA match outside the returned set will not be considered by the comparison. Search quality therefore matters independently from the model's ability to compare the records it receives.\n142\t\n143\t### Compare with Jev\n144\t\n145\t**2026-11-08 (candidate, [round log](step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§24, 31–35):**\n146\t- Comparison reads the full passages; the 600-character cut is removed.\n147\t- An unlinked record also searches by its own text.\n148\t- `figures` never enter a search or comparison text.\n149\t- Jev is asked the clean path's own piece question, aligned with line 2's test: a repeat reports the stored piece's main statement, and\n150\t  an official's denial of a report is a revision.\n151\t- When a thesis opens, every stored unlinked group from earlier articles is judged against it in batches of ten (a yes/no question; the\n152\t  company's overall results inform only a revenue or earnings thesis). Each judged pair is recorded once.\n153\t- On c6's records: search 97.9%, wrong merges 1.9% (2 of 104), both lines met; line 3 not re-measured.\n154\t\n155\tJev compares the new evidence with the returned evidence. The intended outcomes are:\n156\t\n157\t| Outcome | Meaning | Example |\n158\t|---|---|---|\n159\t| Repeat | The new material presents the same statement again. | Another article reports the same company forecast. |\n160\t| Revision | A later account changes the same statement. | The company revises its forecast from 12% to 25%. |\n161\t| New evidence | It is not identified as a repeat or revision of the evidence compared. | A different analyst offers a separate forecast. |\n162\t\n163\tThe words, amount, speaker, source and time can matter to the comparison. A later statement about the same subject is not necessarily a revision. A matching amount alone does not prove that two statements are the same.\n164\t\n165\tThe result “new evidence” is the outcome of the comparison performed; it is not proof that no matching evidence exists elsewhere in the library. When the incoming evidence is judged a repeat of several candidates, it joins only the group Jev judges it most like: the highest same-statement probability, with ties going to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08). A comparison that fails is not saved and is reported.\n166\t\n167\t**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.\n168\t\n169\t**Step 2 acceptance (Alex, 2026-11-08).** Three lines apply:\n170\t1. For incoming evidence whose earlier version is in the library, search returns it at least 90% of the time.\n171\t2. At most 2% of merges are clearly wrong.\n172\t3. Grouped dossiers keep no fewer details added by later reports than no merging does.\n173\t\n174\tThe first measurement against these lines is in the [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12; the isolated modules are `step2/compare.py` and `step3/library.py`.\n175\t\n176\tThe diagram's intended sequence is: use `{thesis, incoming evidence}` to search the evidence library; pass the returned candidates and incoming evidence to Jev; pass the comparison result and the proposed association to Step 3. For a new thesis, search the same entity-wide evidence library. A concrete working draft of the Jev comparison request appears in [section 13](#13-step-2-prompt-jev-working-draft).\n177\t\n178\t## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links\n179\t\n180\tStep 3 takes the proposals from Step 1 and the comparison outcomes from Step 2 and writes the resulting research information.\n181\t\n182\t| Result | What is saved |\n183\t|---|---|\n184\t| New thesis with supporting evidence | The new thesis and its evidence link; save new evidence or reuse a matching record according to Step 2. |\n185\t| New evidence for an existing thesis | The evidence and its link to the thesis. |\n186\t| Repeat | Place the incoming record in the existing evidence record's group: add its source and any missing link to the relevant thesis, and keep the incoming record's own text and fields inside the group. Do not rewrite the existing record, and do not create a separate evidence record for the same statement. |\n187\t| Revision | The new evidence and its source, linked to the earlier statement as a revision. |\n188\t\n189\tA revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original (\"A 继续；跑council loop 开始做\"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). How a judgement reads a group is being tested. Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the chosen thesis, evidence and relationships are saved. How they are physically stored and written is a separate implementation decision.\n190\t\n191\t## 7. Step 4: judge the thesis\n192\t\n193\tJudgement is separate from processing and saving an article. It assesses what the evidence implies for an affected thesis and whether the thesis's current assessment should change.\n194\t\n195\tFor the capital-spending example, saving the revised forecast records **what the company said**. Judgement later considers **what that means for the investment question**. The saved evidence and its relationships must be available to that work.\n196\t\n197\tThis discussion has not yet defined the judgement's input selection, output, timing or decision method. Those are open design questions, not implicit requirements of the earlier steps.\n198\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903","uuid":"8a1fec46-3e95-4e83-a86c-211957073365","timestamp":"2026-11-09T00:55:05.515Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md","content":"## 5. Step 2: find and compare related evidence\n\n### Why this is separate\n\nAn open thesis may accumulate many evidence records. Step 1 needs to understand the article and the open research; it does not need to read every prior statement to do that. Step 2 uses each proposed `{thesis, evidence}` association to find the history worth comparing.\n\n### Find candidate evidence\n\nThe proposed design places semantic search over the evidence library. It uses the thesis and incoming evidence to find top-N related evidence across the current entity, including evidence linked to other theses. The thesis is search context, not a restriction to evidence already linked to that thesis. This also applies when the proposed thesis is new. For example, “raised its revenue outlook” and “expects higher sales” can be related despite different wording. Alex chose this entity-wide scope on 2026-11-06.\n\nSearch returns **candidates**, not a final decision. Alex discussed a starting point of top 10; the implementation and quality requirements need to be measured.\n\nA match outside the returned set will not be considered by the comparison. Search quality therefore matters independently from the model's ability to compare the records it receives.\n\n### Compare with Jev\n\n**2026-11-08 (candidate, [round log](step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§24, 31–35):**\n- Comparison reads the full passages; the 600-character cut is removed.\n- An unlinked record also searches by its own text.\n- `figures` never enter a search or comparison text.\n- Jev is asked the clean path's own piece question, aligned with line 2's test: a repeat reports the stored piece's main statement, and\n  an official's denial of a report is a revision.\n- When a thesis opens, every stored unlinked group from earlier articles is judged against it in batches of ten (a yes/no question; the\n  company's overall results inform only a revenue or earnings thesis). Each judged pair is recorded once.\n- On c6's records: search 97.9%, wrong merges 1.9% (2 of 104), both lines met; line 3 not re-measured.\n\nJev compares the new evidence with the returned evidence. The intended outcomes are:\n\n| Outcome | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Repeat | The new material presents the same statement again. | Another article reports the same company forecast. |\n| Revision | A later account changes the same statement. | The company revises its forecast from 12% to 25%. |\n| New evidence | It is not identified as a repeat or revision of the evidence compared. | A different analyst offers a separate forecast. |\n\nThe words, amount, speaker, source and time can matter to the comparison. A later statement about the same subject is not necessarily a revision. A matching amount alone does not prove that two statements are the same.\n\nThe result “new evidence” is the outcome of the comparison performed; it is not proof that no matching evidence exists elsewhere in the library. When the incoming evidence is judged a repeat of several candidates, it joins only the group Jev judges it most like: the highest same-statement probability, with ties going to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08). A comparison that fails is not saved and is reported.\n\n**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.\n\n**Step 2 acceptance (Alex, 2026-11-08).** Three lines apply:\n1. For incoming evidence whose earlier version is in the library, search returns it at least 90% of the time.\n2. At most 2% of merges are clearly wrong.\n3. Grouped dossiers keep no fewer details added by later reports than no merging does.\n\nThe first measurement against these lines is in the [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12; the isolated modules are `step2/compare.py` and `step3/library.py`.\n\nThe diagram's intended sequence is: use `{thesis, incoming evidence}` to search the evidence library; pass the returned candidates and incoming evidence to Jev; pass the comparison result and the proposed association to Step 3. For a new thesis, search the same entity-wide evidence library. A concrete working draft of the Jev comparison request appears in [section 13](#13-step-2-prompt-jev-working-draft).\n\n## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links\n\nStep 3 takes the proposals from Step 1 and the comparison outcomes from Step 2 and writes the resulting research information.\n\n| Result | What is saved |\n|---|---|\n| New thesis with supporting evidence | The new thesis and its evidence link; save new evidence or reuse a matching record according to Step 2. |\n| New evidence for an existing thesis | The evidence and its link to the thesis. |\n| Repeat | Place the incoming record in the existing evidence record's group: add its source and any missing link to the relevant thesis, and keep the incoming record's own text and fields inside the group. Do not rewrite the existing record, and do not create a separate evidence record for the same statement. |\n| Revision | The new evidence and its source, linked to the earlier statement as a revision. |\n\nA revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original (\"A 继续；跑council loop 开始做\"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). How a judgement reads a group is being tested. Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the chosen thesis, evidence and relationships are saved. How they are physically stored and written is a separate implementation decision.\n\n## 7. Step 4: judge the thesis\n\nJudgement is separate from processing and saving an article. It assesses what the evidence implies for an affected thesis and whether the thesis's current assessment should change.\n\nFor the capital-spending example, saving the revised forecast records **what the company said**. Judgement later considers **what that means for the investment question**. The saved evidence and its relationships must be available to that work.\n\nThis discussion has not yet defined the judgement's input selection, output, timing or decision method. Those are open design questions, not implicit requirements of the earlier steps.\n","numLines":70,"startLine":129,"totalLines":1504}}}
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/round-1.jsonl:33:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACHB28bLBzN6ntzeveMjVL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Step 2 validation: retrieval and comparison (council loop, from 2026-11-07)\n2\t\n3\tAlex, 2026-11-07: \"好开始；我觉得这几次的循环很有效的逼近问题我觉得应该把他写成一个skill 你思考下怎么construct 然后跑这个skill\". Run\n4\twith the `council-loop` skill (`.claude/skills/council-loop/SKILL.md`). This is a validation of Step 2, not an end-to-end claim:\n5\tthere is no Step 4.\n6\t\n7\t## 0. Frame\n8\t\n9\t- **Objective.** On stored Step 1 output, measure whether Step 2 finds the earlier statement a new evidence item repeats or revises,\n10\t  and whether the comparison labels the pair correctly. Then read what the deduplicated dossier of three investment questions looks\n11\t  like.\n12\t- **Alex's decided contract (PRD §§5–6, Codex thread [16]–[21], answers on 2026-11-06):**\n13\t  - a search engine over the evidence returns the top 10 for {thesis, evidence};\n14\t  - the search covers the entity's evidence across theses (choice B);\n15\t  - Jev labels each candidate repeat, revision or different;\n16\t  - a repeat adds its new source, and any missing thesis link, to the existing record;\n17\t  - a revision keeps both records, linked.\n18\t- **Proposed pass line (not confirmed by Alex):**\n19\t  - retrieval: at least 90% of true repeats and revisions have their earlier counterpart in the top 10;\n20\t  - comparison: at least 90% of found true pairs labelled correctly;\n21\t  - wrong merges of different parties' statements: at most 2% of comparisons.\n22\t- **Reference system.** Production's Step 2: word-overlap ranking within one thesis, top 10 (`views/matching.rank_evidence`), with\n23\t  the existing Jev piece question (`views/jev_matching.PIECES`).\n24\t- **Ruler.** The frozen golden-v5 does not measure Step 2 (AGENTS §7.1, \"what it does not measure\"). The pair ground truth built\n25\t  here is an exploratory reference by independent readers. It is not a frozen ruler until Alex signs it, and it is labelled as such\n26\t  in every result.\n27\t- **Data.** The Flash K6b draw-1 forward run over 115 gold articles (`poc/data/clean-step1-takeover-2026-11-07/gold-k6b-flash/`):\n28\t  282 kept items with summaries, passages, party, dates, tier and thesis links, in forward order.\n29\t- **Out of scope.** Step 3's writer, Step 4, the UI, frozen production files.\n30\t- **Budget.** Embeddings (`text-embedding-3-small`) and Jev ($0.042 per M input tokens). Calls and cost are counted before any send.\n31\t\n32\t## 1. Council A (independent design, then one challenge round)\n33\t\n34\tThe fresh Codex session is `01a1b870…`; its records are `council/a-handoff.md`, `a-codex.md`, `a-claude.md` and `b-*.md`. Agreed\n35\tdesign:\n36\t\n37\t- **Reference pairs (exploratory, model-authored, labelled as such).**\n38\t  - Population: ordered pairs (earlier item, later item) from different articles, with availability set by forward article order.\n39\t    Same-article pairs are excluded. An item linked to two theses is one item, queried once per {thesis, evidence} association.\n40\t  - Candidate pairs come from routes that do not use the embedding search under test:\n41\t    - shared figures;\n42\t    - shared parties and named entities;\n43\t    - summary-word Jaccard;\n44\t    - same-thesis lexical rank.\n45\t  - Readers do not see which route found a pair, nor any search or model output.\n46\t  - Labels:\n47\t    - repeat;\n48\t    - revision;\n49\t    - same quantity, different party (a subtype of different);\n50\t    - different;\n51\t    - insufficient information;\n52\t    - and, for repeats, whether the later item is richer or qualified (D204's risk).\n53\t\n54\t    Two blind readers label each pair with the same full source context; disagreements are kept, then adjudicated with the source.\n55\t- **Generator-miss control.**\n56\t  - Sample: 30 incoming items, drawn uniformly within three time strata (articles 1–40, 41–80, 81–115) with a fixed seed. Inclusion\n57\t    probabilities are recorded and the estimate weighted.\n58\t  - Scan: the reader reads the incoming item's full passages, then every earlier summary.\n59\t  - Audit: a random sample of unflagged pairs is re-checked with full context.\n60\t  - Expected to be inconclusive if positives are few; expand rather than claim.\n61\t- **Retrieval arms.**\n62\t  - R0: production as written, word overlap within the linked thesis, top 10; a new thesis has no candidates.\n63\t  - R0e: entity-wide word overlap.\n64\t  - R1: embedding of summary, verified passages, party, time and period; query of thesis description, outcome and period plus the\n65\t    incoming summary and passages; entity-wide; top 10.\n66\t  - R2: R1 without the thesis in the query.\n67\t  - Strict forward availability throughout.\n68\t- **Comparison.** Jev with the cross-thesis wording and full fields, tested first on the labelled pairs, apart from retrieval, then on\n69\t  the retrieved candidates. An uncertainty option is open; an uncertain pair is never silently called different.\n70\t- **Metrics.**\n71\t  - recall at 10 by type and by cross-thesis status;\n72\t  - the Jev confusion matrix;\n73\t  - false repeats by cause, over true-different and over predicted repeats;\n74\t  - the combined path (right target and right label);\n75\t  - the dossier per thesis before and after dedup, labelled a simulation.\n76\t\n77\t  The 90% figures are discussion targets until Alex agrees.\n78\t- **Open for Alex:** what is saved when one item is a repeat of one candidate and a revision of another, or a repeat of several.\n79\t\n80\t**Dossiers fixed before retrieval** (Flash d1 thesis ids):\n81\t1. Customer financing and backstops: clean-1, the revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n82\t2. Competition: clean-18 (custom silicon and AMD) and clean-24 (inference-chip challengers).\n83\t3. Product roadmap: clean-11 (Vera Rubin volume shipping) and clean-17 (Kyber).\n84\t\n85\t## 2. Reference (2026-11-07/08)\n86\t\n87\t- **v1** (`reference.json`, kept as history): 573 pairs, from 488 triage-possible pairs, 66 scan-flagged pairs and a 60-pair audit.\n88\t  - Two blind readers agreed on 94.9%; a third settled 29 disputes.\n89\t  - Readers marked 313 of 397 \"repeats\" as richer. A hand-check of 6 found 2 that were not repeats.\n90\t- **v2** (`reference-v2.json`, council round 1): two fresh readers answered two separate questions, the relation and the material the\n91\t  later item adds, over 497 pairs (all v1 positives, 60 sampled negatives, 6 insufficient).\n92\t  - Relation agreement 93.4%; 24 of the 33 disputes were between same statement and different fact of the same matter.\n93\t  - Result: same_statement 333, different_fact_same_matter 105, revision 25, same quantity other party 8, different 24, insufficient 2.\n94\t  - All 60 v1 \"repeats\" were relabelled as different facts.\n95\t  - My hand-read of 18 same_statement pairs: 2 clearly wrong (NVL144 against NVL576), 2 arguable.\n96\t- **v3 additions** (`v3-answer-E/F-*`): the 48 never-labelled pairs Jev called repeats on the product path or in the forward\n97\t  simulation, two readers.\n98\t  - 24 same statement, 17 different fact, 1 other party, 6 disputed.\n99\t  - So the reference had missed about half of those pairs, and Jev was wrong on the other half.\n100\t\n101\t## 3. Search: recall at 10 (`score_retrieval_v2.py`, forward simulation)\n102\t\n103\t| Query | Per association, raw history (391) | Deduplicated history | Forward simulation, real Jev decisions |\n104\t|---|---|---|---|\n105\t| R0, production, within thesis | 82.1% | | |\n106\t| R0e, word overlap, entity-wide | 78.5% | | |\n107\t| R1, thesis + evidence (agreed) | 90.0% | 125/125 | 112/113 |\n108\t| R2, evidence only | 95.9% | 113/113 | |\n109\t\n110\t- Revisions: R1 found 22 of 25.\n111\t- Cross-thesis positives: only 3, so no conclusion can be drawn on cross-thesis search.\n112\t- The raw-history misses come from many copies of the same story crowding the top 10; merging removes that crowding.\n113\t- 112/113 counts only known matches, from one Jev draw.\n114\t\n115\t## 4. Comparison (Jev)\n116\t\n117\t**Pair level on the labelled pairs** (`score_jev.py`):\n118\t\n119\t| Arm | Agreement | Wrong merges |\n120\t|---|---|---|\n121\t| J0, production question | 82.4% | 30 (9.4%; 14 from one bundle record) |\n122\t| J1, cross-thesis wording | 80.8% | 22 (7.5%) |\n123\t\n124\tPair level is not the verdict. A record is saved or discarded whole, and one true match is enough.\n125\t\n126\t**Product path, per incoming record** (`score_product_path.py`; J0 over the R1 top 10):\n127\t\n128\t| Arm | Merged into a true same statement | No true match | Duplicates kept |\n129\t|---|---|---|---|\n130\t| J0 | 106 | 6 | 4 |\n131\t| J1 | 97 | 2 | 13 |\n132\t\n133\t**Forward simulation** (`forward_sim.py`, J0, article order, the library each decision would see): 282 items become 180 records.\n134\t- 102 merged.\n135\t- Both loss readers say 93 of those have a true same-statement home. 9 do not: by my read, 3 clear (Sharon AI into Firmus; analysts' 82%\n136\t  and 41% into the analysts' revenue-doubling record; NVL576 into NVL144) and 6 arguable.\n137\t- 10 cite the incoming source on an extra record that the incoming record does not state.\n138\t- 14 true repeats in the results were left unmerged.\n139\t\n140\t## 5. What merging loses (`loss-answer-G/H-*`, all 102 merged items, two readers)\n141\t\n142\t- Both readers say a material fact is lost that no other record holds in 66 of 102 (agreement 93/102). The readers over-flag.\n143\t- My hand-read of a random 12 of the 66:\n144\t  - 3 material: Q1 net income, EPS and margin; Q2 guidance \"±2%, assumes no China data-centre revenue\"; the Taiwan raid on Supermicro\n145\t    bundled into a Singapore record;\n146\t  - 3 moderate;\n147\t  - 6 trivial.\n148\t- A random 4 of the no-loss items: 4 had no loss.\n149\t- This is a small diagnostic sample, not a population estimate.\n150\t- **Mechanism:**\n151\t  - Step 1 records carry one matter, often several facts.\n152\t  - Later articles retell a statement and add figures or qualifications.\n153\t  - The current rule keeps only the earlier text and adds the source, so those additions are discarded.\n154\t\n155\t**Probe J2** (`jev-J2-merges.json`, $0.004): same_statement only when the incoming record adds nothing an analyst would keep.\n156\t- Merges fell from 102 to 22 on the same 102 merges. This is not a new forward run.\n157\t- All 3 material and all 3 moderate losses were kept, but so were 5 of 6 trivial and 3 of 4 no-loss items.\n158\t- Jev cannot separate trivial from material additions any better than the readers can, so a materiality gate either loses facts or\n159\t  undoes most of the merging.\n160\t\n161\t## 6. Council B (rounds 1–3: `council/c-*`, `d-*`, `e-*`)\n162\t\n163\t- Round 1: re-label with two separate questions, and score per association.\n164\t- Round 2: do not claim that search passes or that detection is close. Run a forward simulation and read every merged record against the\n165\t  dossier.\n166\t- Round 3: grouping preserves text, but has not been shown to be understood by the judge.\n167\t  - Say \"same statement\", not \"same story\".\n168\t  - J2's 22 is not a forward run.\n169\t  - Group membership with several matches still needs a rule.\n170\t\n171\t## 7. Verdict and the question for Alex (2026-11-08)\n172\t\n173\tThe hypothesis \"top-10 search plus Jev repeat/revision/different, then source-only merging\" is contradicted on the merge action, so the\n174\tline stops (D355).\n175\t\n176\t- Search is not the bottleneck.\n177\t- Detection is near but not at the proposed line: 3 to 9 wrong merges in 102, and 14 duplicates in 113.\n178\t- Source-only merging discards facts in a noticeable share of merges.\n179\t\n180\tQuestion for Alex: when a later article repeats a stored statement with more detail, what does the library do?\n181\t- **(A) Recommended:** keep the later record's text and source inside the stored record's group. The original is not rewritten, and the\n182\t  judge reads the group as one statement.\n183\t- **(B)** Merge only exact retellings.\n184\t- **(C)** Keep source-only merging.\n185\t\n186\tThe proposed pass lines and the conflict rule for several matches are still not confirmed.\n187\t\n188\t**Next test if (A):** build the groups on the forward history, show the judge's input for the three predeclared dossiers before and\n189\tafter, and check that grouped details are visible and that nothing is counted twice. The Kyber group of 14 retellings is the size case.\n190\tThen take the next class: wrong groups and missed repeats.\n191\t\n192\t**Cost:** Jev about $0.19 in all (labelled $0.021, product path $0.116, forward $0.054, J2 $0.004, at the listed rate). Embeddings\n193\tearlier. Readers were Codex read-only sessions.\n194\t\n195\t## 8. Log rows\n196\t\n197\t| Arm | Hypothesis | Change | Result | Kept |\n198\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n199\t| R1 against R0 | An entity-wide embedding finds cross-thesis repeats | Embedding query with thesis + evidence | 90.0% against 82.1% per association; 112/113 in the forward run | yes (agreed design) |\n200\t| J1 against J0 | Removing the \"same thesis\" premise reduces wrong merges | Cross-thesis wording | Fewer wrong merges (2 against 6 per record), more duplicates (13 against 4) | open |\n201\t| J2 | A stricter repeat removes the loss | same_statement requires nothing new | Loss removed; merges 102 → 22; trivial retellings kept | no: refuted as a gate; it motivates grouping |\n202\t\n203\t## 9. Alex's decision (2026-11-08)\n204\t\n205\tOn the question in §7, Alex replied: \"A 继续；跑council loop 开始做\". **Decided:** each repeat's own text and source are kept inside the\n206\tstored record's group, and the original is not rewritten. Recorded in the clean PRD, §5 repeat handoff and §6. Still not confirmed:\n207\tthe Step 2 pass lines, and the rule for several matches.\n208\t\n209\t## 10. Grouping round (2026-11-08, council loop)\n210\t\n211\t**Frame.** Alex chose A (§9).\n212\t- Goal: build grouping and test whether readers notice what later reports add, without double counting or being misled, compared with\n213\t  source-only merging and with no merging (the full-information control).\n214\t- No model judge exists (PRD §7), so blind readers act as the dossier's readers.\n215\t\n216\t**Council A** (`council/group/a-*`, `b-*`).\n217\t- Agreed:\n218\t  - the group contract: the original is never rewritten; members keep their own id, text, fields and source; links carry their\n219\t    provenance; revisions are links; a replay is idempotent; one unit per item; distinct articles are counted;\n220\t  - the dossier view;\n221\t  - three views built from one stored history;\n222\t  - a key written by independent readers before any view is read;\n223\t  - one view per reader.\n224\t- Settled in the challenge:\n225\t  - several matches (15 items): kept as their own entry with `also_matches` references in both merged views. This is a **test\n226\t    convention only**; the product rule stays Alex's.\n227\t  - What was built: the isolated library with only the decided rows, refusing a failed comparison and several targets.\n228\t\n229\t**Built.**\n230\t- `clean-arch/step3/library.py`: groups, members, links, revisions, refusals and the dossier text.\n231\t- `test_library.py`: 12 tests, one per table row. All 5 mutants were caught (the original rewritten, a replay appended, articles not\n232\t  distinct, a failed comparison saved, several targets joined).\n233\t- `build_views.py` replays the forward simulation's 282 stored decisions through the library: 195 groups, 0 refused. This is a\n234\t  fixed-decision reconstruction, not a new forward run.\n235\t\n236\t**Reader test** (`reader-test/`).\n237\t- Questions and scoring rules were frozen first (`FROZEN.sha256`, checked by the scorer): 39 questions over three dossiers.\n238\t- Key: two readers; a third settled the 16 questions where the drafts differed.\n239\t- 18 masked view readers; 3 blind graders. Tally: `score_reader_test.py`, 234 grades.\n240\t\n241\t| Dossier | Detail: none / source / group (out of 12) | Unsupported claims: none / source / group | Characters: none / source / group |\n242\t|---|---|---|---|\n243\t| financing | 7.0 / 1.0 / 6.5 | 0 / 4 / 0 | 43.7k / 23.0k / 42.5k |\n244\t| competition | 7.5 / 2.0 / 8.0 | 2 / 2 / 2 | 18.1k / 15.2k / 22.5k |\n245\t| roadmap | 10.5 / 0.0 / 10.5 | 2 / 0 / 0 | 62.9k / 40.2k / 65.9k |\n246\t\n247\t- Detail in total: none 25, source 3, group 25, out of 36.\n248\t- Double counting: 0 in every view, on 4 independence questions.\n249\t- Controls, comparison and independence scores are about equal; group is slightly ahead on comparison (9 against 8 against 6).\n250\t- No frozen falsifier fired. Detail: group trails none in financing by 0.25 question per reader, under the 1-question threshold. The\n251\t  wrong-group questions scored the same in every view.\n252\t\n253\t**Traces read.**\n254\t- C3: group readers saw both Lisa Su accounts, but read the ambiguous sentence as covering MI450 only. That is a judgement point, not a\n255\t  missed detail.\n256\t- R5: group readers put \"orders\" and \"sales\" side by side, while none readers added an inferred year.\n257\t- F1 is partial everywhere because grading was strict.\n258\t\n259\t**Council B** (`council/group/c-*`).\n260\t- The test supports preserving later text. It does not prove equivalence with no merging, overall dossier quality (the questions were\n261\t  chosen for added details), or wrong-group safety (the questions pointed readers at the facts).\n262\t- Size is about no merging, up to 25% more in competition: groups bring members whose own links point elsewhere. Grouping gives\n263\t  structure, not compression.\n264\t- Next class: wrong groups.\n265\t\n266\t**Wrong-group census ($0; the 9 no-home merges, read against exactly what Jev saw):**\n267\t- M2, Sharon AI into Firmus: the incoming record's quote is the whole paragraph, Firmus sentence included, so Jev matched on the quote.\n268\t  Step 1 bundle on the incoming side.\n269\t- M57, NVL576 into NVL144: the stored record's own summary also states the NVL576 claim, so the merge is defensible. Step 1 bundle on\n270\t  the stored side.\n271\t- M30, analysts' 82% and 41% into the analysts' revenue path: different figures. A Jev error.\n272\t- 6 arguable (the same announcement, the same figure, or a relayed report): under grouping both texts sit side by side.\n273\t- So 2 real wrong groups out of 102, and their texts survive.\n274\t\n275\t**Open for Alex:**\n276\t- the several-match rule (pending, best match, or own entry with references);\n277\t- the Step 2 pass lines.\n278\t\n279\t## 11. Log rows\n280\t\n281\t| Arm | Hypothesis | Change | Result | Kept |\n282\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n283\t| Grouping (A) | Keeping each repeat's text keeps added details noticeable without double counting | Isolated library and group view | Detail 25/36, the same as no merging, against 3/36 source-only; 0 double counting; size about the same as no merging | yes (Alex's decision; tested) |\n284\t\n285\t## 12. Close-out (2026-11-08)\n286\t\n287\t**Alex's decisions (2026-11-08):**\n288\t- a record that is a repeat of several groups joins only the one Jev judges it most like (the highest same_statement probability; ties\n289\t  go to the earliest group);\n290\t- the Step 2 lines: (1) search at least 90%; (2) clear wrong merges at most 2%; (3) grouped dossiers keep no fewer later details than\n291\t  no merging.\n292\t\n293\tBoth are recorded in PRD §5.\n294\t\n295\t**Built.**\n296\t- `clean-arch/step2/compare.py`: search, Jev comparison, the outcome with best match, forward order, and a failed comparison refused and\n297\t  reported. `test_compare.py`: 6 tests; all 4 mutants caught.\n298\t- `run_forward.py` reads a stored Step 1 trace directly.\n299\t- **Run** (`poc/data/clean-step2-forward-2026-11-08/`, a fresh Jev draw): 282 records made 178 groups.\n300\t  - 104 repeats, 3 revisions, 0 refused; 16 records had several same_statement answers.\n301\t  - The cached embedding texts were verified identical: 282 of 282 documents, 313 of 313 queries.\n302\t\n303\t**Council A** (`council/group/d-*`):\n304\t- report search as recall over known labelled counterparts;\n305\t- blind-review every merge, previously labelled positives included;\n306\t- verify the cache texts;\n307\t- re-run the reader test on the new views.\n308\t\n309\t| Line | Result | Met |\n310\t|---|---|---|\n311\t| 1. Search | 112/113 known labelled counterparts had a holding group among the candidates (99.1%). For 107 of them the group's original, which is what Jev saw, is itself a counterpart. This is not recall over all real repeats. | yes |\n312\t| 2. Clear wrong merges | 104 merges read by two blind readers; 85 accepted by both; the 19 rejected by either went to a third reader: acceptable 1, overlap 15, **wrong 3** = 2.9% | **no** (missed by one merge) |\n313\t| 3. Later details | Reader test re-run on the rebuilt views with the frozen questions and key: detail none 27.5, source 3, group **28** of 36; unsupported claims 3 / 2 / 2; double counting 0 | yes |\n314\t\n315\t**Line 2 detail.**\n316\t- The 3 wrong merges:\n317\t  - NVL576 into the NVL144 group, twice. The group's original Step 1 record states both claims.\n318\t  - Analysts' 82% and 41% growth into the analysts' revenue-path record: different figures, a Jev error.\n319\t- 15 overlaps, which the third reader called imperfect but not misleading to a reader of the record's own text. Among them:\n320\t  - orders against sales ($1tn);\n321\t  - expected revenue against committed off-take (Firmus);\n322\t  - bundled records: the business model into the Sharon group, the Kyber delay report into the backup-rack group, the Taiwan raid\n323\t    alongside the Singapore mansion.\n324\t- My own reading had 5 wrong (Sharon AI into Firmus, and the business-model bundle into the Sharon group, both overlap to the third\n325\t  reader). Both readings miss the line.\n326\t\n327\t**Council B** (`council/group/e-*`):\n328\t- do not offer the result as a pass;\n329\t- report 3 wrong, 15 overlap and 1 acceptable after dispute;\n330\t- a later look at bundled records would explain only part of it;\n331\t- stop and report.\n332\t\n333\t**Verdict.** Step 2 meets lines 1 and 3 and misses line 2 by one merge, in one Jev draw. Per Alex's instruction, the work stops here,\n334\tand the census goes to him.\n335\t\n336\t**Not done:** the unprompted wrong-group reading test, and a fix for line 2.\n337\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903","uuid":"f6ec76a6-9ebc-460a-9529-2d7d6cd01fe3","timestamp":"2026-11-09T00:55:09.966Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md","content":"# Step 2 validation: retrieval and comparison (council loop, from 2026-11-07)\n\nAlex, 2026-11-07: \"好开始；我觉得这几次的循环很有效的逼近问题我觉得应该把他写成一个skill 你思考下怎么construct 然后跑这个skill\". Run\nwith the `council-loop` skill (`.claude/skills/council-loop/SKILL.md`). This is a validation of Step 2, not an end-to-end claim:\nthere is no Step 4.\n\n## 0. Frame\n\n- **Objective.** On stored Step 1 output, measure whether Step 2 finds the earlier statement a new evidence item repeats or revises,\n  and whether the comparison labels the pair correctly. Then read what the deduplicated dossier of three investment questions looks\n  like.\n- **Alex's decided contract (PRD §§5–6, Codex thread [16]–[21], answers on 2026-11-06):**\n  - a search engine over the evidence returns the top 10 for {thesis, evidence};\n  - the search covers the entity's evidence across theses (choice B);\n  - Jev labels each candidate repeat, revision or different;\n  - a repeat adds its new source, and any missing thesis link, to the existing record;\n  - a revision keeps both records, linked.\n- **Proposed pass line (not confirmed by Alex):**\n  - retrieval: at least 90% of true repeats and revisions have their earlier counterpart in the top 10;\n  - comparison: at least 90% of found true pairs labelled correctly;\n  - wrong merges of different parties' statements: at most 2% of comparisons.\n- **Reference system.** Production's Step 2: word-overlap ranking within one thesis, top 10 (`views/matching.rank_evidence`), with\n  the existing Jev piece question (`views/jev_matching.PIECES`).\n- **Ruler.** The frozen golden-v5 does not measure Step 2 (AGENTS §7.1, \"what it does not measure\"). The pair ground truth built\n  here is an exploratory reference by independent readers. It is not a frozen ruler until Alex signs it, and it is labelled as such\n  in every result.\n- **Data.** The Flash K6b draw-1 forward run over 115 gold articles (`poc/data/clean-step1-takeover-2026-11-07/gold-k6b-flash/`):\n  282 kept items with summaries, passages, party, dates, tier and thesis links, in forward order.\n- **Out of scope.** Step 3's writer, Step 4, the UI, frozen production files.\n- **Budget.** Embeddings (`text-embedding-3-small`) and Jev ($0.042 per M input tokens). Calls and cost are counted before any send.\n\n## 1. Council A (independent design, then one challenge round)\n\nThe fresh Codex session is `01a1b870…`; its records are `council/a-handoff.md`, `a-codex.md`, `a-claude.md` and `b-*.md`. Agreed\ndesign:\n\n- **Reference pairs (exploratory, model-authored, labelled as such).**\n  - Population: ordered pairs (earlier item, later item) from different articles, with availability set by forward article order.\n    Same-article pairs are excluded. An item linked to two theses is one item, queried once per {thesis, evidence} association.\n  - Candidate pairs come from routes that do not use the embedding search under test:\n    - shared figures;\n    - shared parties and named entities;\n    - summary-word Jaccard;\n    - same-thesis lexical rank.\n  - Readers do not see which route found a pair, nor any search or model output.\n  - Labels:\n    - repeat;\n    - revision;\n    - same quantity, different party (a subtype of different);\n    - different;\n    - insufficient information;\n    - and, for repeats, whether the later item is richer or qualified (D204's risk).\n\n    Two blind readers label each pair with the same full source context; disagreements are kept, then adjudicated with the source.\n- **Generator-miss control.**\n  - Sample: 30 incoming items, drawn uniformly within three time strata (articles 1–40, 41–80, 81–115) with a fixed seed. Inclusion\n    probabilities are recorded and the estimate weighted.\n  - Scan: the reader reads the incoming item's full passages, then every earlier summary.\n  - Audit: a random sample of unflagged pairs is re-checked with full context.\n  - Expected to be inconclusive if positives are few; expand rather than claim.\n- **Retrieval arms.**\n  - R0: production as written, word overlap within the linked thesis, top 10; a new thesis has no candidates.\n  - R0e: entity-wide word overlap.\n  - R1: embedding of summary, verified passages, party, time and period; query of thesis description, outcome and period plus the\n    incoming summary and passages; entity-wide; top 10.\n  - R2: R1 without the thesis in the query.\n  - Strict forward availability throughout.\n- **Comparison.** Jev with the cross-thesis wording and full fields, tested first on the labelled pairs, apart from retrieval, then on\n  the retrieved candidates. An uncertainty option is open; an uncertain pair is never silently called different.\n- **Metrics.**\n  - recall at 10 by type and by cross-thesis status;\n  - the Jev confusion matrix;\n  - false repeats by cause, over true-different and over predicted repeats;\n  - the combined path (right target and right label);\n  - the dossier per thesis before and after dedup, labelled a simulation.\n\n  The 90% figures are discussion targets until Alex agrees.\n- **Open for Alex:** what is saved when one item is a repeat of one candidate and a revision of another, or a repeat of several.\n\n**Dossiers fixed before retrieval** (Flash d1 thesis ids):\n1. Customer financing and backstops: clean-1, the revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n2. Competition: clean-18 (custom silicon and AMD) and clean-24 (inference-chip challengers).\n3. Product roadmap: clean-11 (Vera Rubin volume shipping) and clean-17 (Kyber).\n\n## 2. Reference (2026-11-07/08)\n\n- **v1** (`reference.json`, kept as history): 573 pairs, from 488 triage-possible pairs, 66 scan-flagged pairs and a 60-pair audit.\n  - Two blind readers agreed on 94.9%; a third settled 29 disputes.\n  - Readers marked 313 of 397 \"repeats\" as richer. A hand-check of 6 found 2 that were not repeats.\n- **v2** (`reference-v2.json`, council round 1): two fresh readers answered two separate questions, the relation and the material the\n  later item adds, over 497 pairs (all v1 positives, 60 sampled negatives, 6 insufficient).\n  - Relation agreement 93.4%; 24 of the 33 disputes were between same statement and different fact of the same matter.\n  - Result: same_statement 333, different_fact_same_matter 105, revision 25, same quantity other party 8, different 24, insufficient 2.\n  - All 60 v1 \"repeats\" were relabelled as different facts.\n  - My hand-read of 18 same_statement pairs: 2 clearly wrong (NVL144 against NVL576), 2 arguable.\n- **v3 additions** (`v3-answer-E/F-*`): the 48 never-labelled pairs Jev called repeats on the product path or in the forward\n  simulation, two readers.\n  - 24 same statement, 17 different fact, 1 other party, 6 disputed.\n  - So the reference had missed about half of those pairs, and Jev was wrong on the other half.\n\n## 3. Search: recall at 10 (`score_retrieval_v2.py`, forward simulation)\n\n| Query | Per association, raw history (391) | Deduplicated history | Forward simulation, real Jev decisions |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R0, production, within thesis | 82.1% | | |\n| R0e, word overlap, entity-wide | 78.5% | | |\n| R1, thesis + evidence (agreed) | 90.0% | 125/125 | 112/113 |\n| R2, evidence only | 95.9% | 113/113 | |\n\n- Revisions: R1 found 22 of 25.\n- Cross-thesis positives: only 3, so no conclusion can be drawn on cross-thesis search.\n- The raw-history misses come from many copies of the same story crowding the top 10; merging removes that crowding.\n- 112/113 counts only known matches, from one Jev draw.\n\n## 4. Comparison (Jev)\n\n**Pair level on the labelled pairs** (`score_jev.py`):\n\n| Arm | Agreement | Wrong merges |\n|---|---|---|\n| J0, production question | 82.4% | 30 (9.4%; 14 from one bundle record) |\n| J1, cross-thesis wording | 80.8% | 22 (7.5%) |\n\nPair level is not the verdict. A record is saved or discarded whole, and one true match is enough.\n\n**Product path, per incoming record** (`score_product_path.py`; J0 over the R1 top 10):\n\n| Arm | Merged into a true same statement | No true match | Duplicates kept |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| J0 | 106 | 6 | 4 |\n| J1 | 97 | 2 | 13 |\n\n**Forward simulation** (`forward_sim.py`, J0, article order, the library each decision would see): 282 items become 180 records.\n- 102 merged.\n- Both loss readers say 93 of those have a true same-statement home. 9 do not: by my read, 3 clear (Sharon AI into Firmus; analysts' 82%\n  and 41% into the analysts' revenue-doubling record; NVL576 into NVL144) and 6 arguable.\n- 10 cite the incoming source on an extra record that the incoming record does not state.\n- 14 true repeats in the results were left unmerged.\n\n## 5. What merging loses (`loss-answer-G/H-*`, all 102 merged items, two readers)\n\n- Both readers say a material fact is lost that no other record holds in 66 of 102 (agreement 93/102). The readers over-flag.\n- My hand-read of a random 12 of the 66:\n  - 3 material: Q1 net income, EPS and margin; Q2 guidance \"±2%, assumes no China data-centre revenue\"; the Taiwan raid on Supermicro\n    bundled into a Singapore record;\n  - 3 moderate;\n  - 6 trivial.\n- A random 4 of the no-loss items: 4 had no loss.\n- This is a small diagnostic sample, not a population estimate.\n- **Mechanism:**\n  - Step 1 records carry one matter, often several facts.\n  - Later articles retell a statement and add figures or qualifications.\n  - The current rule keeps only the earlier text and adds the source, so those additions are discarded.\n\n**Probe J2** (`jev-J2-merges.json`, $0.004): same_statement only when the incoming record adds nothing an analyst would keep.\n- Merges fell from 102 to 22 on the same 102 merges. This is not a new forward run.\n- All 3 material and all 3 moderate losses were kept, but so were 5 of 6 trivial and 3 of 4 no-loss items.\n- Jev cannot separate trivial from material additions any better than the readers can, so a materiality gate either loses facts or\n  undoes most of the merging.\n\n## 6. Council B (rounds 1–3: `council/c-*`, `d-*`, `e-*`)\n\n- Round 1: re-label with two separate questions, and score per association.\n- Round 2: do not claim that search passes or that detection is close. Run a forward simulation and read every merged record against the\n  dossier.\n- Round 3: grouping preserves text, but has not been shown to be understood by the judge.\n  - Say \"same statement\", not \"same story\".\n  - J2's 22 is not a forward run.\n  - Group membership with several matches still needs a rule.\n\n## 7. Verdict and the question for Alex (2026-11-08)\n\nThe hypothesis \"top-10 search plus Jev repeat/revision/different, then source-only merging\" is contradicted on the merge action, so the\nline stops (D355).\n\n- Search is not the bottleneck.\n- Detection is near but not at the proposed line: 3 to 9 wrong merges in 102, and 14 duplicates in 113.\n- Source-only merging discards facts in a noticeable share of merges.\n\nQuestion for Alex: when a later article repeats a stored statement with more detail, what does the library do?\n- **(A) Recommended:** keep the later record's text and source inside the stored record's group. The original is not rewritten, and the\n  judge reads the group as one statement.\n- **(B)** Merge only exact retellings.\n- **(C)** Keep source-only merging.\n\nThe proposed pass lines and the conflict rule for several matches are still not confirmed.\n\n**Next test if (A):** build the groups on the forward history, show the judge's input for the three predeclared dossiers before and\nafter, and check that grouped details are visible and that nothing is counted twice. The Kyber group of 14 retellings is the size case.\nThen take the next class: wrong groups and missed repeats.\n\n**Cost:** Jev about $0.19 in all (labelled $0.021, product path $0.116, forward $0.054, J2 $0.004, at the listed rate). Embeddings\nearlier. Readers were Codex read-only sessions.\n\n## 8. Log rows\n\n| Arm | Hypothesis | Change | Result | Kept |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 against R0 | An entity-wide embedding finds cross-thesis repeats | Embedding query with thesis + evidence | 90.0% against 82.1% per association; 112/113 in the forward run | yes (agreed design) |\n| J1 against J0 | Removing the \"same thesis\" premise reduces wrong merges | Cross-thesis wording | Fewer wrong merges (2 against 6 per record), more duplicates (13 against 4) | open |\n| J2 | A stricter repeat removes the loss | same_statement requires nothing new | Loss removed; merges 102 → 22; trivial retellings kept | no: refuted as a gate; it motivates grouping |\n\n## 9. Alex's decision (2026-11-08)\n\nOn the question in §7, Alex replied: \"A 继续；跑council loop 开始做\". **Decided:** each repeat's own text and source are kept inside the\nstored record's group, and the original is not rewritten. Recorded in the clean PRD, §5 repeat handoff and §6. Still not confirmed:\nthe Step 2 pass lines, and the rule for several matches.\n\n## 10. Grouping round (2026-11-08, council loop)\n\n**Frame.** Alex chose A (§9).\n- Goal: build grouping and test whether readers notice what later reports add, without double counting or being misled, compared with\n  source-only merging and with no merging (the full-information control).\n- No model judge exists (PRD §7), so blind readers act as the dossier's readers.\n\n**Council A** (`council/group/a-*`, `b-*`).\n- Agreed:\n  - the group contract: the original is never rewritten; members keep their own id, text, fields and source; links carry their\n    provenance; revisions are links; a replay is idempotent; one unit per item; distinct articles are counted;\n  - the dossier view;\n  - three views built from one stored history;\n  - a key written by independent readers before any view is read;\n  - one view per reader.\n- Settled in the challenge:\n  - several matches (15 items): kept as their own entry with `also_matches` references in both merged views. This is a **test\n    convention only**; the product rule stays Alex's.\n  - What was built: the isolated library with only the decided rows, refusing a failed comparison and several targets.\n\n**Built.**\n- `clean-arch/step3/library.py`: groups, members, links, revisions, refusals and the dossier text.\n- `test_library.py`: 12 tests, one per table row. All 5 mutants were caught (the original rewritten, a replay appended, articles not\n  distinct, a failed comparison saved, several targets joined).\n- `build_views.py` replays the forward simulation's 282 stored decisions through the library: 195 groups, 0 refused. This is a\n  fixed-decision reconstruction, not a new forward run.\n\n**Reader test** (`reader-test/`).\n- Questions and scoring rules were frozen first (`FROZEN.sha256`, checked by the scorer): 39 questions over three dossiers.\n- Key: two readers; a third settled the 16 questions where the drafts differed.\n- 18 masked view readers; 3 blind graders. Tally: `score_reader_test.py`, 234 grades.\n\n| Dossier | Detail: none / source / group (out of 12) | Unsupported claims: none / source / group | Characters: none / source / group |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| financing | 7.0 / 1.0 / 6.5 | 0 / 4 / 0 | 43.7k / 23.0k / 42.5k |\n| competition | 7.5 / 2.0 / 8.0 | 2 / 2 / 2 | 18.1k / 15.2k / 22.5k |\n| roadmap | 10.5 / 0.0 / 10.5 | 2 / 0 / 0 | 62.9k / 40.2k / 65.9k |\n\n- Detail in total: none 25, source 3, group 25, out of 36.\n- Double counting: 0 in every view, on 4 independence questions.\n- Controls, comparison and independence scores are about equal; group is slightly ahead on comparison (9 against 8 against 6).\n- No frozen falsifier fired. Detail: group trails none in financing by 0.25 question per reader, under the 1-question threshold. The\n  wrong-group questions scored the same in every view.\n\n**Traces read.**\n- C3: group readers saw both Lisa Su accounts, but read the ambiguous sentence as covering MI450 only. That is a judgement point, not a\n  missed detail.\n- R5: group readers put \"orders\" and \"sales\" side by side, while none readers added an inferred year.\n- F1 is partial everywhere because grading was strict.\n\n**Council B** (`council/group/c-*`).\n- The test supports preserving later text. It does not prove equivalence with no merging, overall dossier quality (the questions were\n  chosen for added details), or wrong-group safety (the questions pointed readers at the facts).\n- Size is about no merging, up to 25% more in competition: groups bring members whose own links point elsewhere. Grouping gives\n  structure, not compression.\n- Next class: wrong groups.\n\n**Wrong-group census ($0; the 9 no-home merges, read against exactly what Jev saw):**\n- M2, Sharon AI into Firmus: the incoming record's quote is the whole paragraph, Firmus sentence included, so Jev matched on the quote.\n  Step 1 bundle on the incoming side.\n- M57, NVL576 into NVL144: the stored record's own summary also states the NVL576 claim, so the merge is defensible. Step 1 bundle on\n  the stored side.\n- M30, analysts' 82% and 41% into the analysts' revenue path: different figures. A Jev error.\n- 6 arguable (the same announcement, the same figure, or a relayed report): under grouping both texts sit side by side.\n- So 2 real wrong groups out of 102, and their texts survive.\n\n**Open for Alex:**\n- the several-match rule (pending, best match, or own entry with references);\n- the Step 2 pass lines.\n\n## 11. Log rows\n\n| Arm | Hypothesis | Change | Result | Kept |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Grouping (A) | Keeping each repeat's text keeps added details noticeable without double counting | Isolated library and group view | Detail 25/36, the same as no merging, against 3/36 source-only; 0 double counting; size about the same as no merging | yes (Alex's decision; tested) |\n\n## 12. Close-out (2026-11-08)\n\n**Alex's decisions (2026-11-08):**\n- a record that is a repeat of several groups joins only the one Jev judges it most like (the highest same_statement probability; ties\n  go to the earliest group);\n- the Step 2 lines: (1) search at least 90%; (2) clear wrong merges at most 2%; (3) grouped dossiers keep no fewer later details than\n  no merging.\n\nBoth are recorded in PRD §5.\n\n**Built.**\n- `clean-arch/step2/compare.py`: search, Jev comparison, the outcome with best match, forward order, and a failed comparison refused and\n  reported. `test_compare.py`: 6 tests; all 4 mutants caught.\n- `run_forward.py` reads a stored Step 1 trace directly.\n- **Run** (`poc/data/clean-step2-forward-2026-11-08/`, a fresh Jev draw): 282 records made 178 groups.\n  - 104 repeats, 3 revisions, 0 refused; 16 records had several same_statement answers.\n  - The cached embedding texts were verified identical: 282 of 282 documents, 313 of 313 queries.\n\n**Council A** (`council/group/d-*`):\n- report search as recall over known labelled counterparts;\n- blind-review every merge, previously labelled positives included;\n- verify the cache texts;\n- re-run the reader test on the new views.\n\n| Line | Result | Met |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Search | 112/113 known labelled counterparts had a holding group among the candidates (99.1%). For 107 of them the group's original, which is what Jev saw, is itself a counterpart. This is not recall over all real repeats. | yes |\n| 2. Clear wrong merges | 104 merges read by two blind readers; 85 accepted by both; the 19 rejected by either went to a third reader: acceptable 1, overlap 15, **wrong 3** = 2.9% | **no** (missed by one merge) |\n| 3. Later details | Reader test re-run on the rebuilt views with the frozen questions and key: detail none 27.5, source 3, group **28** of 36; unsupported claims 3 / 2 / 2; double counting 0 | yes |\n\n**Line 2 detail.**\n- The 3 wrong merges:\n  - NVL576 into the NVL144 group, twice. The group's original Step 1 record states both claims.\n  - Analysts' 82% and 41% growth into the analysts' revenue-path record: different figures, a Jev error.\n- 15 overlaps, which the third reader called imperfect but not misleading to a reader of the record's own text. Among them:\n  - orders against sales ($1tn);\n  - expected revenue against committed off-take (Firmus);\n  - bundled records: the business model into the Sharon group, the Kyber delay report into the backup-rack group, the Taiwan raid\n    alongside the Singapore mansion.\n- My own reading had 5 wrong (Sharon AI into Firmus, and the business-model bundle into the Sharon group, both overlap to the third\n  reader). Both readings miss the line.\n\n**Council B** (`council/group/e-*`):\n- do not offer the result as a pass;\n- report 3 wrong, 15 overlap and 1 acceptable after dispute;\n- a later look at bundled records would explain only part of it;\n- stop and report.\n\n**Verdict.** Step 2 meets lines 1 and 3 and misses line 2 by one merge, in one Jev draw. Per Alex's instruction, the work stops here,\nand the census goes to him.\n\n**Not done:** the unprompted wrong-group reading test, and a fix for line 2.\n","numLines":337,"startLine":1,"totalLines":337}}}
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/round-1.jsonl:40:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tn1pyarmYA5KWGY1Q6pQjq","type":"tool_result","content":"1603\t## 43. Acceptance on the fresh set: every line met; links are the weak point (2026-11-09)\n1604\t\n1605\tThe fresh Step 1 run finished: 50 of 50 articles complete in 52 calls. Two format failures were resent once and passed; in one of\n1606\tthem the model had added a field the schema does not have.\n1607\t- Cost: $0.66 at published rates (input $0.3/M, output $1.2/M, no cache share applied).\n1608\t- 188 kept items and 10 opened theses.\n1609\t- Step 2 placed the 188 records in 131 groups (57 repeats, 0 refused). Later linking judged 182 pairs and linked 1.\n1610\t\n1611\t**Readers.** Two readers per batch of ten articles, and a third reader on the 23 disputes (8 matters, 15 links). On the two articles\n1612\tI had traced myself (9 and 42) the readers agree with me.\n1613\t\n1614\t| Line | Result | Pass |\n1615\t|---|---|---|\n1616\t| Helpful matters kept | 132 / 139 = **95.0%** (core 83 / 83). All 7 misses are supporting: two author estimates, Huang's agent-compute claim (kept in another article), the capital-return policy, a driver exploit report, handheld openness, a Kioxia SSD | ≥ 81.2%: **met** |\n1617\t| Wrongly opened theses | **1** of 10, by both readers: clean-5, \"will Vera Rubin account for about 20% of data-center revenue in fiscal Q3\", a revenue share and so a financial measure (D382) | ≤ 2: **met** |\n1618\t| Search | **141 / 143** settled repeat pairs = 98.6%; 2 pairs not exercised because a member was not kept | ≥ 90%: **met** |\n1619\t| Wrong merges | **1 / 57** = 1.8%, none unjudged. 52 were accepted by both readers; of the 5 disputed, the third reader found 2 acceptable, 2 overlap and 1 wrong (Vera Rubin deliveries merged into production status and revenue share) | ≤ 2%: **met** |\n1620\t| Detail retention | grouped **22 / 26** = ungrouped 22 / 26. This is structural: all 188 records are stored with their own text. The 4 details missing are Step 1 omissions | no fewer: **met** |\n1621\t\n1622\t**My disagreement with the readers on theses.** I would also call two theses wrong:\n1623\t- clean-9, global semiconductor revenue in 2026 (Deloitte's estimate): an industry aggregate that adds little beyond NVIDIA's own\n1624\t  guidance;\n1625\t- clean-10, NAND demand created by NVIDIA's CMX: a memory supplier's quantity.\n1626\t\n1627\tBoth readers judged them shared quantities that the standard allows. By my reading the count is 3, which misses the line. This is\n1628\treported both ways.\n1629\t\n1630\t**Reported without a line.**\n1631\t- **Kept items.** Both readers called 138 helpful, 20 noise and 13 optional; the rest were split.\n1632\t- **Links: 59 / 93 judged right (63%).** This is the weak point. I read all 34 links that were not judged right:\n1633\t  - about 25 are real errors, where an item is linked to the topically nearest open thesis without bearing on its question. Examples:\n1634\t    SK Hynix's Indiana plant linked to NVIDIA's supplier commitments; a Fed-cut probability linked to hyperscaler capex; Wistron's\n1635\t    share sale linked to Vera Rubin's revenue share; Micron's revenue linked to NVIDIA's commitments;\n1636\t  - about 9 look like reader strictness. Examples: the acquisition announcement that founded the Hugging Face thesis; memory-cost\n1637\t    margin pressure linked to the server DRAM price thesis.\n1638\t- **The class in production.** It is production's open defect 1, where news is filed under the nearest revenue question (D355),\n1639\t  and the later-link disagreement of §41.\n1640\t\n1641\t**Next class (for the council):** link precision. First, a $0 check on the dev draws:\n1642\t- links per item and the share of items linked to two or more theses, c8 against c7;\n1643\t- whether c8's \"link one statement to every thesis it informs\" (an audit amendment) raised over-linking.\n1644\t\n1645\t## 44. Dev ×3, the reflection council, and the link recheck (2026-11-09)\n1646\t\n1647\t**Dev ×3 of the frozen c8** (115 dev articles, an empty start, each draw complete: 116, 115 and 117 calls; frozen golden-v5 dev,\n1648\tsha `6f9476bb…`, scored as a lead):\n1649\t\n1650\t| Measure | Draw 1 | Draw 2 | Draw 3 | c7 (earlier) |\n1651\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1652\t| Helpful, of 234 (line 190) | 214 | 211 | 212 | 212 |\n1653\t| Core, of 86 | 85 | 86 | 86 | 86 |\n1654\t| Not-kept spans kept | 27 | 27 | 30 | 30 |\n1655\t| Theses opened | 8 | 10 | 10 | 10 |\n1656\t| must_open / may_open / none / must_not (one reader) | 4/3/1/0 | 3/3/3/1 (lidar) | 3/2/5/0 | 4/2/3/1 |\n1657\t| must_open covered, of 28 | 25 | 24 | 27 | 27 |\n1658\t| Search (reference mapped onto the draw) | 98/99 = 99.0% | 91/92 = 98.9% | 97/97 = 100% | |\n1659\t| Clear wrong merges (third reader) | 1/100 | 1/100 | 1/106 | |\n1660\t| Overlaps | 14 | 12 | 15 | |\n1661\t\n1662\t- **Stable across the three draws:** the six core theses (credit support, Vera Rubin, China H200, hyperscaler capex, Kyber, supply\n1663\t  commitments).\n1664\t- **Varies:** the edges (memory prices, Rubin Ultra, China budget shift, lidar, DeepSeek inquiry, custom ASIC pool, inference-chip\n1665\t  startups).\n1666\t- **Not covered in any draw:** the Ohio-guarantee yes/no question. The broader credit-support amount thesis is opened instead.\n1667\t\n1668\t**Reflection council** (a fresh thread, `council/ap-codex.md`, then `aq-codex.md`). It confirmed helpful, search and theses. It\n1669\tretracted its first claim of 3/57 wrong merges after reading the third reader's prompt: `merge_acceptable: false` is forced on\n1670\toverlaps, which the prompt defines as \"not misleading\", and the agreed line is clear wrong merges (D381). Agreed caveats:\n1671\t- report search both ways: 141/143 for search alone, 141/145 (97.2%) for the whole system. One 11-member group supplies 55 pairs, and\n1672\t  revisions are barely exercised;\n1673\t- line 3 is a storage comparison, not a usability test;\n1674\t- my thesis dissent is recorded as a breadth concern, not a count: the council reads clean-9 and clean-10 as supported shared\n1675\t  quantities;\n1676\t- c8 did not raise links per item, but that says nothing about whether the links are right.\n1677\t\n1678\tVerdict: REPORT AS STATED.\n1679\t\n1680\t**Link recheck (the council's cheapest next step).** A fresh senior-analyst reader saw the 34 links not judged right, mixed blind\n1681\twith 10 links judged right, and asked whether the item helps assess the question:\n1682\t\n1683\t| Original verdict | Useful | Weak | Wrong |\n1684\t|---|---|---|---|\n1685\t| Not right (34) | 13 | 19 | 2 |\n1686\t| Right, as controls (10) | 10 | 0 | 0 |\n1687\t\n1688\tOver all 93 links, about 72 are useful (77%), 19 are weak (20%: related, but they would not change the view) and 2 are wrong (2%).\n1689\tThe link problem is mainly clutter, not misleading filing. My earlier estimate of 25 real errors is withdrawn.\n1690\t\n1691\t**Next, for Alex:**\n1692\t- whether to adopt c8 as the clean Step 1 configuration (PRD §12);\n1693\t- whether link clutter, about one link in five, is worth a round. If it is, the round starts with the model's own account on weak\n1694\t  links, a few cents, before any design.\n1695\t\n1696\t## 45. Alex adopts c8 (D387); the PRD carries it; next, the account of weak links (2026-11-09)\n1697\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903","uuid":"2c2ad6f9-4b05-4544-84f8-5283f6e5411e","timestamp":"2026-11-09T00:55:21.631Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md","content":"## 43. Acceptance on the fresh set: every line met; links are the weak point (2026-11-09)\n\nThe fresh Step 1 run finished: 50 of 50 articles complete in 52 calls. Two format failures were resent once and passed; in one of\nthem the model had added a field the schema does not have.\n- Cost: $0.66 at published rates (input $0.3/M, output $1.2/M, no cache share applied).\n- 188 kept items and 10 opened theses.\n- Step 2 placed the 188 records in 131 groups (57 repeats, 0 refused). Later linking judged 182 pairs and linked 1.\n\n**Readers.** Two readers per batch of ten articles, and a third reader on the 23 disputes (8 matters, 15 links). On the two articles\nI had traced myself (9 and 42) the readers agree with me.\n\n| Line | Result | Pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| Helpful matters kept | 132 / 139 = **95.0%** (core 83 / 83). All 7 misses are supporting: two author estimates, Huang's agent-compute claim (kept in another article), the capital-return policy, a driver exploit report, handheld openness, a Kioxia SSD | ≥ 81.2%: **met** |\n| Wrongly opened theses | **1** of 10, by both readers: clean-5, \"will Vera Rubin account for about 20% of data-center revenue in fiscal Q3\", a revenue share and so a financial measure (D382) | ≤ 2: **met** |\n| Search | **141 / 143** settled repeat pairs = 98.6%; 2 pairs not exercised because a member was not kept | ≥ 90%: **met** |\n| Wrong merges | **1 / 57** = 1.8%, none unjudged. 52 were accepted by both readers; of the 5 disputed, the third reader found 2 acceptable, 2 overlap and 1 wrong (Vera Rubin deliveries merged into production status and revenue share) | ≤ 2%: **met** |\n| Detail retention | grouped **22 / 26** = ungrouped 22 / 26. This is structural: all 188 records are stored with their own text. The 4 details missing are Step 1 omissions | no fewer: **met** |\n\n**My disagreement with the readers on theses.** I would also call two theses wrong:\n- clean-9, global semiconductor revenue in 2026 (Deloitte's estimate): an industry aggregate that adds little beyond NVIDIA's own\n  guidance;\n- clean-10, NAND demand created by NVIDIA's CMX: a memory supplier's quantity.\n\nBoth readers judged them shared quantities that the standard allows. By my reading the count is 3, which misses the line. This is\nreported both ways.\n\n**Reported without a line.**\n- **Kept items.** Both readers called 138 helpful, 20 noise and 13 optional; the rest were split.\n- **Links: 59 / 93 judged right (63%).** This is the weak point. I read all 34 links that were not judged right:\n  - about 25 are real errors, where an item is linked to the topically nearest open thesis without bearing on its question. Examples:\n    SK Hynix's Indiana plant linked to NVIDIA's supplier commitments; a Fed-cut probability linked to hyperscaler capex; Wistron's\n    share sale linked to Vera Rubin's revenue share; Micron's revenue linked to NVIDIA's commitments;\n  - about 9 look like reader strictness. Examples: the acquisition announcement that founded the Hugging Face thesis; memory-cost\n    margin pressure linked to the server DRAM price thesis.\n- **The class in production.** It is production's open defect 1, where news is filed under the nearest revenue question (D355),\n  and the later-link disagreement of §41.\n\n**Next class (for the council):** link precision. First, a $0 check on the dev draws:\n- links per item and the share of items linked to two or more theses, c8 against c7;\n- whether c8's \"link one statement to every thesis it informs\" (an audit amendment) raised over-linking.\n\n## 44. Dev ×3, the reflection council, and the link recheck (2026-11-09)\n\n**Dev ×3 of the frozen c8** (115 dev articles, an empty start, each draw complete: 116, 115 and 117 calls; frozen golden-v5 dev,\nsha `6f9476bb…`, scored as a lead):\n\n| Measure | Draw 1 | Draw 2 | Draw 3 | c7 (earlier) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Helpful, of 234 (line 190) | 214 | 211 | 212 | 212 |\n| Core, of 86 | 85 | 86 | 86 | 86 |\n| Not-kept spans kept | 27 | 27 | 30 | 30 |\n| Theses opened | 8 | 10 | 10 | 10 |\n| must_open / may_open / none / must_not (one reader) | 4/3/1/0 | 3/3/3/1 (lidar) | 3/2/5/0 | 4/2/3/1 |\n| must_open covered, of 28 | 25 | 24 | 27 | 27 |\n| Search (reference mapped onto the draw) | 98/99 = 99.0% | 91/92 = 98.9% | 97/97 = 100% | |\n| Clear wrong merges (third reader) | 1/100 | 1/100 | 1/106 | |\n| Overlaps | 14 | 12 | 15 | |\n\n- **Stable across the three draws:** the six core theses (credit support, Vera Rubin, China H200, hyperscaler capex, Kyber, supply\n  commitments).\n- **Varies:** the edges (memory prices, Rubin Ultra, China budget shift, lidar, DeepSeek inquiry, custom ASIC pool, inference-chip\n  startups).\n- **Not covered in any draw:** the Ohio-guarantee yes/no question. The broader credit-support amount thesis is opened instead.\n\n**Reflection council** (a fresh thread, `council/ap-codex.md`, then `aq-codex.md`). It confirmed helpful, search and theses. It\nretracted its first claim of 3/57 wrong merges after reading the third reader's prompt: `merge_acceptable: false` is forced on\noverlaps, which the prompt defines as \"not misleading\", and the agreed line is clear wrong merges (D381). Agreed caveats:\n- report search both ways: 141/143 for search alone, 141/145 (97.2%) for the whole system. One 11-member group supplies 55 pairs, and\n  revisions are barely exercised;\n- line 3 is a storage comparison, not a usability test;\n- my thesis dissent is recorded as a breadth concern, not a count: the council reads clean-9 and clean-10 as supported shared\n  quantities;\n- c8 did not raise links per item, but that says nothing about whether the links are right.\n\nVerdict: REPORT AS STATED.\n\n**Link recheck (the council's cheapest next step).** A fresh senior-analyst reader saw the 34 links not judged right, mixed blind\nwith 10 links judged right, and asked whether the item helps assess the question:\n\n| Original verdict | Useful | Weak | Wrong |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Not right (34) | 13 | 19 | 2 |\n| Right, as controls (10) | 10 | 0 | 0 |\n\nOver all 93 links, about 72 are useful (77%), 19 are weak (20%: related, but they would not change the view) and 2 are wrong (2%).\nThe link problem is mainly clutter, not misleading filing. My earlier estimate of 25 real errors is withdrawn.\n\n**Next, for Alex:**\n- whether to adopt c8 as the clean Step 1 configuration (PRD §12);\n- whether link clutter, about one link in five, is worth a round. If it is, the round starts with the model's own account on weak\n  links, a few cents, before any design.\n\n## 45. Alex adopts c8 (D387); the PRD carries it; next, the account of weak links (2026-11-09)\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1603,"totalLines":2286}}}
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/round-1.jsonl:42:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018RXqdiV24m7f9QC4p7KLsh","type":"tool_result","content":"1131\t## 32. Step 2 re-measured on the c4 records; Jev's criteria aligned with the merge line (2026-11-08)\n1132\t\n1133\t**Run** (`clean-step2-forward-c4-2026-11-08/`): 289 records made 179 groups; 110 repeats, 4 revisions, 0 refused.\n1134\t\n1135\t**Line 1, search** (the reference carried onto the new records by `map_reference.py`: an identity rule on shared verbatim passages in the\n1136\tsame article; 89% of the old records map, 326 of 333 same-statement pairs): **104 of 105 known counterparts found (99.0%), met.**\n1137\t\n1138\t**Line 2, wrong merges** (two blind Codex readers per packet, a third on every pair either rejected; the same prompts as before, packets\n1139\tbuilt by `build_merge_packets.py`): **8 of 110 wrong (7.3%), missed**, against 3 of 104 before. Each wrong merge read:\n1140\t- **6 are one cascade.** Step 1 put SemiAnalysis's Kyber-delay report and NVIDIA's denial (\"Our roadmap is intact\") into one record\n1141\t  (49:e1). Every later outlet's denial was then judged by Jev a \"same_statement\" of that record, with probability 0.79–0.96.\n1142\t  - In k6b d1, k6b d2 and c2 the denial was its own record. Five re-sends of article 49 with c4 (`probe-r8-kyber-c4/`) separated it\n1143\t    5/5. So the bundling is rare (1 of 9 samples), but one bundle of a widely repeated statement multiplies into many wrong merges.\n1144\t  - The earlier Step 2 run's wrong merges were the same class (NVL576 into the NVL144 record, twice).\n1145\t- **The other 2 are Jev errors at low confidence:**\n1146\t  - X30, the analysts' $253bn → $392bn path against 82%/41% growth, the same pair as the earlier run (0.52);\n1147\t  - X99, China data-centre revenue at zero against no H200 revenue (0.57).\n1148\t- **A probability cut is not a fix.** Below 0.6, the c4 run's merges are 3 wrong and 4 acceptable; the earlier run's are 0 wrong and 5\n1149\t  acceptable.\n1150\t- **An original-party identity rule is not a fix either.** The free-text party differs in 28 accepted merges (\"Firmus Technologies\" and\n1151\t  \"Firmus\").\n1152\t\n1153\t**One-ruler fix (AGENTS §7.1).**\n1154\t- **The mismatch:** the merge line's test is \"later reports earlier's **main** statement\"; production's Jev criterion was \"the same\n1155\t  evidence\".\n1156\t- **The fix:** the clean Step 2 now asks Jev its own piece question:\n1157\t  - same_statement = the incoming piece reports the stored piece's main statement; a report of only a secondary statement is not it;\n1158\t  - supersedes keeps \"an official confirms or denies that reported claim\".\n1159\t- **The replay** (`…-p2/`; every other cached answer reused): 108 repeats, 3 revisions.\n1160\t  - Verdicts were carried to the 95 identical pairs; the 13 new pairs were read by two readers and one disputed pair by the third.\n1161\t  - **4 of 108 wrong (3.7%).**\n1162\t  - X30 and X99 no longer merge. Two denials became a revision of the report and a repeat of the standalone denial group.\n1163\t  - **The 4 left are the early denials into the bundled record 49:e1**, which arrived before any standalone denial group existed.\n1164\t- **Every other merge is acceptable or overlap.**\n1165\t\n1166\t**Later linking on the new criteria:** 173 judged, 1 linked (Valar). Line 3 (the reader test) was not re-measured: its dossiers are\n1167\tkeyed to the old run's thesis ids.\n1168\t\n1169\t**Next (the root of the remaining class).**\n1170\t- Step 1's unit, from the signed standard's own definition, \"陈述：原文里某一方说的一句话或一件事\" (a statement is what one party said):\n1171\t  one item holds one party's statement, and another party's response to it is its own item.\n1172\t- The c4 sentence \"an estimate and a target price … drawn from it are two statements\" is the same rule and is folded into it.\n1173\t\n1174\t## 33. Arm c5: one party's statement per item (2026-11-08)\n1175\t\n1176\t**c5** = c4 plus one closing-check sentence before \"Return only the evidence items you keep\":\n1177\t- the sentence: \"Each item you keep holds one party's statement: another party's response to it (a denial, a confirmation, a comment) is\n1178\t  its own item, and so is a valuation someone draws from an estimate\";\n1179\t- c4's system sentence \"An estimate and a target price or valuation someone draws from it are two statements\" is removed, because the\n1180\t  general rule now carries it;\n1181\t- sources: the signed standard's glossary, \"陈述：原文里某一方说的一句话或一件事\"; K6b §4, \"one evidence item records one statement\";\n1182\t  Alex's EPS/target ruling;\n1183\t- size: system 14,491 → 14,403; closing 8,114 → 8,302.\n1184\t\n1185\t**Check sends, 2 draws each** (`probe-r9-c5/`, $0.14):\n1186\t\n1187\t| Case | Result |\n1188\t|---|---|\n1189\t| Kyber article 49 | the denial is its own item 2/2 |\n1190\t| d1-57, d1-62 | one speaker's revenue and margin guidance stays one item; no over-splitting (3 and 7–8 items, as under c4) |\n1191\t| d1-66 | EPS kept, target dropped |\n1192\t| pos-1 | credit-support thesis opened |\n1193\t\n1194\t**Runs launched in parallel**, inputs byte-identical (source sha256 checked):\n1195\t- `gold-c5-flash/` (115 dev);\n1196\t- `test-c5-flash/` (26 test, scored once for c5).\n1197\t\n1198\tThe runner retries a cut answer (§30). Step 2 follows on the c5 dev records with the aligned Jev criteria (§32).\n1199\t\n1200\t## 34. c5 measured; Step 2 lines 1–2 met on its records; c6 narrows the rule and raises the output cap (2026-11-08)\n1201\t\n1202\t**c5 test** (`test-c5-flash/`, once): helpful **38/42**, core 9/9, **noise 11** (c4 4), items 57, theses 9 (0 small, 0 duplicates).\n1203\t\n1204\t**c5 dev** (`gold-c5-flash/`): 114 of 115. Article 54 (`…9756d6`, the SemiAnalysis backstop article, 16 helpful entries) was cut at the\n1205\t32k output cap twice and failed; the run continued, as Alex's rule says.\n1206\t\n1207\t| On the 114 articles both completed | **c5** | c4 |\n1208\t|---|---|---|\n1209\t| Helpful (of 218) | 204 | 203 |\n1210\t| Core | 80 | 79 |\n1211\t| Not-kept spans kept | **39** | 33 |\n1212\t\n1213\t**Regression read item by item on dev** (tuning set):\n1214\t- **What:** the extra noise is other parties' remarks extracted as their own items (Mizuho's and Bloomberg's analysts' remarks, a\n1215\t  defendant's lawyers' statement, Huang's \"parabolic\", startup CEOs' IPO plans).\n1216\t- **Mechanism:** c5's \"another party's response to it (a denial, a confirmation, **a comment**) is its own item\" licensed creating items\n1217\t  from remarks, not only unfolding a bundle.\n1218\t- The test showed the same direction.\n1219\t\n1220\t**The output cap is too low for the longest articles.**\n1221\t- Article 54 took 27k completion tokens in c2, 18k in c4, and over 32k twice in c5.\n1222\t- Re-sent once with a 64k cap, it completed: 37,210 tokens (32,074 reasoning), valid JSON, 16 items.\n1223\t- The cap was an implementation detail of the close-out (32,000), not a setting Alex chose. **Raised to 64,000.** Only long answers use\n1224\t  more.\n1225\t\n1226\t**Step 2 on the c5 records** (`clean-step2-forward-c5-2026-11-08/`, aligned Jev criteria):\n1227\t- **Kyber split cleanly:** the report group with 10 retellings, the denial group with 6.\n1228\t- Line 1, search: **94 of 96 (97.9%), met.** The reference is now mapped by input id: c5's failed article 54 had shifted the numbering\n1229\t  of every later article, so 34% mapped before the fix and 86% after; c4's mapping is unchanged at 89%.\n1230\t- Line 2, wrong merges: **2 of 105 (1.9%), met.** Both are isolated Jev errors: two different revenue-sharing programmes (0.84); three\n1231\t  companies' water work against NVIDIA's DSX design (0.50).\n1232\t- Later linking: 325 judged, 4 linked.\n1233\t\n1234\t**Identity fix in Step 2:** `run_forward.step1_records` and `build_merge_packets` number an article by its position in the first\n1235\tsegment's manifest, so a failed item no longer shifts later ids (AGENTS §3, identity). c4 and c2 numbers are unchanged; stored c5 Step\n1236\t2 results stay internally consistent.\n1237\t\n1238\t**c6** = c5 with:\n1239\t- the closing sentence narrowed to \"Do not fold another party's statement into an item: a report and the denial or confirmation of it are\n1240\t  two items, and so are an estimate and a valuation someone draws from it, each kept only if it passes the test above\";\n1241\t- max_output_tokens 64,000.\n1242\t\n1243\t**Checks** (`probe-r10-c6/`, 2 draws each, $0.14):\n1244\t- Kyber denial separate 2/2; no over-splitting (d1-57);\n1245\t- the lawyers' statement and the Mizuho and Bloomberg remarks are gone;\n1246\t- startup CEOs' own IPO remarks remain (their own statements, not folds).\n1247\t\n1248\t`gold-c6-flash/` and `test-c6-flash/` launched 04:10, inputs byte-identical.\n1249\t\n1250\t## 35. c6 measured: Step 2 lines 1–2 met; Step 1 misses only \"no fragments\" by one per set; the question gold is far stricter than the readers (2026-11-08)\n1251\t\n1252\t**c6 dev** (`gold-c6-flash/`): 115 of 115 in 115 calls; articles 54 and 59 completed under the 64k cap with no retries.\n1253\t\n1254\t| 115 dev articles, one draw | **c6** | c4 | c2 | k6b d1 / d2 | production v108 |\n1255\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1256\t| Helpful (line 190) | **219** | 216 | 209 | 205 / 197 | 162–171 |\n1257\t| Core | 85 | 84 | 85 | 84 / 85 | 75–76 |\n1258\t| Not-kept spans kept | **33** | 35 | 41 | 40 / 38 | 30–34 |\n1259\t| Theses opened | 20 | 14 | 24 | 25 / 18 | — |\n1260\t| Small / duplicate theses (majority of 3 source-backed readers) | 2 / **1** | 2 / 0 | 3 / 0 | 2–3 / 0 | — |\n1261\t\n1262\t**c6 test** (`test-c6-flash/`, once):\n1263\t- helpful **40/42**, core 9/9, not-kept 8 (c4 4, c5 11, k6b 8), 10 theses, small 2, **duplicate 1**;\n1264\t- misses: the valuation assumptions (Alex's ruling) and the telecom providers working on AI-RAN.\n1265\t\n1266\t**Step 2 on the c6 records** (`clean-step2-forward-c6-2026-11-08/`):\n1267\t- 291 records → 187 groups, 104 repeats; Kyber split cleanly (report 10, denial 8);\n1268\t- line 1, search: **95 of 97 (97.9%), met**;\n1269\t- line 2, wrong merges: **2 of 104 (1.9%), met** (X4, the two revenue-sharing programmes, recurring; X83);\n1270\t- later linking: 242 judged, 3 linked;\n1271\t- line 3 not re-measured (dossiers keyed to the old thesis ids; Step 3's grouping code unchanged since it passed).\n1272\t\n1273\t**The two duplicates, traced.**\n1274\t- **The theses:** dev \"AI labs building their own custom inference chips (DeepSeek, OpenAI)\" against the open \"non-Huawei competitors or\n1275\t  alternative stacks such as Cerebras\"; test \"xAI's Colossus 2 buildout\" against the open \"neocloud GPU rental prices as the demand\n1276\t  read-out\".\n1277\t- **The model's accounts** (`gold-c6-flash/fragment-accounts.json`): it applied K6b's separate-bet test (\"their answers can move apart\")\n1278\t  with analyst reasons: customer insourcing is not merchant competition; one lab's deployment is not neocloud pricing.\n1279\t- **The frozen question gold** marks both must_not: \"A DeepSeek chip development question\" and \"An OpenAI Jalapeno deployment question\";\n1280\t  \"Will xAI's Colossus 2 reach about 1.21 million NVIDIA GPUs by the end of 2026?\". So they are errors by both references, not a\n1281\t  reader's threshold.\n1282\t\n1283\t**Census: theses against the frozen question gold** (c6 dev 20, test 10; read against each opening article's must_not):\n1284\t- **About half match a must_not entry:**\n1285\t  - Intel's stake;\n1286\t  - the Singapore case;\n1287\t  - AI debt above $7T;\n1288\t  - Hugging Face robotics;\n1289\t  - Chinese-lidar legislation;\n1290\t  - SpaceX orbital data centres;\n1291\t  - DeepSeek/OpenAI chips;\n1292\t  - quantum (twice);\n1293\t  - SpaceX's $40bn financing;\n1294\t  - RTX Spark OEMs;\n1295\t  - AI-RAN revenue;\n1296\t  - xAI Colossus.\n1297\t- **The required openings are present:** credit support, Kyber NVL144, China access, supply commitments.\n1298\t- **Most of the must_not theses were judged \"bet\" by the readers.** k6b and c4 opened theses of the same kinds (c4's test opened the same\n1299\t  xAI thesis, then not flagged because nothing overlapped).\n1300\t- **So:** the reader-based pass line (small ≤ 3, fragments 0) and the frozen question gold disagree widely on what merits its own\n1301\t  thesis. Which one governs Step 1's thesis granularity is Alex's call (the council-loop trap: when references split on \"how big is\n1302\t  big\", his rulings are the ruler).\n1303\t\n1304\t**Status against the agreed lines** (one draw each):\n1305\t- **c6 meets:**\n1306\t  - Step 1 helpful (dev 219, test 40), small (2/2), noise (dev 33, at production's level);\n1307\t  - Step 2 search (97.9%) and wrong merges (1.9%).\n1308\t- **c6 misses:** \"no fragments\" by 1 on each set.\n1309\t- **c4** meets every Step 1 line but misses Step 2 wrong merges (3.7%, from one rare bundling cascade that c6's rule prevents).\n1310\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c06c24f-8f77-4add-9443-7081a27b7903","uuid":"796127db-4f24-49a3-a916-6ebb33423a10","timestamp":"2026-11-09T00:55:23.061Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md","content":"## 32. Step 2 re-measured on the c4 records; Jev's criteria aligned with the merge line (2026-11-08)\n\n**Run** (`clean-step2-forward-c4-2026-11-08/`): 289 records made 179 groups; 110 repeats, 4 revisions, 0 refused.\n\n**Line 1, search** (the reference carried onto the new records by `map_reference.py`: an identity rule on shared verbatim passages in the\nsame article; 89% of the old records map, 326 of 333 same-statement pairs): **104 of 105 known counterparts found (99.0%), met.**\n\n**Line 2, wrong merges** (two blind Codex readers per packet, a third on every pair either rejected; the same prompts as before, packets\nbuilt by `build_merge_packets.py`): **8 of 110 wrong (7.3%), missed**, against 3 of 104 before. Each wrong merge read:\n- **6 are one cascade.** Step 1 put SemiAnalysis's Kyber-delay report and NVIDIA's denial (\"Our roadmap is intact\") into one record\n  (49:e1). Every later outlet's denial was then judged by Jev a \"same_statement\" of that record, with probability 0.79–0.96.\n  - In k6b d1, k6b d2 and c2 the denial was its own record. Five re-sends of article 49 with c4 (`probe-r8-kyber-c4/`) separated it\n    5/5. So the bundling is rare (1 of 9 samples), but one bundle of a widely repeated statement multiplies into many wrong merges.\n  - The earlier Step 2 run's wrong merges were the same class (NVL576 into the NVL144 record, twice).\n- **The other 2 are Jev errors at low confidence:**\n  - X30, the analysts' $253bn → $392bn path against 82%/41% growth, the same pair as the earlier run (0.52);\n  - X99, China data-centre revenue at zero against no H200 revenue (0.57).\n- **A probability cut is not a fix.** Below 0.6, the c4 run's merges are 3 wrong and 4 acceptable; the earlier run's are 0 wrong and 5\n  acceptable.\n- **An original-party identity rule is not a fix either.** The free-text party differs in 28 accepted merges (\"Firmus Technologies\" and\n  \"Firmus\").\n\n**One-ruler fix (AGENTS §7.1).**\n- **The mismatch:** the merge line's test is \"later reports earlier's **main** statement\"; production's Jev criterion was \"the same\n  evidence\".\n- **The fix:** the clean Step 2 now asks Jev its own piece question:\n  - same_statement = the incoming piece reports the stored piece's main statement; a report of only a secondary statement is not it;\n  - supersedes keeps \"an official confirms or denies that reported claim\".\n- **The replay** (`…-p2/`; every other cached answer reused): 108 repeats, 3 revisions.\n  - Verdicts were carried to the 95 identical pairs; the 13 new pairs were read by two readers and one disputed pair by the third.\n  - **4 of 108 wrong (3.7%).**\n  - X30 and X99 no longer merge. Two denials became a revision of the report and a repeat of the standalone denial group.\n  - **The 4 left are the early denials into the bundled record 49:e1**, which arrived before any standalone denial group existed.\n- **Every other merge is acceptable or overlap.**\n\n**Later linking on the new criteria:** 173 judged, 1 linked (Valar). Line 3 (the reader test) was not re-measured: its dossiers are\nkeyed to the old run's thesis ids.\n\n**Next (the root of the remaining class).**\n- Step 1's unit, from the signed standard's own definition, \"陈述：原文里某一方说的一句话或一件事\" (a statement is what one party said):\n  one item holds one party's statement, and another party's response to it is its own item.\n- The c4 sentence \"an estimate and a target price … drawn from it are two statements\" is the same rule and is folded into it.\n\n## 33. Arm c5: one party's statement per item (2026-11-08)\n\n**c5** = c4 plus one closing-check sentence before \"Return only the evidence items you keep\":\n- the sentence: \"Each item you keep holds one party's statement: another party's response to it (a denial, a confirmation, a comment) is\n  its own item, and so is a valuation someone draws from an estimate\";\n- c4's system sentence \"An estimate and a target price or valuation someone draws from it are two statements\" is removed, because the\n  general rule now carries it;\n- sources: the signed standard's glossary, \"陈述：原文里某一方说的一句话或一件事\"; K6b §4, \"one evidence item records one statement\";\n  Alex's EPS/target ruling;\n- size: system 14,491 → 14,403; closing 8,114 → 8,302.\n\n**Check sends, 2 draws each** (`probe-r9-c5/`, $0.14):\n\n| Case | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Kyber article 49 | the denial is its own item 2/2 |\n| d1-57, d1-62 | one speaker's revenue and margin guidance stays one item; no over-splitting (3 and 7–8 items, as under c4) |\n| d1-66 | EPS kept, target dropped |\n| pos-1 | credit-support thesis opened |\n\n**Runs launched in parallel**, inputs byte-identical (source sha256 checked):\n- `gold-c5-flash/` (115 dev);\n- `test-c5-flash/` (26 test, scored once for c5).\n\nThe runner retries a cut answer (§30). Step 2 follows on the c5 dev records with the aligned Jev criteria (§32).\n\n## 34. c5 measured; Step 2 lines 1–2 met on its records; c6 narrows the rule and raises the output cap (2026-11-08)\n\n**c5 test** (`test-c5-flash/`, once): helpful **38/42**, core 9/9, **noise 11** (c4 4), items 57, theses 9 (0 small, 0 duplicates).\n\n**c5 dev** (`gold-c5-flash/`): 114 of 115. Article 54 (`…9756d6`, the SemiAnalysis backstop article, 16 helpful entries) was cut at the\n32k output cap twice and failed; the run continued, as Alex's rule says.\n\n| On the 114 articles both completed | **c5** | c4 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Helpful (of 218) | 204 | 203 |\n| Core | 80 | 79 |\n| Not-kept spans kept | **39** | 33 |\n\n**Regression read item by item on dev** (tuning set):\n- **What:** the extra noise is other parties' remarks extracted as their own items (Mizuho's and Bloomberg's analysts' remarks, a\n  defendant's lawyers' statement, Huang's \"parabolic\", startup CEOs' IPO plans).\n- **Mechanism:** c5's \"another party's response to it (a denial, a confirmation, **a comment**) is its own item\" licensed creating items\n  from remarks, not only unfolding a bundle.\n- The test showed the same direction.\n\n**The output cap is too low for the longest articles.**\n- Article 54 took 27k completion tokens in c2, 18k in c4, and over 32k twice in c5.\n- Re-sent once with a 64k cap, it completed: 37,210 tokens (32,074 reasoning), valid JSON, 16 items.\n- The cap was an implementation detail of the close-out (32,000), not a setting Alex chose. **Raised to 64,000.** Only long answers use\n  more.\n\n**Step 2 on the c5 records** (`clean-step2-forward-c5-2026-11-08/`, aligned Jev criteria):\n- **Kyber split cleanly:** the report group with 10 retellings, the denial group with 6.\n- Line 1, search: **94 of 96 (97.9%), met.** The reference is now mapped by input id: c5's failed article 54 had shifted the numbering\n  of every later article, so 34% mapped before the fix and 86% after; c4's mapping is unchanged at 89%.\n- Line 2, wrong merges: **2 of 105 (1.9%), met.** Both are isolated Jev errors: two different revenue-sharing programmes (0.84); three\n  companies' water work against NVIDIA's DSX design (0.50).\n- Later linking: 325 judged, 4 linked.\n\n**Identity fix in Step 2:** `run_forward.step1_records` and `build_merge_packets` number an article by its position in the first\nsegment's manifest, so a failed item no longer shifts later ids (AGENTS §3, identity). c4 and c2 numbers are unchanged; stored c5 Step\n2 results stay internally consistent.\n\n**c6** = c5 with:\n- the closing sentence narrowed to \"Do not fold another party's statement into an item: a report and the denial or confirmation of it are\n  two items, and so are an estimate and a valuation someone draws from it, each kept only if it passes the test above\";\n- max_output_tokens 64,000.\n\n**Checks** (`probe-r10-c6/`, 2 draws each, $0.14):\n- Kyber denial separate 2/2; no over-splitting (d1-57);\n- the lawyers' statement and the Mizuho and Bloomberg remarks are gone;\n- startup CEOs' own IPO remarks remain (their own statements, not folds).\n\n`gold-c6-flash/` and `test-c6-flash/` launched 04:10, inputs byte-identical.\n\n## 35. c6 measured: Step 2 lines 1–2 met; Step 1 misses only \"no fragments\" by one per set; the question gold is far stricter than the readers (2026-11-08)\n\n**c6 dev** (`gold-c6-flash/`): 115 of 115 in 115 calls; articles 54 and 59 completed under the 64k cap with no retries.\n\n| 115 dev articles, one draw | **c6** | c4 | c2 | k6b d1 / d2 | production v108 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Helpful (line 190) | **219** | 216 | 209 | 205 / 197 | 162–171 |\n| Core | 85 | 84 | 85 | 84 / 85 | 75–76 |\n| Not-kept spans kept | **33** | 35 | 41 | 40 / 38 | 30–34 |\n| Theses opened | 20 | 14 | 24 | 25 / 18 | — |\n| Small / duplicate theses (majority of 3 source-backed readers) | 2 / **1** | 2 / 0 | 3 / 0 | 2–3 / 0 | — |\n\n**c6 test** (`test-c6-flash/`, once):\n- helpful **40/42**, core 9/9, not-kept 8 (c4 4, c5 11, k6b 8), 10 theses, small 2, **duplicate 1**;\n- misses: the valuation assumptions (Alex's ruling) and the telecom providers working on AI-RAN.\n\n**Step 2 on the c6 records** (`clean-step2-forward-c6-2026-11-08/`):\n- 291 records → 187 groups, 104 repeats; Kyber split cleanly (report 10, denial 8);\n- line 1, search: **95 of 97 (97.9%), met**;\n- line 2, wrong merges: **2 of 104 (1.9%), met** (X4, the two revenue-sharing programmes, recurring; X83);\n- later linking: 242 judged, 3 linked;\n- line 3 not re-measured (dossiers keyed to the old thesis ids; Step 3's grouping code unchanged since it passed).\n\n**The two duplicates, traced.**\n- **The theses:** dev \"AI labs building their own custom inference chips (DeepSeek, OpenAI)\" against the open \"non-Huawei competitors or\n  alternative stacks such as Cerebras\"; test \"xAI's Colossus 2 buildout\" against the open \"neocloud GPU rental prices as the demand\n  read-out\".\n- **The model's accounts** (`gold-c6-flash/fragment-accounts.json`): it applied K6b's separate-bet test (\"their answers can move apart\")\n  with analyst reasons: customer insourcing is not merchant competition; one lab's deployment is not neocloud pricing.\n- **The frozen question gold** marks both must_not: \"A DeepSeek chip development question\" and \"An OpenAI Jalapeno deployment question\";\n  \"Will xAI's Colossus 2 reach about 1.21 million NVIDIA GPUs by the end of 2026?\". So they are errors by both references, not a\n  reader's threshold.\n\n**Census: theses against the frozen question gold** (c6 dev 20, test 10; read against each opening article's must_not):\n- **About half match a must_not entry:**\n  - Intel's stake;\n  - the Singapore case;\n  - AI debt above $7T;\n  - Hugging Face robotics;\n  - Chinese-lidar legislation;\n  - SpaceX orbital data centres;\n  - DeepSeek/OpenAI chips;\n  - quantum (twice);\n  - SpaceX's $40bn financing;\n  - RTX Spark OEMs;\n  - AI-RAN revenue;\n  - xAI Colossus.\n- **The required openings are present:** credit support, Kyber NVL144, China access, supply commitments.\n- **Most of the must_not theses were judged \"bet\" by the readers.** k6b and c4 opened theses of the same kinds (c4's test opened the same\n  xAI thesis, then not flagged because nothing overlapped).\n- **So:** the reader-based pass line (small ≤ 3, fragments 0) and the frozen question gold disagree widely on what merits its own\n  thesis. Which one governs Step 1's thesis granularity is Alex's call (the council-loop trap: when references split on \"how big is\n  big\", his rulings are the ruler).\n\n**Status against the agreed lines** (one draw each):\n- **c6 meets:**\n  - Step 1 helpful (dev 219, test 40), small (2/2), noise (dev 33, at production's level);\n  - Step 2 search (97.9%) and wrong merges (1.9%).\n- **c6 misses:** \"no fragments\" by 1 on each set.\n- **c4** meets every Step 1 line but misses Step 2 wrong merges (3.7%, from one rare bundling cascade that c6's rule prevents).\n","numLines":180,"startLine":1131,"totalLines":2286}}}
