poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/judge/reader-B-3.log:74:- Alex, on a record that repeats several groups: "只放进最像的那组". On the lines he chose "三条线": search at least 90%; clear wrong merges
poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/judge/reader-B-3.log:1442:    "item": "22:ev-11",
poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/judge/reader-B-3.log:3358:{"article": 22, "title": "Nvidia is no longer just an AI chip company. It is financing the revolution it built", "text": "Nvidia is no longer just an AI chip company. It is financing the revolution it built\nNvidia has expanded from GPUs into networking, software, computing systems and strategic investments in AI companies, while helping finance the infrastructure that buys its products. With revenue expected to approach $400 billion this year, investors are asking how long the extraordinary growth can continue.\nNvidia’s numbers have long since transcended the boundaries of a typical chip company. The company is expanding from processors into communications, software, computing systems and investments in AI companies, while also helping to finance the ecosystem that buys its products. In doing so, it is becoming not just the major supplier of the AI revolution, but one of the companies shaping and funding it.\nA week and a half ago, Nvidia published its second-quarter results for fiscal 2027. Nvidia reports according to a fiscal year that does not coincide with the calendar year, with its fiscal year ending in the last week of January. The company’s revenue totaled $96.2 billion, an increase of 106% from the corresponding quarter, and it expects revenue of about $108 billion in the next quarter. The results and forecast reinforced expectations that rapid growth will continue through calendar 2027. Following the earnings report, Nvidia’s stock soared, and on August 27, the company’s market value reached about $5.5 trillion. The following day, the stock experienced a slight sell-off, and its market value fell to $5.24 trillion.\nNvidia is the company with the greatest impact on the AI revolution. The processors, computing systems, networking equipment and software it develops form much of the infrastructure on which many of the world’s most advanced AI models are built and run. In recent years, the company has rapidly expanded its product and service offering, from GPU chips to processors, communications systems, storage, software and complete computing systems, with the aim of becoming a central provider of almost every layer of data center infrastructure required for the AI era. Some of that expansion has been achieved through acquisitions.\nNvidia’s success raises a broader question: Is this an unusual investment cycle, or the beginning of a long-term structural change in the semiconductor industry?\nNvidia is the world’s most valuable company and the clear leader in the semiconductor sector. Traditionally, the sector has been cyclical, with periods of rapid growth in demand and investment alternating with periods of overcapacity, inventory buildup and slowdown. A common rule of thumb is a three- to five-year cycle, although the length varies widely across different segments of the industry.\nThe cyclical nature of the semiconductor industry is particularly evident in the memory chip market, but it also exists in processors, CPUs and other areas. However, the expansion of demand for GPUs and AI systems is taking place on a different scale. Huge investments by cloud companies and data centers in AI infrastructure are rapidly increasing demand for computing and may extend the positive investment cycle beyond the traditional cyclicality of the chip industry. As a result, investors in the semiconductor sector are constantly looking for signs that the growth rate is beginning to change.\nThe performance of the capital markets illustrates how unusual the current cycle is. The SOXX basket fund, which invests in semiconductor stocks, rose about tenfold from the end of June 2019 to the end of June 2026, compared with a 2.5-fold increase in the SPY basket fund, which tracks the S&P 500. Nvidia’s stock itself rose about 48-fold over the same period. Part of the extraordinary increase is also related to the acquisition of Mellanox, completed in 2020, which significantly expanded Nvidia’s activities in communications and networking for data centers.\nSince the beginning of July this year, the SOXX basket fund has retreated by about 20%. Nvidia, on the other hand, returned to a price close to the peak it recorded earlier this year following its strong earnings report. The gap between Nvidia’s performance and that of the broader sector illustrates that investors do not value all chip companies in the same way, and that Nvidia continues to benefit from exceptionally high growth expectations.\nNvidia employs approximately 42,000 people worldwide, including about 6,000 in Israel. Its Israeli operations have expanded rapidly since the acquisition of Mellanox and include a significant presence in communications, chips, software and systems engineering. Because of its size and scope, Nvidia has a growing impact on the Israeli economy, including exports, employment, activity in the high-tech industry and state tax revenues.\nOver the past decade and into the beginning of this decade, Nvidia has significantly upgraded its GPU chips. Products that once focused primarily on graphics processing have become central parallel processors for artificial intelligence applications, simulations and data centers.\nIn 2022, Nvidia introduced the Hopper GPU architecture, led by the H100 processor. The product family is primarily designed for training and running large AI models, high-performance computing and data centers.\nIn 2024, Nvidia launched its Blackwell architecture, expanding the company’s approach from a single processor to a complete system that includes a CPU, GPU, memory, communication between accelerators and software.\nIn December 2025, Nvidia acquired a significant portion of Groq’s technology, particularly its LPU architecture, which was designed specifically for inference, running already-trained models and generating responses at high speed. The deal included a payment of $13 billion and an additional $4 billion in future payments.\nAnother leap forward is the Vera Rubin architecture. Its main innovation is the closer integration of processors, AI chips, memory and communications, allowing large systems to operate faster and more efficiently. Vera Rubin is designed not only for training models but also for optimizing inference, producing more responses in less time and with lower power consumption, particularly for large models. During the analyst call following the earnings release, Nvidia said that systems based on Vera Rubin had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.\nOver the weekend, Nvidia announced that it had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $11.9 billion in cash and another $1 billion in options. Hugging Face will bring Nvidia models, a developer community and runtime capabilities. Together with the Groq deal, the acquisitions expand Nvidia’s product and service offering into additional parts of the AI chain, as the company seeks to increase the sources of revenue it generates from each data center.\nThe expectation is that the mix of data center workloads will gradually change, shifting from a high proportion of training, the process of developing models, performed mainly by companies building large language models, toward a growing share of inference, or running already-trained models to generate answers, perform tasks and power AI-based applications.\nNvidia’s preparation for this shift is evident in the combination of Groq technology, which specializes in fast, low-latency inference, and the Vera Rubin platform, which can integrate Groq 3 LPX accelerators dedicated to inference alongside Vera Rubin’s GPU and CPU.\nInvestors’ concern stems from the expectation that Nvidia will face more intense competition in chips designed for inference, particularly from Google. A shift in the mix of data center workloads toward inference could therefore create a greater competitive challenge for Nvidia.\n92% of revenue comes from data center infrastructure\nThe AI revolution has enabled Nvidia to deliver an extraordinary increase in revenue. In fiscal 2023, which ended in January 2023, the company’s revenue totaled $26.97 billion. In fiscal 2024, it jumped to $60.92 billion, and in fiscal 2025, it doubled again to $130.5 billion. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, the growth rate moderated to 65%, while revenue reached $215.9 billion.\nThis year, however, revenue is expected to jump to about $400 billion, an increase of about 85% from the previous year. During the analyst call, Nvidia said it expects revenue to increase by another 70% next year, with that fiscal year ending in January 2028. The company’s confidence in the forecast stems, among other things, from its assessment that demand will remain high and that the factor limiting sales will be chip supply and production capacity.\nNvidia’s gross margin was about 75% in each of the first two quarters. It is expected to fall to about 74% in the third quarter and 71%-72% in the fourth quarter, mainly because of the sharp increase in memory chip prices. The operating margin was about 66% in the first two quarters, and its expected decline later this year is expected to be more moderate than that of the gross margin, thanks to the sharp increase in revenue and the fixed component of operating expenses.\nBased on these assumptions, the company’s operating income this year is expected to be approximately $260 billion, while net income on a non-GAAP basis is expected to reach approximately $218 billion.\nIn this analysis, it is preferable to use non-GAAP data, partly because of the company’s conservative approach to neutralizing certain accounting expenses and because these figures exclude capital gains from investments, which were significant this year. Nvidia reported investment gains of $15.94 billion in the first quarter and $7.77 billion in the second quarter. As noted, these gains are excluded from our analysis.\nApproximately 92% of Nvidia’s revenue comes from data center infrastructure, including chips and connectivity. In the first half of the year, the company’s three largest direct customers accounted for 44% of revenue. Geographically, about 62.4% of revenue came from customers headquartered in the United States. Since these are companies with multinational operations, some of those sales ultimately went to other regions. About 28% of revenue came from Taiwan, about 8% from China and Hong Kong, with China’s direct share accounting for less than 1%, and 1.6% from the rest of the world.\nNvidia’s expected earnings multiple for this year is about 24, based on a market value of $5.24 trillion and estimated non-GAAP net income of $218 billion. However, the company’s revenue and earnings are expected to continue growing rapidly next year. According to the assumptions in this analysis, based on revenue growth of about 70% and a gross margin of about 72%, revenue could reach approximately $680 billion and net income about $370 billion. That would bring the forward earnings multiple down to about 14, which appears low relative to the expected growth rate.\nUnder these assumptions, Nvidia’s stock does not look expensive based on its forward earnings multiple relative to its expected growth rate. This conclusion is also supported by the relatively high probability that the company will meet its sales target for next year. If demand for data center infrastructure continues to grow rapidly from 2028 onward and Nvidia manages to maintain its leading position, further growth in revenue and profits could support a further increase in the share price.\nThe negative scenario, however, includes a slowdown in the construction of new data centers beginning toward the end of the current decade, the emergence of significant competition from other companies, and the accelerated development of dedicated AI chips by Nvidia’s largest customers themselves. These factors could slow the growth of revenue and profits and weigh on the share price.\nAnother risk is rapid improvement in computing efficiency. If more AI functionality can be extracted from each unit of hardware, the growth rate in demand for chips could be lower than the growth rate in AI usage.\nNvidia divides its data center revenue into two main markets: hyperscale customers, which mainly include cloud providers and large internet companies, and the rest of the market, defined as ACIE, AI Clouds, Industrial & Enterprise.\nNvidia makes this analytical distinction to show the sources of growth in its data center business and illustrate the expansion of demand beyond the relatively limited group of hyperscale customers. A broader distribution of demand could reduce Nvidia’s dependence on hyperscale customers, which are themselves exposed to the risk of developing dedicated chips, as well as to changes in the composition of demand as the AI market gradually shifts from model training toward inference.\nIn the second quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia’s revenue from hyperscale customers reached $48.7 billion, an increase of 13% from the previous quarter. ACIE revenue totaled $40.3 billion, an increase of 25%.\nAccording to Nvidia’s forecast, most of the growth in the third quarter is expected to come from ACIE, while growth in hyperscale is expected to accelerate again in the fourth quarter and throughout fiscal 2028 as the supply of the Vera Rubin platform expands. As noted, Nvidia expects fiscal 2028 revenue to grow by about 70%, with supply constraints expected to remain a limiting factor at least through the end of that year.\nAlongside its business expansion, Nvidia has significantly increased its investment activity across the AI ecosystem. The company reported that, as of the end of the quarter, it had about $99 billion invested in shares of other companies, as well as another $25 billion in commitments for future investments. These investments are focused on AI model makers, infrastructure companies and other private companies.\nThe investments help fast-growing companies finance the computing and data center infrastructure they need, allowing Nvidia to bridge the gap between the large investments those companies require and the future cash flow expected to result from them. But this also raises concerns about potential circular financing: Nvidia invests in companies that can then use that money to purchase Nvidia infrastructure and processors.\nNvidia, on the other hand, argues that these are also strategic investments in companies with the potential to become technology leaders, strengthening the ecosystem and supporting long-term demand for accelerated computing.\nOne of Nvidia’s most notable investments was in xAI, Elon Musk’s artificial intelligence company, which was later merged into SpaceX. Following the merger, Nvidia held 122.8 million SpaceX shares at the end of June, now worth about $17 billion. Another significant investment is in Intel. At the end of June, Nvidia held 214.8 million Intel shares worth about $20 billion. In both cases, Nvidia benefited from a significant increase in the value of its holdings.\nIn August 2026, Nvidia entered into memoranda of understanding with six major investment and financial institutions to establish independent financing platforms designed to raise more than $500 billion from third parties over time to build AI infrastructure. The move is intended to facilitate financing for the acquisition of Nvidia products.\nWill demand for AI grow fast enough?\nNvidia expects a significant decline in gross margin over the next two quarters, mainly because of the sharp increase in component costs, particularly memory prices. As noted, the gross margin was 75% in the second quarter, and Nvidia expects it to decline later this year. In fiscal 2028, the gross margin is expected to stabilize at around 72%-73%, after the price increases take effect in the first quarter.\nThe advanced memory market is largely dominated by three manufacturers: SK Hynix and Samsung of South Korea, and Micron of the United States. Of particular importance is HBM, or high-bandwidth memory, which has become a vital component in advanced AI computing systems and one of the key growth drivers of the memory market. Demand for HBM is expected to continue growing rapidly in 2027, with shipments expected to increase by approximately 50%-60%, although still not enough to meet full demand.\nHowever, part of the increase in memory manufacturers’ revenue and profitability also stems from a sharp rise in component prices caused by supply constraints. This creates a risk for the shares of the three memory manufacturers. If they increase production capacity faster than expected, if additional manufacturers enter the market, or if the growth rate of AI demand moderates, the balance of power in the market could change.\nIn such a scenario, a decline in memory prices could lead to a sharp drop in the profitability of memory manufacturers and, consequently, weigh on their share prices. On the other hand, at least over the next two years, the current pricing environment could allow memory manufacturers to report exceptionally high profitability.\nThe conclusion is that the AI revolution is expected to change the intensity and length of the investment cycle in the semiconductor industry, but it will not eliminate the cycle itself.\nNvidia is in a unique position. It is benefiting from the rapid expansion of demand for accelerated computing, expanding its share of the data center value chain and trying to ensure that its massive investments across the AI infrastructure ecosystem will continue to generate demand for its products even as the center of gravity shifts from training to inference.\nThe key question is whether demand for AI will grow fast enough to justify the massive investments currently being made in AI infrastructure. Nvidia is the biggest beneficiary of that spending, but it is also increasingly dependent on the future development of the very market it is helping to build.\nThe writer is an economist at an Israel-based tech company.", "matters": [["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#1", "Nvidia expects about $108 billion of revenue in the next quarter."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#2", "Nvidia said Vera Rubin systems were commercially delivered to customers in August 2026."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#3", "The article reports that Nvidia agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $11.9 billion in cash and $1 billion in options."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#4", "Nvidia expects revenue to grow 70% in the fiscal year ending January 2028, with supply and production capacity limiting sales."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#5", "The article expects gross margin to fall to about 74% in the third quarter and 71%–72% in the fourth, mainly because of memory prices."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#6", "The author's analysis estimates approximately $260 billion in operating income and $218 billion in non-GAAP net income this year."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#7", "Nvidia's three largest direct customers accounted for 44% of first-half revenue."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#8", "The author's analysis estimates approximately $680 billion in revenue and $370 billion in net income next year under the stated growth and margin assumptions."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#9", "Nvidia's second-quarter hyperscaler revenue was $48.7 billion and ACIE revenue was $40.3 billion, up 13% and 25% sequentially."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#10", "Nvidia expects ACIE to drive most third-quarter growth and hyperscaler growth to accelerate in the fourth quarter and fiscal 2028 as Vera Rubin supply expands."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#11", "Nvidia reported about $99 billion in equity investments and another $25 billion in future investment commitments at quarter end."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#12", "Nvidia signed memoranda with six financial institutions for platforms intended to raise more than $500 billion from third parties over time for AI infrastructure."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#13", "The article expects fiscal 2028 gross margin to stabilize around 72%–73% after memory price increases."]], "before": [{"id": "clean-1", "question": "How much will Nvidia's commitments to suppliers (purchase obligations, primarily memory procurement) be at its next annual report, after rising to $279 billion in the second quarter from $119 billion the prior quarter?"}, {"id": "clean-2", "question": "Will Nvidia complete its announced $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, the open-source AI model repository, by a check date within 18 months of the September 2026 announcement?"}, {"id": "clean-3", "question": "How much will Nvidia have guaranteed or committed in credit support for AI infrastructure projects at its next annual report, after agreeing in August 2026 to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio?"}, {"id": "clean-4", "question": "Will the combined capital expenditures of the top five hyperscalers approach $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027, as Nvidia expects?"}, {"id": "clean-5", "question": "Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, the next-generation system now in production and receiving purchase orders from hyperscalers, account for about 20% of the data center segment's revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, as management guided?"}, {"id": "clean-6", "question": "Will server DRAM prices jump another 260% in calendar 2026, after rising 64% in the second half of 2025, as the item projects?"}], "items": [["22:ev-1", "Nvidia reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from the corresponding quarter a year earlier.", []], ["22:ev-2", "Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion.", []], ["22:ev-3", "Calcalistech estimates Nvidia's fiscal 2027 revenue at about $400 billion, up about 85% from fiscal 2026.", []], ["22:ev-4", "On its fiscal Q2 2027 analyst call, Nvidia said it expects revenue to grow by another 70% in fiscal 2028, with demand high and chip supply and production capacity the limiting factor.", []], ["22:ev-5", "Nvidia reported gross margin of about 75% in each of fiscal Q1-Q2 2027 and operating margin of about 66%; it guided gross margin to about 74% in fiscal Q3 2027 and 71%-72% in fiscal Q4 2027, stabilizing around 72%-73% in fiscal 2028, mainly because of sharp increases in memory chip prices.", [["clean-6", "Will server DRAM prices jump another 260% in calendar 2026, after rising 64% in the second half of 2025, as the item projects?"]]], ["22:ev-6", "Calcalistech estimates Nvidia's fiscal 2027 operating income at approximately $260 billion and non-GAAP net income at approximately $218 billion.", []], ["22:ev-7", "Nvidia reported investment gains of $15.94 billion in fiscal Q1 2027 and $7.77 billion in fiscal Q2 2027.", []], ["22:ev-8", "Nvidia disclosed that approximately 92% of revenue comes from data center infrastructure; in the first half of fiscal 2027 its three largest direct customers accounted for 44% of revenue, and about 62.4% of revenue came from US-headquartered customers, 28% from Taiwan, 8% from China and Hong Kong (direct China less than 1%), and 1.6% from the rest of the world.", []], ["22:ev-9", "In fiscal Q2 2027, Nvidia's revenue from hyperscale customers was $48.7 billion, up 13% sequentially, while ACIE revenue was $40.3 billion, up 25% sequentially.", []], ["22:ev-10", "Nvidia forecast that most of its fiscal Q3 2027 growth would come from ACIE, while hyperscale growth would accelerate again in fiscal Q4 2027 and throughout fiscal 2028 as supply of the Vera Rubin platform expands.", [["clean-5", "Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, the next-generation system now in production and receiving purchase orders from hyperscalers, account for about 20% of the data center segment's revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, as management guided?"]]], ["22:ev-11", "Nvidia said on its fiscal Q2 2027 analyst call that systems based on its Vera Rubin architecture had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.", [["clean-5", "Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, the next-generation system now in production and receiving purchase orders from hyperscalers, account for about 20% of the data center segment's revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, as management guided?"]]], ["22:ev-12", "Over the weekend before the 2026-09-06 article, Nvidia announced it had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $11.9 billion in cash and another $1 billion in options.", [["clean-2", "Will Nvidia complete its announced $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, the open-source AI model repository, by a check date within 18 months of the September 2026 announcement?"]]], ["22:ev-13", "As of the end of fiscal Q2 2027, Nvidia reported about $99 billion invested in shares of other companies and another $25 billion in commitments for future investments, focused on AI model makers, infrastructure companies and other private companies.", [["clean-7", "How much will Nvidia's commitments for future equity investments in AI and infrastructure companies be at its next annual report, after reporting $25 billion of such commitments at the end of fiscal Q2 2027?"]]], ["22:ev-14", "In August 2026, Nvidia entered into memoranda of understanding with six major investment and financial institutions to establish independent financing platforms designed to raise more than $500 billion from third parties over time to build AI infrastructure and facilitate purchases of Nvidia products.", [["clean-3", "How much will Nvidia have guaranteed or committed in credit support for AI infrastructure projects at its next annual report, after agreeing in August 2026 to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio?"]]], ["22:ev-15", "The article projects that demand for high-bandwidth memory (HBM) will continue growing rapidly in 2027, with shipments expected to increase by approximately 50%-60%, still not enough to meet full demand.", [["clean-8", "Will global high-bandwidth memory (HBM) shipments grow by approximately 50%-60% in calendar 2027, still falling short of demand, as the item projects?"]]], ["22:ev-16", "After xAI merged into SpaceX, Nvidia held 122.8 million SpaceX shares at the end of June, worth about $17 billion, and 214.8 million Intel shares worth about $20 billion; the article says Nvidia benefited from significant increases in both holdings.", []]], "opened": [{"thesis": "clean-7", "question": "How much will Nvidia's commitments for future equity investments in AI and infrastructure companies be at its next annual report, after reporting $25 billion of such commitments at the end of fiscal Q2 2027?", "outcome": "The reported amount of Nvidia's commitments for future investments at its next annual report.", "period": "Next annual report: Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027 (filed in early 2027); set by the rule that an obligation already undertaken is tracked at the next annual report.", "opens_as": "binding_obligation", "settled_by": "Nvidia's next Form 10-K, including its commitments and contingencies and investment disclosures.", "closest_open_thesis": "clean-3", "why_a_separate_bet": "Equity investment commitments are not guarantees or supplier purchase obligations; they are cash commitments to AI companies that can create circular financing and demand, and their amount can move independently of Nvidia's guarantee total and supplier purchase obligations."}, {"thesis": "clean-8", "question": "Will global high-bandwidth memory (HBM) shipments grow by approximately 50%-60% in calendar 2027, still falling short of demand, as the item projects?", "outcome": "Global HBM shipment growth and whether HBM supply remains short of demand in calendar 2027.", "period": "Calendar 2027; checked against HBM shipment data and memory manufacturers' 2027 reports.", "opens_as": "shared_quantity", "settled_by": "HBM shipment and pricing data from TrendForce/DRAMeXchange and memory manufacturers' quarterly disclosures for 2027.", "closest_open_thesis": "clean-6", "why_a_separate_bet": "HBM is a distinct memory product with its own capacity and demand; server DRAM price inflation in 2026 can reverse while HBM remains short in 2027, and Nvidia's GPU supply and costs depend on HBM specifically."}]}
poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/judge/reader-B-3.log:3371:{"article": 22, "matters": [["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#1", "Nvidia expects about $108 billion of revenue in the next quarter."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#2", "Nvidia said Vera Rubin systems were commercially delivered to customers in August 2026."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#3", "The article reports that Nvidia agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $11.9 billion in cash and $1 billion in options."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#4", "Nvidia expects revenue to grow 70% in the fiscal year ending January 2028, with supply and production capacity limiting sales."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#5", "The article expects gross margin to fall to about 74% in the third quarter and 71%–72% in the fourth, mainly because of memory prices."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#6", "The author's analysis estimates approximately $260 billion in operating income and $218 billion in non-GAAP net income this year."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#7", "Nvidia's three largest direct customers accounted for 44% of first-half revenue."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#8", "The author's analysis estimates approximately $680 billion in revenue and $370 billion in net income next year under the stated growth and margin assumptions."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#9", "Nvidia's second-quarter hyperscaler revenue was $48.7 billion and ACIE revenue was $40.3 billion, up 13% and 25% sequentially."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#10", "Nvidia expects ACIE to drive most third-quarter growth and hyperscaler growth to accelerate in the fourth quarter and fiscal 2028 as Vera Rubin supply expands."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#11", "Nvidia reported about $99 billion in equity investments and another $25 billion in future investment commitments at quarter end."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#12", "Nvidia signed memoranda with six financial institutions for platforms intended to raise more than $500 billion from third parties over time for AI infrastructure."], ["gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#13", "The article expects fiscal 2028 gross margin to stabilize around 72%–73% after memory price increases."]], "items": [["22:ev-1", "Nvidia reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from the corresponding quarter a year earlier.", []], ["22:ev-2", "Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion.", []], ["22:ev-3", "Calcalistech estimates Nvidia's fiscal 2027 revenue at about $400 billion, up about 85% from fiscal 2026.", []], ["22:ev-4", "On its fiscal Q2 2027 analyst call, Nvidia said it expects revenue to grow by another 70% in fiscal 2028, with demand high and chip supply and production capacity the limiting factor.", []], ["22:ev-5", "Nvidia reported gross margin of about 75% in each of fiscal Q1-Q2 2027 and operating margin of about 66%; it guided gross margin to about 74% in fiscal Q3 2027 and 71%-72% in fiscal Q4 2027, stabilizing around 72%-73% in fiscal 2028, mainly because of sharp increases in memory chip prices.", [["clean-6", "Will server DRAM prices jump another 260% in calendar 2026, after rising 64% in the second half of 2025, as the item projects?"]]], ["22:ev-6", "Calcalistech estimates Nvidia's fiscal 2027 operating income at approximately $260 billion and non-GAAP net income at approximately $218 billion.", []], ["22:ev-7", "Nvidia reported investment gains of $15.94 billion in fiscal Q1 2027 and $7.77 billion in fiscal Q2 2027.", []], ["22:ev-8", "Nvidia disclosed that approximately 92% of revenue comes from data center infrastructure; in the first half of fiscal 2027 its three largest direct customers accounted for 44% of revenue, and about 62.4% of revenue came from US-headquartered customers, 28% from Taiwan, 8% from China and Hong Kong (direct China less than 1%), and 1.6% from the rest of the world.", []], ["22:ev-9", "In fiscal Q2 2027, Nvidia's revenue from hyperscale customers was $48.7 billion, up 13% sequentially, while ACIE revenue was $40.3 billion, up 25% sequentially.", []], ["22:ev-10", "Nvidia forecast that most of its fiscal Q3 2027 growth would come from ACIE, while hyperscale growth would accelerate again in fiscal Q4 2027 and throughout fiscal 2028 as supply of the Vera Rubin platform expands.", [["clean-5", "Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, the next-generation system now in production and receiving purchase orders from hyperscalers, account for about 20% of the data center segment's revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, as management guided?"]]], ["22:ev-11", "Nvidia said on its fiscal Q2 2027 analyst call that systems based on its Vera Rubin architecture had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.", [["clean-5", "Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, the next-generation system now in production and receiving purchase orders from hyperscalers, account for about 20% of the data center segment's revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, as management guided?"]]], ["22:ev-12", "Over the weekend before the 2026-09-06 article, Nvidia announced it had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $11.9 billion in cash and another $1 billion in options.", [["clean-2", "Will Nvidia complete its announced $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, the open-source AI model repository, by a check date within 18 months of the September 2026 announcement?"]]], ["22:ev-13", "As of the end of fiscal Q2 2027, Nvidia reported about $99 billion invested in shares of other companies and another $25 billion in commitments for future investments, focused on AI model makers, infrastructure companies and other private companies.", [["clean-7", "How much will Nvidia's commitments for future equity investments in AI and infrastructure companies be at its next annual report, after reporting $25 billion of such commitments at the end of fiscal Q2 2027?"]]], ["22:ev-14", "In August 2026, Nvidia entered into memoranda of understanding with six major investment and financial institutions to establish independent financing platforms designed to raise more than $500 billion from third parties over time to build AI infrastructure and facilitate purchases of Nvidia products.", [["clean-3", "How much will Nvidia have guaranteed or committed in credit support for AI infrastructure projects at its next annual report, after agreeing in August 2026 to provide guarantees of up to $105 billion for a data center project in Ohio?"]]], ["22:ev-15", "The article projects that demand for high-bandwidth memory (HBM) will continue growing rapidly in 2027, with shipments expected to increase by approximately 50%-60%, still not enough to meet full demand.", [["clean-8", "Will global high-bandwidth memory (HBM) shipments grow by approximately 50%-60% in calendar 2027, still falling short of demand, as the item projects?"]]], ["22:ev-16", "After xAI merged into SpaceX, Nvidia held 122.8 million SpaceX shares at the end of June, worth about $17 billion, and 214.8 million Intel shares worth about $20 billion; the article says Nvidia benefited from significant increases in both holdings.", []]], "opened": [{"thesis": "clean-7", "question": "How much will Nvidia's commitments for future equity investments in AI and infrastructure companies be at its next annual report, after reporting $25 billion of such commitments at the end of fiscal Q2 2027?", "outcome": "The reported amount of Nvidia's commitments for future investments at its next annual report.", "period": "Next annual report: Nvidia's Form 10-K for the fiscal year ending late January 2027 (filed in early 2027); set by the rule that an obligation already undertaken is tracked at the next annual report.", "opens_as": "binding_obligation", "settled_by": "Nvidia's next Form 10-K, including its commitments and contingencies and investment disclosures.", "closest_open_thesis": "clean-3", "why_a_separate_bet": "Equity investment commitments are not guarantees or supplier purchase obligations; they are cash commitments to AI companies that can create circular financing and demand, and their amount can move independently of Nvidia's guarantee total and supplier purchase obligations."}, {"thesis": "clean-8", "question": "Will global high-bandwidth memory (HBM) shipments grow by approximately 50%-60% in calendar 2027, still falling short of demand, as the item projects?", "outcome": "Global HBM shipment growth and whether HBM supply remains short of demand in calendar 2027.", "period": "Calendar 2027; checked against HBM shipment data and memory manufacturers' 2027 reports.", "opens_as": "shared_quantity", "settled_by": "HBM shipment and pricing data from TrendForce/DRAMeXchange and memory manufacturers' quarterly disclosures for 2027.", "closest_open_thesis": "clean-6", "why_a_separate_bet": "HBM is a distinct memory product with its own capacity and demand; server DRAM price inflation in 2026 can reverse while HBM remains short in 2027, and Nvidia's GPU supply and costs depend on HBM specifically."}]}
poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/judge/reader-B-3.log:3671:{"articles":[{"article":21,"items":[{"item":"21:ev-1","matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"21:ev-2","matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-2","judgement":"right","why":"The announced acquisition is the transaction whose completion the thesis tracks."}]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#1","status":"kept","item":"21:ev-1","note":"The item calls the market server/host CPUs, which the source does not specify."},{"matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#2","status":"kept","item":"21:ev-2","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#3","status":"kept","item":"21:ev-2","note":""}],"theses":[]},{"article":22,"items":[{"item":"22:ev-1","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: past actual results presented as background.","links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-2","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-3","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The current-year revenue estimate is useful under §5.1 but has no settled matter in this article; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-4","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#4","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-5","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#5","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-6","judgement":"wrong","why":"Higher memory costs affect Nvidia's margin, but the item does not establish the change in server DRAM prices that the thesis asks about."}]},{"item":"22:ev-6","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#6","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-7","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: earlier quarterly actual gains are presented as background.","links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-8","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#7","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-9","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#9","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-10","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#10","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-5","judgement":"right","why":"The forecast ties accelerating customer growth to expanded Vera Rubin supply, which bears on its revenue ramp."}]},{"item":"22:ev-11","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-5","judgement":"right","why":"Commercial deliveries show that Vera Rubin has begun reaching customers before the quarter being judged."}]},{"item":"22:ev-12","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-2","judgement":"right","why":"The item reports the agreement whose completion the thesis tracks."}]},{"item":"22:ev-13","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#11","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-7","judgement":"right","why":"The reported $25 billion is the starting level of the investment commitments being tracked."}]},{"item":"22:ev-14","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#12","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-3","judgement":"wrong","why":"The planned platforms would raise third-party financing; the item does not say Nvidia guarantees or commits credit support."}]},{"item":"22:ev-15","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The HBM shipment forecast is useful under §5.1 and directly addresses an open thesis, but it has no settled matter; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[{"thesis":"clean-8","judgement":"right","why":"The item states the shipment growth and shortfall forecast that the thesis asks about."}]},{"item":"22:ev-16","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: existing equity holdings and their values are presented as background.","links":[]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#1","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-2","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#2","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-11","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#3","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-12","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#4","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-4","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#5","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-5","note":"The item attributes the Q3 and Q4 estimates to Nvidia, while this passage presents them as expectations without explicitly attributing them to management."},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#6","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-6","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#7","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-8","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#8","status":"missed","item":null,"note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#9","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-9","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#10","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-10","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#11","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-13","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#12","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-14","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#13","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-5","note":""}],"theses":[{"thesis":"clean-7","judgement":"ok","class":null,"against":null,"why":"The large, already undertaken future investment commitments are a distinct obligation from supplier purchases and credit guarantees."},{"thesis":"clean-8","judgement":"ok","class":null,"against":null,"why":"Global HBM shipments are a shared quantity with a distinct product and period from the existing server DRAM price question."}]},{"article":23,"items":[{"item":"23:e1","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The recent reported results are useful under §5.1; the source does not clearly present them as an old retelling, and there is no settled matter for them.","links":[]},{"item":"23:e2","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"The CFO's forecast directly states the 2027 hyperscaler spending figure being tracked."}]},{"item":"23:e3","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"23:e4","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"A large cloud backlog is concrete evidence of demand that can support future hyperscaler infrastructure spending."}]},{"item":"23:e5","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The quantified increase in Alphabet's model usage is potentially useful demand evidence under §5.1 but has no settled matter; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[]},{"item":"23:e6","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#1","status":"kept","item":"23:e4","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#2","status":"kept","item":"23:e2","note":"The separate item 23:e6 keeps the longer-term $3 trillion–$4 trillion spending estimate."},{"matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#3","status":"kept","item":"23:e3","note":"The $461 billion conditional operating-income estimate is missing."}],"theses":[]},{"article":24,"items":[{"item":"24:e1","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: past quarterly actual results are presented as background.","links":[]},{"item":"24:e2","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#10","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e3","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#7","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e4","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#8","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-1","judgement":"right","why":"The item reports the current amount and schedule of the supply commitments being tracked."}]},{"item":"24:e5","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#11","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-6","judgement":"wrong","why":"The effect of memory prices on Nvidia's gross margin does not establish the magnitude of server DRAM price changes."}]},{"item":"24:e6","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#12","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-3","judgement":"right","why":"The Ohio guarantee is the concrete credit commitment named in the thesis."}]},{"item":"24:e7","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#13","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-3","judgement":"wrong","why":"The share of Nvidia's business from labs receiving balance-sheet support does not quantify the guarantees or credit commitments outstanding."}]},{"item":"24:e8","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#4","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e9","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#5","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e10","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#6","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e11","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"The item gives the projected top-five hyperscaler spending that the thesis tracks."}]},{"item":"24:e12","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"The cloud backlog indicates customer demand that can support hyperscaler infrastructure investment."}]},{"item":"24:e13","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#9","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e14","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e15","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The dated market-implied rate forecast is a concrete macro estimate under §5.1 but has no settled matter; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"wrong","why":"A possible single rate hike is too indirect to establish whether the specified hyperscalers will spend $1.3 trillion in 2027."}]},{"item":"24:e16","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-5","judgement":"wrong","why":"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt does not show Vera Rubin's share of data-center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027."}]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#1","status":"kept","item":"24:e14","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#2","status":"kept","item":"24:e11","note":"The backlog is preserved separately in 24:e12."},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#3","status":"kept","item":"24:e16","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#4","status":"kept","item":"24:e8","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#5","status":"kept","item":"24:e9","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#6","status":"kept","item":"24:e10","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#7","status":"kept","item":"24:e3","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#8","status":"kept","item":"24:e4","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#9","status":"kept","item":"24:e13","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#10","status":"kept","item":"24:e2","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#11","status":"kept","item":"24:e5","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#12","status":"kept","item":"24:e6","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#13","status":"kept","item":"24:e7","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#14","status":"missed","item":null,"note":""}],"theses":[]},{"article":25,"items":[{"item":"25:E1","matter":"gnews:5aaa1773b10d0e7c8402bea7#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-5","judgement":"wrong","why":"A Groq rack deployment alongside Vera Rubin at one neocloud does not show the platform's share of Nvidia's Q3 data-center revenue."}]},{"item":"25:E2","matter":"gnews:5aaa1773b10d0e7c8402bea7#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-7","judgement":"right","why":"The reported OpenAI commitment bears on the scale of Nvidia's future investment commitments."}]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:5aaa1773b10d0e7c8402bea7#1","status":"kept","item":"25:E1","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:5aaa1773b10d0e7c8402bea7#2","status":"kept","item":"25:E2","note":""}],"theses":[]},{"article":26,"items":[{"item":"26:ev-1","matter":"gnews:600b1392a71d33ef099f12a7#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"26:ev-2","matter":"gnews:600b1392a71d33ef099f12a7#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:600b1392a71d33ef099f12a7#1","status":"kept","item":"26:ev-1","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:600b1392a71d33ef099f12a7#2","status":"kept","item":"26:ev-2","note":""}],"theses":[]},{"article":27,"items":[{"item":"27:e1","matter":"gnews:73c49e8bc3fd457e8209fa2d#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-7","judgement":"right","why":"The disclosed $25 billion is the existing amount of the future investment commitments being tracked."}]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:73c49e8bc3fd457e8209fa2d#1","status":"kept","item":"27:e1","note":""}],"theses":[]},{"article":28,"items":[{"item":"28:E1","matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"The projection directly states the top-five hyperscaler spending being tracked."}]},{"item":"28:E2","matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"28:E3","matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"28:E4","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The trailing profit margin is a concrete company financial figure under §5.1 and an input to the author's estimate, but has no settled matter; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[]},{"item":"28:E5","matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#4","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#1","status":"kept","item":"28:E1","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#2","status":"kept","item":"28:E2","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#3","status":"kept","item":"28:E3","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e#4","status":"kept","item":"28:E5","note":""}],"theses":[]},{"article":29,"items":[{"item":"29:ev-1","matter":"gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"29:ev-2","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"Broadcom's reported AI-chip growth is a concrete competitor result under §5.1 but has no settled matter; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"Rapid growth in a major AI-chip supplier's sales provides evidence of the infrastructure demand behind hyperscaler spending, though it does not measure that spending directly."}]},{"item":"29:ev-3","matter":"gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"A major AI-chip supplier's multiyear revenue forecast is evidence about demand for the infrastructure hyperscalers buy."}]},{"item":"29:ev-4","matter":"gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"Azure's backlog is concrete customer demand that can support Microsoft's infrastructure spending."}]},{"item":"29:ev-5","matter":"gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"Rapid cloud revenue growth is evidence of demand for Alphabet's cloud infrastructure, although it does not measure capital spending."}]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5#1","status":"kept","item":"29:ev-3","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5#2","status":"kept","item":"29:ev-1","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5#3","status":"kept","item":"29:ev-4","note":"Google Cloud's growth is preserved separately in 29:ev-5."}],"theses":[]},{"article":30,"items":[{"item":"30:e1","matter":"gnews:a68f4be941102b46a8fbce0f#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-2","judgement":"right","why":"The item reports the agreement and expected closing date for the acquisition being tracked."}]},{"item":"30:e2","matter":"gnews:a68f4be941102b46a8fbce0f#4","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-2","judgement":"wrong","why":"The target's revenue and deal multiple do not establish whether the agreed acquisition will close by the check date."}]},{"item":"30:e3","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: past quarterly actual results are used as background.","links":[]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:a68f4be941102b46a8fbce0f#1","status":"kept","item":"30:e1","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:a68f4be941102b46a8fbce0f#2","status":"kept","item":"30:e1","note":"The item says the platform will remain open but omits the explicit promise that Nvidia compute will not be required."},{"matter":"gnews:a68f4be941102b46a8fbce0f#3","status":"kept","item":"30:e1","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:a68f4be941102b46a8fbce0f#4","status":"kept","item":"30:e2","note":"The item also keeps an 86-times valuation conclusion, which D385 says to discard."}],"theses":[]}]}
poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/judge/reader-B-3.log:3674:{"articles":[{"article":21,"items":[{"item":"21:ev-1","matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"21:ev-2","matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-2","judgement":"right","why":"The announced acquisition is the transaction whose completion the thesis tracks."}]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#1","status":"kept","item":"21:ev-1","note":"The item calls the market server/host CPUs, which the source does not specify."},{"matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#2","status":"kept","item":"21:ev-2","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:39120c8fb43ef925adeb53ca#3","status":"kept","item":"21:ev-2","note":""}],"theses":[]},{"article":22,"items":[{"item":"22:ev-1","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: past actual results presented as background.","links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-2","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-3","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The current-year revenue estimate is useful under §5.1 but has no settled matter in this article; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-4","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#4","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-5","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#5","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-6","judgement":"wrong","why":"Higher memory costs affect Nvidia's margin, but the item does not establish the change in server DRAM prices that the thesis asks about."}]},{"item":"22:ev-6","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#6","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-7","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: earlier quarterly actual gains are presented as background.","links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-8","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#7","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-9","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#9","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"22:ev-10","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#10","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-5","judgement":"right","why":"The forecast ties accelerating customer growth to expanded Vera Rubin supply, which bears on its revenue ramp."}]},{"item":"22:ev-11","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-5","judgement":"right","why":"Commercial deliveries show that Vera Rubin has begun reaching customers before the quarter being judged."}]},{"item":"22:ev-12","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-2","judgement":"right","why":"The item reports the agreement whose completion the thesis tracks."}]},{"item":"22:ev-13","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#11","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-7","judgement":"right","why":"The reported $25 billion is the starting level of the investment commitments being tracked."}]},{"item":"22:ev-14","matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#12","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-3","judgement":"wrong","why":"The planned platforms would raise third-party financing; the item does not say Nvidia guarantees or commits credit support."}]},{"item":"22:ev-15","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The HBM shipment forecast is useful under §5.1 and directly addresses an open thesis, but it has no settled matter; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[{"thesis":"clean-8","judgement":"right","why":"The item states the shipment growth and shortfall forecast that the thesis asks about."}]},{"item":"22:ev-16","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: existing equity holdings and their values are presented as background.","links":[]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#1","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-2","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#2","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-11","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#3","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-12","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#4","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-4","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#5","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-5","note":"The item attributes the Q3 and Q4 estimates to Nvidia, while this passage presents them as expectations without explicitly attributing them to management."},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#6","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-6","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#7","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-8","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#8","status":"missed","item":null,"note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#9","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-9","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#10","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-10","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#11","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-13","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#12","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-14","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed#13","status":"kept","item":"22:ev-5","note":""}],"theses":[{"thesis":"clean-7","judgement":"ok","class":null,"against":null,"why":"The large, already undertaken future investment commitments are a distinct obligation from supplier purchases and credit guarantees."},{"thesis":"clean-8","judgement":"ok","class":null,"against":null,"why":"Global HBM shipments are a shared quantity with a distinct product and period from the existing server DRAM price question."}]},{"article":23,"items":[{"item":"23:e1","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The recent reported results are useful under §5.1; the source does not clearly present them as an old retelling, and there is no settled matter for them.","links":[]},{"item":"23:e2","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"The CFO's forecast directly states the 2027 hyperscaler spending figure being tracked."}]},{"item":"23:e3","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#3","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"23:e4","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#1","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-4","judgement":"right","why":"A large cloud backlog is concrete evidence of demand that can support future hyperscaler infrastructure spending."}]},{"item":"23:e5","matter":null,"verdict":null,"row":"The quantified increase in Alphabet's model usage is potentially useful demand evidence under §5.1 but has no settled matter; the supplied verdict choices do not include this case.","links":[]},{"item":"23:e6","matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#2","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]}],"matters":[{"matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#1","status":"kept","item":"23:e4","note":""},{"matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#2","status":"kept","item":"23:e2","note":"The separate item 23:e6 keeps the longer-term $3 trillion–$4 trillion spending estimate."},{"matter":"gnews:58037b5fd4d25e05d8e39198#3","status":"kept","item":"23:e3","note":"The $461 billion conditional operating-income estimate is missing."}],"theses":[]},{"article":24,"items":[{"item":"24:e1","matter":null,"verdict":"optional","row":"§5.3 known facts retold: past quarterly actual results are presented as background.","links":[]},{"item":"24:e2","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#10","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e3","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#7","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[]},{"item":"24:e4","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#8","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis":"clean-1","judgement":"right","why":"The item reports the current amount and schedule of the supply commitments being tracked."}]},{"item":"24:e5","matter":"gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce#11","verdict":null,"row":null,"links":[{"thesis"