reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/partial-review.out:18:运行在第 7 个案例 `18:e2` 的第一次请求发生连接重置后停止：**36/66 次完成，1 次结果不明，另外 29 次未发送**。剩余五个案例都没有完整对照，包括原先预测会改善的 Vera Rubin 错误合并案例 `22:ev-11`。因此不能声称分数无效、等价或整体没有风险；只能说**已完成的六个案例没有显示分组改善或恶化，也没有修订关系变化**。
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-2.stderr:38:     9	SOURCE=ROOT/'poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09'; STEP=SOURCE/'step2';IDS=['22:ev-11','13:e11','38:ev-2','18:e2','43:e3','3:e1','9:e6','14:e3','5:e1','9:e1','19:ev-guidance-70']
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-2.stderr:96:     3	Alex asks whether sending embedding similarity to Jev helps, given existing high retrieval recall and merge quality. This is an isolated fixed-history diagnostic, not a product change, adoption, a fresh held-out acceptance, or a comparison of search recall. Prior recorded acceptance 141/143 is search recall, 1/57 clearly wrong merges is not recall of Jev's repeat decisions. The fresh frozen reference and old labels do not change. Start with 11 archived c8 fresh comparison requests, selected before sending, covering the clearly wrong merge, disputed overlaps, accepted repeats, and new-evidence controls: `22:ev-11`, `13:e11`, `38:ev-2`, `18:e2`, `43:e3`, `3:e1`, `9:e6`, `14:e3`, `5:e1`, `9:e1`, `19:ev-guidance-70`. Two arms times three draws = 66 calls, with the original candidate sets and state fixed. This is a selected diagnostic and cannot measure overall precision or recall. A larger comparison is considered only if the cheap probe reveals a useful signal (AGENTS §7.3); the raw articles are already used, so neither probe is fresh acceptance.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-2.stderr:140:    47	1. Report every request, candidate, transport failure, cost and baseline variability. Quantify disagreements C vs T and change in wrong merges, missed repeats, revisions and target group, by request, comparing the three draws in each arm. Score-sensitivity is not proof of accuracy. No search recall improvement can be attributed to this intervention because search is held fixed.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-2.stderr:151:    58	Hypothesis: score information moves at least one relevant comparison and makes at least one source-backed decision better without a source-backed decision getting worse. Prediction: the clearly wrong X22 grouping becomes separate more often in T than C, while the selected accepted-repeat controls remain correctly grouped. We do not predict a perfect result. Predeclare individual decision comparisons, 3 samples per arm and show all 66 outcomes and errors. For each outcome and selected source-backed candidate, count correct and incorrect choices across three draws per arm. A repeatable improvement requires at least one agreed previously incorrect decision to become correct in at least 2 of 3 T draws and fewer than 2 of 3 C draws, with no agreed previously correct decision becoming wrong in at least 2 of 3 T draws while correct in at least 2 of 3 C draws. A repeatable regression stops the line. If neither occurs or independent readers disagree, report inconclusive. This is a screening rule for selected examples, not a quantitative estimate of product impact. Blind-read all eleven incoming records against all their candidate sets and source articles, including unchanged controls; report gaps. Register case ids and score mapping and historical top-ten replay before any send; send no paid calls until council review and preflight pass.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-2.stderr:155:    62	The read-only Codex reviewer cleared harness design but not sending. Claude reviewed the earlier 10-case/three-arm draft and found lack of revision coverage, unsupported X22 direction and number presentation ambiguity; an eleventh recorded revision-bearing case was added. Its conclusion that 40 borderline probabilities bound opportunity is a hypothesis; confident outputs can also be wrong. The X22 prediction is a falsifiable probe hypothesis, not evidence of likely improvement. The shuffled arm is removed due to false attribution; C versus T assesses the total intervention, not score causation.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-2.stderr:531:reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/CONCLUSIONS.md:19:Reconstruct identical saved candidate requests; interleave fresh no-score controls with true-score requests and a score-shuffled control, with fixed score representation and randomization, repeated draws, cache misses, and identical model, candidates and question content except score lines. Make each score's query origin explicit: multiple search queries may yield different scores. A shuffled control tests sensitivity to a number but needs care with singleton/equal-score cases and preserving query origin. A source-blind independent reader judges differing pairs and controls without seeing arm, score, or prior decision. Report wrong merges, missed repeats, incorrect revision classifications and group choices separately, all cases, with same-arm variability, cost and limitations; fixed-candidate comparisons do not prove forward-run performance. The fresh set has few or no revisions; use separately identified stored revision cases without presenting isolated pair results as product-path evidence. Freeze design, audit and preflight under repository rules before any paid send. A score can also encode context Jev did not directly receive (e.g. thesis wording or period), so improvement would not itself establish the mechanism.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-2.stderr:534:reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:58:Hypothesis: score information moves at least one relevant comparison and makes at least one source-backed decision better without a source-backed decision getting worse. Prediction: the clearly wrong X22 grouping becomes separate more often in T than C, while the selected accepted-repeat controls remain correctly grouped. We do not predict a perfect result. Predeclare individual decision comparisons, 3 samples per arm and show all 66 outcomes and errors. For each outcome and selected source-backed candidate, count correct and incorrect choices across three draws per arm. A repeatable improvement requires at least one agreed previously incorrect decision to become correct in at least 2 of 3 T draws and fewer than 2 of 3 C draws, with no agreed previously correct decision becoming wrong in at least 2 of 3 T draws while correct in at least 2 of 3 C draws. A repeatable regression stops the line. If neither occurs or independent readers disagree, report inconclusive. This is a screening rule for selected examples, not a quantitative estimate of product impact. Blind-read all eleven incoming records against all their candidate sets and source articles, including unchanged controls; report gaps. Register case ids and score mapping and historical top-ten replay before any send; send no paid calls until council review and preflight pass.
