reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/codex-review.stderr:1018:    58	Hypothesis: score information moves at least one relevant comparison beyond the change caused by a shuffled number and makes at least one source-backed decision better without a source-backed decision getting worse. Prediction: the clearly wrong X22 grouping becomes separate more often in T than C, while the selected accepted-repeat controls remain correctly grouped. We do not predict a perfect result. Predeclare individual decision comparisons, 3 samples per arm and show all 90 outcomes and errors. If T fails to improve any inspected decision, or causes a material wrong merge or loses a valid repeat without a balancing demonstrated gain, stop this line and report; no full experiment follows automatically. If a decisive contrast is absent or conflicting, report inconclusive. This is a heuristic stop on the selected probe, not a quantitative estimate of product impact. Blind-read every differing case plus each selected known-merge reference and at least two unchanged controls, including all original source material. Register case ids and score mapping before any send; send no paid calls until council review and preflight pass.
