reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review.stderr:689:reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:3:Alex asks whether sending embedding similarity to Jev helps, given existing high retrieval recall and merge quality. This is an isolated fixed-history diagnostic, not a product change, adoption, a fresh held-out acceptance, or a comparison of search recall. Prior recorded acceptance 141/143 is search recall, 1/57 clearly wrong merges is not recall of Jev's repeat decisions. The fresh frozen reference and old labels do not change. Start with 11 archived c8 fresh comparison requests, selected before sending, covering the clearly wrong merge, disputed overlaps, accepted repeats, and new-evidence controls: `22:ev-11`, `13:e11`, `38:ev-2`, `18:e2`, `43:e3`, `3:e1`, `9:e6`, `14:e3`, `5:e1`, `9:e1`, `19:ev-guidance-70`. Two arms times three draws = 66 calls, with the original candidate sets and state fixed. This is a selected diagnostic and cannot measure overall precision or recall. A larger comparison is considered only if the cheap probe reveals a useful signal (AGENTS §7.3); the raw articles are already used, so neither probe is fresh acceptance.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review.stderr:692:reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:58:Hypothesis: score information moves at least one relevant comparison and makes at least one source-backed decision better without a source-backed decision getting worse. Prediction: the clearly wrong X22 grouping becomes separate more often in T than C, while the selected accepted-repeat controls remain correctly grouped. We do not predict a perfect result. Predeclare individual decision comparisons, 3 samples per arm and show all 66 outcomes and errors. For each outcome and selected source-backed candidate, count correct and incorrect choices across three draws per arm. A repeatable improvement requires at least one agreed previously incorrect decision to become correct in at least 2 of 3 T draws and fewer than 2 of 3 C draws, with no agreed previously correct decision becoming wrong in at least 2 of 3 T draws while correct in at least 2 of 3 C draws. A repeatable regression stops the line. If neither occurs or independent readers disagree, report inconclusive. This is a screening rule for selected examples, not a quantitative estimate of product impact. Blind-read all eleven incoming records against all their candidate sets and source articles, including unchanged controls; report gaps. Register case ids and score mapping and historical top-ten replay before any send; send no paid calls until council review and preflight pass.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review.stderr:693:reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:62:The read-only Codex reviewer cleared harness design but not sending. Claude reviewed the earlier 10-case/three-arm draft and found lack of revision coverage, unsupported X22 direction and number presentation ambiguity; an eleventh recorded revision-bearing case was added. Its conclusion that 40 borderline probabilities bound opportunity is a hypothesis; confident outputs can also be wrong. The X22 prediction is a falsifiable probe hypothesis, not evidence of likely improvement. The shuffled arm is removed due to false attribution; C versus T assesses the total intervention, not score causation.
